      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <p>PRIVATE CASII ACCOMMODATION. TO £OOO. BEST TERMS. LOWEST INTEREST AND REPAYMENTS IN ENGLAND. CASH ADVANCES MADE DAILY on the following ,errns or as mutually agreed upon :</p>
        <p>£3 loan Is. Od. weekli £lO loan 2s. 6d. weekly. £5 loan is. 6d. wetkly I £2O loan ss. Od. weekly. £7 loan 2s. Od. weekly I £5O loan 12s. 6d. weekly. Other sums in same proportion. If you are in need of cash assistance you will do well to apply to me at once and I will advance you sufficient for your needs. Fair treatment and honest Lancashire dealings is my motto. Strictest privacy observed.</p>
        <p>NO FINES. - NO SURETIES. Apply in confidence by letter or personally to M. SEFTON, 31, CAIRO STREET, WARRINGTON. Miss C. COTTERIM, (Manageress). ZSTO 5,000 LENT DAILY LOWEST INTEREST IN ENGLAND. £5 loan, Repay 2s. weekly I £25 loan, Rem 10s. weekly £lO „ 4s. „ I £2O „ „ 12s. ~ £l5 „ 6s. „ I £5O i,, „ 20s. £2O „ Bs. „ I £lOO „ „ 355. „ No Sureties, no Delay, no Publicity. Special attention _ _ _</p>
        <p>appiicaas from this aistria. METROPOLITAN LOAN &amp; DISCOUNT CO.,</p>
        <p>219, BRUNSWICK ST.,OXFORD RD., MANCHESTER; and at 19, WITTON ST., (over Bradley's Clothing Shop) N ORTHWIC 65, EDLESTON RD. (corner of Camm St.), CREWE 8, TRENTHAM ST., RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>COLWYN BAY.—Furnished Unfurnished 1-I;uses, Apartments. Send stamp and requiremerits.— MtLLOR and ALLEN.</p>
        <p>LLANDLIDNO.—APARTMENTS (Board if desired). Every convenience and very central. Terms low till August.—PRETORTA VILLA, •</p>
        <p>POLWYN BAY.—APAR EVENTS or Board. Electric ligbt ; large front lawn ; near shore, woods, and shops.—PROPRIETRESS, Kimberley Mount.</p>
        <p>LLANDUDNO and NORTH WALES COAST:—For Furnished and Unfurnished Houses.—Apply M. RYAN, Estate Agent and Auctioner, Llandudno.</p>
        <p>NARBOROUGH.—Comfortable spacious APART- MENTS, central ; near station; on tram route.— Mica LUMLEY, Rose Villa, 55, Westboro. Stamp.</p>
        <p>APARTNIEMS, near woods; sea; pr emenade ; station ; Fairy Glen ; special low terms. Easter and Whitsun.—CHESTERLEA, Llanelian rd.. Colwyn Bay -</p>
        <p>ISLE OF WIGHT for Sunshine.—Comfortable APARTMENTS or Board; pleasant house, close to sea, highly recornmended.—" THE BRIARS," Sbanklin.</p>
        <p>• HONIE for FEEBLEMINDED, or Epileptics; children from £45 per annum;' trained nurse; country.-PRINCIPAL, Malvern House, Frankby,Cheshire.</p>
        <p>,ECAMBE.—APARTMENTS, with or without .I._ board ; home comforts.—Mrs. DAWSON, 'Mountain View," 16, Eidesforth Terrace, Promenade,</p>
        <p>COLWYN BAY.—Comfortable APARTMENTS,, three minutes from station and shore. Electric light, piano, modennte. PERKS, Galen House, Penrhyn rd.</p>
        <p>PUBUC and Private APARTMENTS, board if required.— HODGSON AND LEACH, 8, Molyneux Drive, New Brighton (formerly of Todmorden and Summit.)</p>
        <p>MORECAMBE W.E.—Public and private APART- MENTS; good sea view; terms moderate.— Misses BROUGHTON, Graham H onse, 11, Windsor Terrace, Heysham rd.</p>
        <p>SOUTHPORT.—" Courtlands" 29 Saunders St. opposite Sunnyside Hydro. One minute from promeriode. Homely apartments. Moderate terms.— Mrs. BfIISTOWE. 111* RIGHTON.—SPLENDID RESIDENCE overlook- LP unrivalled sea front; 11 bedrooms; well fur- nished ; ten guineas ; unfurnished £l6O. Others &lt;C26 to £llo.—YouNGs, Auctioneers, Second Avenue, Brighton</p>
        <p>PANTASAPH, North Wales.—Comfortable APART- MENTS; close to monastery, church and con- vent; beautiful scenery, sea and mountain air; accommodation motors, cycles; terms moderate.—Miss FAY, Bishop's House.</p>
        <p>MO LET, nice Furnished COTTAGE, in its own grounds; dining and sitting-room, 3 bedrooms, and attics; healthy neighbourhood, with lovely walks, also good fishing near; one mile from station, on Great Central Railway.—Apply WHITE HORSE HOTEL Overton-on-Dee, Ellesmere.</p>
        <p>Education.</p>
        <p>SOUTHPORT MODERN SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>AN IDEAL SCHOOL FOR BOYS.—For Booklet and terms, apply to HEADMASTER.</p>
        <p>DRAWING TAUGHT BY POST.—Moderate Terms, including Materials.—J. CHA RTER, Osmonville, \Veiling, Kent.</p>
        <p>OUTHPORT MODERN SCHOOL.—AN IDEAL SCHOOL FOR BOYS. Languages, Science, and Commercial Subjects, Swimming, Gymnastics, Cadet Company. Staff includes 8.A.,M.5c., F.C.V., Officier d' Academie, Scc. Foreign pupils received. Prospectus from HEADM ASTER.</p>
        <p>MHE PRIORY SCHOOL. WARRINGTON. PRINCIPAL : MISS DRING L.L.A., A.C.P. AStISTED by a qualiffed staff including 8.A., and L.R.A.M. NEXT TERM COMMENCES MAY 11Tri, PROSPECTUS ON APPLICATION.</p>
        <p>MR. ERNEST WILKES, A.L.C.M., Professor of Music, Organist and Choirmaster of St. Paul's Church, PIANO, ORGAN, THEORY, HARMONY COUNTERPOIN T, MUSICAL FORM. Upwards of 100 Certificates and Prizes gained by Pupils. Large three- manual Organ for pupil's practice.—For terms apply, 134, LOVELY LANE and LEGII ST., Warrington.</p>
        <p>MARPLE HIGH SCHOOL, CHESHIRE. Principals: The Misses RICHARDSON, High-class School for Girls and Young Boys; thorough education; pupils prepared for external examination if desired; special advantages offered in Music and Modern Languages, Beautiful country situation. Bracing Air. Boarders received New Term, January 19th.</p>
        <p>in OTE LE R GRAMMAR SCHOOL, WAR- RINGTON.—The SUMMER TERM will COMMENCE on WEDNESDAY, 28th April.</p>
        <p>The HEAD MASTER will be pleased to INTER- VIEW PARENTS at the School on the 27th inst. between the hours of 11 and 1; 3 and 5. The Head Master will be pleased to send the SCHOOL PROSPECTUS to PARENTS on APPLICA- TION for the same. VERB B. DAVIES, Law Clerk to the Governors.</p>
        <p>GOVERNMENT SITUATIONS ARE WITHIN THE REACH OF ALL YOUNG MEN BETWEEN 15 AND 22 YEARS OF AGE, AND OF ALL YOUNG LADIES BETWEEN 15 AND 20. Ai Examination in Hand. EVERYONE HAS AN writing, Composition, Aritb- EQUAL CHANCE. metic, Geograpby' &amp;c., has NO FAVOURITISM. to be passed, but from 3to 6 months' study is sufficient I to ensure SUCCeSg. EXCELLENT COMMENCING SALARY. Full Particulars in Civil Service Guide, Post Free from the. Secretary.</p>
        <p>SKERRY'S COLLEGE, 5, BOLD STREET, LIVERPOOL. Last Year the Total First Year's Salaries of Students who gained Situations by means of the College reacbed the large sum-of •=O,OOO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>Easter Eggs.—These are still the most popular form of Easter souvenirs, and we have Easter eggs of all shapes, sizes, and prices, mostly containing trifles such as chocolates, sweetmeats, and a Toddy Bear, of the novelty toy of some kind. Many • are contained in natural egg siells. They are intended for a surprise, and many contain some handsome Easter gift of jewellery for the lucky recipients. The eggs of the 1 guillemot, a pale green streaked with brown, are also used as receptacles for such gifts. The eggs are' gathered from the sea cliffs, on the North of England. They are very difficult to reach, and are much the size and shape of the William pear, so designed, it is said, by the wise guillemot, in order that they should not be so easily blown off the exposed- places where they are deposited, as if they see of the shape of ducks' eggs. Love Charms.—ln many countries and in Germany in particularetbere are superstitious customs for making cupid play the part at Easter. With us most of the old traditions and customs have died out, but that there are people apparently of ordinary intelligence, who still believe in the poseecy of love charms,. was evidenced by a letter which appeared recently in the "Courtship Column" of a Isciies' paper. The writer. mishine for the retein of her estaineed admirer, said she had heard of a powder, which, if she put it on the fire, and read averse from the Bible, would bring him rack. She very much wanted to know the nature of the powder, and how to use it. There are scores, and hundieds of old superstitious, equally droll or ridiculous, but one does not often meet with such faith nowadays, in the virtue of an old-time philtre or love charms. Lord Dalmeny's Wedding.—Untoward events have contrived to cast a gloom over the wedding prepara- tions for the marriage of Lord Dslmeny, and Miss Dorothy Grosvenor, which is fixed to take place in Lon- don on April 15. The Duke ant Duchess of West- . =baster have only recently list their little son, and this has been followed by the sad accident to Lady Crichton, daughter of the late Doke of Westminster, and aunt to tho bride. it is singu,ar that the last Grosvenor wedding was also overshadowed by an ill- ness in the family, in consequence of which the marriage of Lord itugh Grosvenor and Lady Mabel Crichton took • place privately. The sufferer— dangerously ill at the time—was, on that occasion Lady Crichton's sister. Lady Helen Grosvenor, who, if all goes well is to be bridesmaid to her niece, Miss Dorothy Grosvenor. According to the arrangements Lord Dalmany and his bride will spend the early part of their wedding holiday at Lillesball in Shropshire, lent to them by the Duke of Sutherland, aho is nearly retated to the bride's family. Nonagenarian Peers.—The death of Lord Gaydyr, snakes Lord Wernyes the " father" of the House of Lords. Lord Gwydyr attributed his long life to temperance in eating and drinking, non-smoking, and plenty of exercise. Had be lived be would have entered -upon his hundredth ;ear on the 27th of this month, and he remembered, as a boy of five, hearing the news of Waterloo being cried in the streets of London. He was present at four crronatiens, those of (Aorge IV., William IV., Queen Vicsoria and King Edward. Lord Vemyss was born three years alter the b .ttle of Waterloo, and was ninety-one on Monday, April sth, on -which occasion he was Presented with a portrait of himself by Mr. Sergeant, contributed by some 400 friends and relatives. He is a large landowner, and possesses several country seats, including Elcho Castle, whilst he has played many parts in life wthdisinction, having been sculptor, poor, orator, soldier, and is still an active member of the House of Lords.</p>
        <p>m 5,•.•••.,.,• v 5..., • - - In Bookland.—Lovers of Mrs. Humphry Ward's covets will be interested in the announcement by Messrs. Smith, Eden and Co., of a new edition of " Mrs. Bretherton " at 3s. 6d. Yet another reduction announced by this firm has reference to the Knutsford Edition of Mrs. ,Gaskell's works. All eight volumes are now appearing at 3s. 6d. instead of 4s. 6d. as formerly. Fashions in Faders.—Fenders with tall ends and</p>
        <p>seats thereon are in high favour. Of course they need rooms of at any rate moderate dimensions, in which to look really well, still they do not -requires() much space as the tall old-world fender with the seat all round. Again it is more easy to get a (rood " toast tip" when the seats are at the end only, a7tcl one has but to step over the. low rail in front of it and sit inside. Mirror-Glass Fire Screens.—Many novelties await those in search of mirror fire screens, among them the variety showing a cut design on the gists, in the form of a wreath or a star in the centre, festoons at the corner, etc., etc. Undoubtedly these particular screens are to be preferred to the" hand-painted "specimens . . . . . .</p>
        <p>although it must certainly be admitted that in the case of the star centre, the idea is suggested that the house- maid has been using the handle of her broom too vigorously. Then there are the mirror-glass ec,eens, tearing brass, or bronze ornaments in the centre, or at the corners, but bore again the corner ornatue`ntations are to be preferred, the centre one suggesting the covering-up of an accident. But hest of all perhaps is the perfectly plain bevelled-glass screen,plainly mounted to match the fender, than which nothing is in Letter taste, and less likely to clash with its surroundings. Putting Away Winter Clothes.—lt is perhaps a little early to store away winter garments. 'rho weather, as we know, is not to be trusted just set, but at the same time it is a mistake to let heavy wraps, furs, coats, and skirts, hang uselessly in the wardrobe for weeks before putting them away to make room for summer things. At least some of the heavy we'ght parments can go iuto their winter quarters, and they will Le all the better for this early attention when next they make their appearance. Of course' every garment should be care- fatly brushed, shaken and 'aired, before being put away in tarred paper. or still better in tightly gummed paper bags, with some moth preventive. . Among other old- fashioned moth preventives, such as lavender aid camphor, were dried musk seeds, or pods of the ordinary musk plant, and allspice berries, but there is nothing so reliable as the carbon or raphthaline, and although the smell is disegrecable, it can always be removed by giving the garment few hours' airing. The Rose ?.Behind the Ear.—The pretty fashion of -wearing a single rose behind the ear has been revived, and one welcomes it all the more fervently because it replaces the ahisps of tulle, the feathers, wings, and other odds and ends we have grown accustomed to see massed upon heads dressed for the evening. One or two leading Society women are at the moment permitting a couple of curls to fall upon the shoulders, and this idea, together with the notion of the rose behind the ear, brings baok to memory the modes of the fifties of last century.</p>
        <p>A Neck ornamant.—A fascinating neck ornament for evening wear is the narrow black watered ribbon band sewn sparingly with diamonds.imitation or real,and tied vory high up on the throat. When a clasp or a hook and eye in diamonds or paste can be indulged in, so much the better, but the less fortunate among us can simply tie the ribbon in a s:na,it little bow, and still be in Fathion's van.</p>
        <p>Care of the Eyem.—Oculists are generally agreed that there is quite as great a danger of injury to the eyes from too bright, a light, as from one that is too weak, and people who have work to get through in the evening should especially bear this matter in mind. Electric lights or incandescent lights should be carefully and thoughtfully shaded, while for much reading, writing or needlework, an oil lamp shaded so that the light does not fall upon the eyes is best. Yet another frequent cause of injury to the eyes is the glare of the sunlight</p>
        <p>on country roads. People who suffer from herdaChes when taking their walks abroad in bright cunsbine, should forthwith indulge in a pair of " Play " glasses, which fold rouni the sides, when they will not only, in all probability lose the headaches, and preserve their eyes, but experience an exquisite sense of restfulness. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>=CYdIuFROR McDOUGAI L's Paterit SELF-RAISING FLOUR The Examiner. Saturday, April 10, 1909,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>FLYING YISIT TO GERMANY.</head>
        <p>THE FLYING YISIT TO GERMANY.</p>
        <p>THE COST AND CONDITIONS OF LIVING.</p>
        <p>In a previous article we have shewn how the com- piler of the report of the English Heavy Woollen District deputation--7-which visited Germany for a twenty-four hours'. inquiiy in a country where four out of five of them knew nothing of the language-- attempted to make out that woollen workers' wagEs in Germany were higher than thpse paid in the district from which these "inquirers " had come, namely, by comparing prices paid for working- NI miscellaneous and highly-varied kinds of manufactures, wOrsteds, high-class woollens, low-class woollens, heavy woollens, &amp;c., with prices paid in the English Heavy Woollen District for actual heavy woollen work only.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>FOOD PRICV,'S,</head>
        <p>FOOD PRICV,'S,</p>
        <p>We have next to look at the cost of living amongst these German textile workers by comparison, with. the cost of living in the Heavy Woollen District 01 this country. In regard to this matter there is a table stowed away in an Odd corner of the deputation's report, and comment upon which is entirely avoided in the body of the report, which completely gives away the case of those who desire us to believe that the German worker can get his eaw material, namely, food and health and comfort, at as low a cost, and in as satisfactory a way as the British worker can. From this table, which gives a range of prices for each article of food mentioned, we take the following minimum prices ; it being necessary, Of course, for any comparision in which you cannot arrive at a true average either to take the minimum or the maximum, and in regard to the food of purchasers whose buying power is limited to a low rate of payment for work, the minimum .prices dre clearly the most important ;, and this comparison shews that ,the Germans pay 18 per cent. more for their food except potatoes:</p>
        <p>_ _ HE avy Wcollen German. District. Flour, per stone 2 0 Yeast, lb. Bread, 71b. Sugar, lb 0 2 Currants, lb. Raisins, lb. 1-ultanss, lb. Lard, lb. . . Dripping, lb. Beef, low sorts lb. Beef; choice, 11 Beef, steak, lb. Veal. lb. Mutton, lb. 0 91 Pork, lb. Bacon, smoked, lb 0 10 Ham, smoked, lb 1 3 Ham, toiled, lb 1 71 Butter; lb. • Margarine, lb. Cheese, lb. Peas, lb. Tea, lb 1 3 C.,ffee, ground. lb 0 9 ...... 1 0 Coffee, beans, lb. Cocos, lb. ... . ... 2 7 Sago, lb 0 51 Milk, quart 0 3 Potatoes, cwt. Haricot Beans, lb 0 11 0 2 LONG HOURS OF GERMAN LABOUR.</p>
        <p>We search through the report in vain for any proof of labour conditions which might be quoted as com- pensating in some measure for .the grave dis- advantages of the Germans in regard to wages and food prices: • ..• _ _</p>
        <p>The. German conditions of labour are, admittedly, more onerous than the conditions in Dewsbury and Batley. The report proves - that, as against the uniform 55,1 hottrs week of the heavy woolleddistrict, the hours of the German-workers in the towns vited vary from a Minimum of 59 to a maximum of 65: The Saturday afternoon holiday, which is so dear to our British workers, is unknown to the textile operatives of these towns. The proportion of married women is scandalously high. The men, it. is found, do not consider the textile occupation yield 6 well enough for them, and they (to into other employ- ments .by preference. The boys, for the same reason, cannot be tempted to enter the mills in any sufficient number: Such is a general summary of the conditiods.. Details,, as -supplied by the evidence of the members of the deputation, follow. Firstly, in regard to hours of labour, the deputation says, "At present the legal working day of Germany is 11 hours (10 on Saturdays) ; total 65. In the heavy woollen district a 551 hours week is almost universal. . . . We find :that in Rummelsburg, Forst, and Cottbus 62 hours are worked each week, in Crimmitschau 60 to 63, and in Nieder Schoneweide 59 hours."•</p>
        <p>In regard to Chablis, we are told, as an excuse for the number of women employed, that "as the textile workers are not the best paid operatives, it is very difficult at times to get good men for the cloth mills. This accounts for the presence of so many women in the factories. Many .of them are Slav women, who have been drawn into the town to supply the deficiency hj. labour." Cottbus is not a place of fine sentiment, however, for the deputation here found that the road- sweepers were• women.</p>
        <p>Whilst the mothers are working in the mills—as happens in a multitude of cases—this report tells us that the children are well taken care of by other people, and the babies who cannot be fed at the breast are in. some cases supplied with milk free of cost. Jn Forst, " the Batley pf Germany,"- says the report of the deput,ition, " no less than 2,660 gallons of milk were distributed at the expense of the municipality." Not only do many of the women woollen workers - of Forst fail, to feed their babies as .nature would have them fed, -but the united labours in the mills of the father and mother do not suffice to enable them to retain their self-respect by buying the necessary cows' milk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>EVERY PICTURE TELLS A STORY.</head>
        <p>EVERY PICTURE TELLS A STORY.</p>
        <p>Coming to the individual testimony of members of the deputation, Mr. T. H. Thomson says : " The wages paid to adult males are lower, but females and young persons are quite as well paid as at home. The hours of labour are longer and the standard of home comfort is not equal to that of. this district." Mr. J. B. Wilson says : "lii Forst men's wages are from 4s„ to 6s. less in most departments than in Yorkshire, whilst in other departments their wages are from' 2s. to 4s. lower. The wages of females very nearly equal those of females_ in Yorkshire:"</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilson sums' up the conditions in very expres- sive terms: "The men have a pale expression, one without life or ambition. . . . An operative of Forst said that their wages were much too low in order for them to live as they ought to and do the work they had to do. I asked if they had any pleasures. He said 'No.' If at week-ends they got a glass, or two of beer and.a dance they were spending money that was required for necessities. . . . The Gerinait woollen worker, speaking generally, is a willing, punctual, steady-ping, easy-to-be-managed worker, patient and 104-suffering... His position is niuch below that of the English worker His position has always bcen, -nd is to-day, poorer than ours. He has not the acknowledged and respected position which the English worker has attained. He does not receive a wage which will keep man and wife alone in comfortable circtunstances. What do people do if there is any family ? Well, the only answer is that they do the best they can, and that best is -a very,poor apology for a living. The mother Must go to work as well." Such are the features of that British Heavy Woollen Workers' report, which, by a wild orgie of :.the imagina five faculty of Tariff Reformers,- has been interpreted as yielding results proving better condi- tions for workers in Germany than in this countrv!</p>
        <p>In this series of specially written articles, we purpose answering tbe various arguments used by advocates of Tariff Reiorm.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The first round in the competition for the captain's prize was played over the links at Appleton on Satur- day. L. Holden qualified in the first division, and J. Hepherd and 0. Lofthouse tied in the second. Scores: FIRST Div [SION. Gross. H'cap. NP t. L. Holden C. G. Hutchings H. G. Roberts IL Kirkconnel W. Greenhouse 95 :.. 10 85 F. Pendlebury 95 8 ... 87 Rev. Lee Jones C. G. Stone 9B ... 9 ... 89 L. A. Voisey J. W. Bibby lOl ... lr ... 90 SECOND DIVISION. J. Hepherd 0. Lofthouse J. Richardson, jun. H. Woods lOl ... 16 ... 85 J. Fairhurst ' 103....15 ... 88 W. Richardson, jun. lO4 ... 16 ... 88</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>Quarter's Sales Over £5,009.</p>
        <p>There was little business of importance at the 103rd quarterly meeting of tbo Warrington Equitable Indus- trial Co-operative Society which was held in Bold-street Wesleyan schools on Monday evening. Mr. T. Wadsworth (president of the society) presided over a fair attendance. At the beginning of the meeting a vote of sympathy was passed with the family of the late Mr. A. Starkey, who was in the service of the Society for over forty years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>RECORD QIIA.RTER..</head>
        <p>A RECORD QIIA.RTER..</p>
        <p>The Committee's report disclosed the fact that the past quarter had been a record one in the Society's history. The sales had reached £51,500 17s. ld., an increase of £4,181 4s. lid, over the corresponding quarter of last year ; the total sales for the twelve months being £193,3.13 4s. 3d., an increase of £15,623 98. Bd., in spite of slackness of trade generally in the district. Although greatly handicapped by removal of stock and congestion of business, the drapery bad again shown an increase of £738, the boot department £390, and Winwiek-street department £l3O. The results of the quarter's trade had exceeded the Committee's expecta- tions, and enabled them again to recommend a dividend of 3s. 2d. in the .2. The coal department had been very largely instrumental in securing these results, and although they could not expect it to do anything like as well during the summer months, the present trade was very gratifying. The Committee had secured two cottages at the corner of Qrford.:aue and Foster-street for a branch grocery. Steps would be taken to mike' the needed alterations at once, so as to open this with as little delay as possible to relieve the trade at Win- wick-street and to help the trade in that new and growing neighbourhood. They were hoping to have the balery completed with the machinery installed in a few we k. The membership now stood at 9,173-295 members having joined and 75 withdrawn or left the district. The Education Committee had camplated their winter programme, and on the whole considered it had been a successful season. Arrangements for the Co-operative exhibition and summer trip were well in hand. The Committee, in accerdance with the resolution of the last quarterly meeting, had granted £35 for the relief of local distress to date, and Lad also been able to obtain a grant of £lO from the Co-operative Whole- sale Society for the same purpose. 'the CHAIRMAN said that notwithstanding trade depression, the Society bad had a very prosperous quarter, and that was something to be proud of. Questions having been asked and answered, in the course of which it was pointed out that several "senti- mental!' investments such as that in the British Cotton Growing Association had been written down to a nominal value, the report and balance sheet were adopted.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING ST kTI STICS.</p>
        <p>Some interesting statistics were contained in the annual return prescribed by the Chief Registrar for Societies carrying on icdustri and trades under the Industrial and 14 8 Provident. Societies Act, which was appended to the balance sheet. The number of meroters at the end of 1908 was 8,959. The Society carries on the productive industries of bakery, boot repairing and pork dep!rtment, and the distributive tradts of grocery and provisions, drapery, boots, clothing and outfittityr, furnishing, hardware and crockery. The particulars productive operations carried on by the Society during the year are :—Total value of productions for the year (wholesale price), £21,063; average number of wock- people employed, 19; amount of wages paid (exclusive of bonus), £1,673; bonus to labour, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>SUGGESTED PENNY BANK.</head>
        <p>SUGGESTED PENNY BANK.</p>
        <p>One of the members suggested that the Society should establish a penny bank for the convenience, of the children of members, but the Secretary said he did not think Warrington needed the facilities, and he saw no reason to run in opposition to the local Savings Bank. There were at present penny banks in connection with the day schools. The question was raised as to whether a reading room would be provided in the new premises, and it was stated that that would depend upon the accommodation available. Mr. England expressed the hope that the reading room would not be abandoned as it as useful to many members. - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CHILDREN'S GALA</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S GALA</p>
        <p>The committee were empowered to arrcmze a childten's gala for the summer months:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>JOHN BULL A "PAIATE."</head>
        <p>JOHN BULL A "PAIATE."</p>
        <p>hear you ask with naive surprise, so real that it, cannot be effected : " Who is going to attack Ger- many ? Are we pirates- that" e should be suspected of such a design ?'4 My dear 11,1-r. Bull, I am sorry to have to reply that you are a pirate, and the son of a pirate, and that the nation which attacked Copenhagen in 1807, need not be snprised if we sin:Mid not be quite sine that Kiel might not tempt lon in 1909. But for this lam not blaming you. Indeed, to some of us your ability to resist the temptation to attack our nascent geet is much more amazing thin would be the bombardment of Keil before breakfast. You may, and I hope devoutly that .von will, continue to practise this self-denying virtue, to which, I confess, I doubt whether tvc should he equal were we in your place ; but the safety of Gerwany cannot he ensured by simple reliance upon the ethical superiority of England. Granting that son are for the moment proof ag,tinst all tempta- tion, there is such a thing as atavism in nations as in individuals, and who can guarantee us against a sudden reversion of Britain to the ethics of Mr. Pitt ?—From "The German Naval Case, in the April "Contemporary."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>[By Redcap.]</head>
        <p>[By Redcap.]</p>
        <p>CHICKEN REARING AND FEEDING.</p>
        <p>As was stated last week, the hatching season is now so well adyanced,or I should like to have given a column on the subject Of incubation. . However, before pro- ceeding with the title of my subject I shall take the opportunity of giving a few hints, which will be useful to those.'of my readers who .have not as yet got all their eggs down. liiope, at tke same time, that others who are more advanced, will have adopted correct methods.</p>
        <p>ARTIFICIAL vEnsus NATURAL INCUBATION</p>
        <p>This .question seems even yet to puzzle the minds of some people, but it need not, because while no one will deny the correctness of naturaloincribation, the last few years have brought with them great improve- ments in the way of artificial incubation, and you need not fear results, if you. see to it that you purchase your machine from a reliable Maker.</p>
        <p>Failure is generally the outcome of using a Cheap and unreliable incubator. Many a valuable batch of eggs have been wasted in such a manner. And so I. would recommend only the ,best. A pound extra spent will mean a ; .return of pounds saved in the successful out-put Of chickens. The same can be said in reference to a foster mother, it is of no use having a good incubator and thinking that if you get good hatching results; any old box or case with an oil lamp stuck in the centre will serve the purpose of a foster mother, because it :will not. Certainly in cases of emergency many an improvised foster mother has been utilised with success, but for all practical purposes my advice to buy the best, and if you cannot afford to do that, then stick to the broody hen. The small farmer need not worry at all, because the broody hen will serve his purperse admirably. But for the farmer who has the foresight and the accom- modation to go in for -chicken rearing for the early market (when the prices are good) then an incubator and a foster mother are not, only a boon and a bless- ing, but an essential. You are. not always able to get bnoody hens when most required, and you cannot make a hen sit if she does not want to, but you can an incubator!</p>
        <p>Space.will not allow, nor is it necessary to go into detail as to the management of an incubator, as full particulars are always sent out with a machine from a reliable firm, the result of practical tests. I might just add that it is very necessary that only one person should have the management during the process of incubation as sometimes when more than one person interferes it is a question of "too many Cooks spoil the broth."</p>
        <p>THE BROODY HES</p>
        <p>First of all see that the nest box to be used for the sitting hen has been thoroughly limewashed and also that it has perfect ventilation. Then cut a good thick sod, place it in the bottom of the box, hollow it out in the shape of a nest, and then a bit of soft clean straw on the top of the sod. Before placing the hen on the nest dust well with insect powder both the nest and the ben. This will destroy any vermin that might exist and add to the comfort of the hen during her long and weary period of sitting' Before actually placing the eggs under, which should be done at night, see that the hen is quite contented and thoroughly settled to her new quarters. This can be ascertained by leaving her for a day on a few pot eggs. If immediately they are placed under her she shuffle them together in a contented manner, and at the end of the day they are quite warm, you can be perfectly certain she means business, Then you can remove the articles of deception for the real thing and place under the hen a number of eggs consistent with size.</p>
        <p>Let your sitting ben be situated in the quietest corner of your house or run, free from damp or draught. Feed on good maize or (Indian corn) once a day, plenty of clean drinking water, sonie flint grit and, last but.not least, a box of fine dry ashes, for an occasional dust bath.. If these items of importance are strictly seen to you may expect the hen to sit closely on the eggs and look forward to a good brood of chicks on the twenty-first. day, sometimes a day or two earlier sometimes a day or two later, according to the freshness of the eggs when put under the hen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>FEEDING THE CHICKS</head>
        <p>FEEDING THE CHICKS</p>
        <p>For at least twenty hours after hatching out no food is necessary,as the chickens during the later period of incubation absorb the contents of the yolk-bag and this in nearly all cases is sufficient to stimulate them for the number.- of .hours stated. But see that they are kept warm and dry. The first day's food should be given every. hour, but give the food so that they clear it all up, and if anything keep them on the hungry side so that they are always ready for each meal timp as it comes along: Feed every two hours on the second day, and then vary the number of meals per day as they continue to grow and feather.</p>
        <p>Nearly every breeder of chickens has his or her own special diet for the early period of feeding. Many things.have been said and written on this subject. Personally I am guided by _practical experience and natural observation. I certainly recommendlle dry feed system, and we owe much thanks to the various poultry food makers who after special and close study of this subject have now placed on the market a per feet dry food for chickens. Feeding on Wet mashes is detrimental to proper digestion, for a chicken is but a weak little creature after all and it lessens activity and growth if the crop of a chicken is crammed full of soft' food o such an extent that it can scarcely walk. The natural inclination of the chick, from the shell upwards, is to scratch and find its food, and by adopt- ing the dry feed system by throwing the food amongst dry short litter, you not only encourage the chickens to the best method of exercise, but they get' their food bit by bit and thus assimilate and masticate the grain in a gradual manner. They will damp their own food as instinct decides, so always have a supply of clean drinking water within easy access, and either a shallow box of flint grit or a "good layer of coarse sand on the bottom of the litter. This is most important for the chicks to grind their food with. A chicken' or a fowl without grit is like a person without teeth. Animal food .in some form is essential for assisting strong growth. Nature again points to this fact, for it is a common farm-yard sight at this season of the year to see a couple of sturdy little chicks Mercilessly butchering a good big fat worm, and they , would not fight 'so keenly for " fresh meat" if they did not like it. However, the foods - now advertised are so well blended as to contain • nearly all the elements that count- for successful chicken rearing, so you need hardly trouble farther. Koep your chickens as dry as possible. This is a great point and almost a sure preventive against the many diseases that they are heir to. If your chicken run , is. damp, erect an artificial shelter and cover the floor with dry sand and litter, and lose no time in isolating the stricken ones as various diseases soon spread and , carry away a whole brood. In cases' of gapes add some 'roup powder in the drinking water, and if very bad, feed on bread that has been soaked in warm milk, and give milk to drink. Constantly overhaul your brood and, if you should discover ticks abbut the head and neck apply a light dressing of ointment specially prepared and sold for that purpose. , In conclusion give attention to every detail pointed out in this article until your chickens are well grown in feather and you will not regret having done so, for it is the chick that has had a good start' in life that will eventually repay the cost in eggs or be fit to grace the banquet table of a Lord Mayor; providing always that they are of a good strain or as the new adage puts it, "You cannot 'rear a good fowl from a fotil hen."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>QI:EIZIES</head>
        <p>QI:EIZIES</p>
        <p>_ May --I again ask my readers to make good use of the Qnery • column. and by so doing lelp themselves and others in ' making their hobby an industrial suecesS. Queries should be sent to the "Examiner" Office, and addressed "Redcap." Please write ehrly in the week, or the publication of replies will be delayed. In all case enclose your name and address. (Next Week: Accommodation and Illousing.) .</p>
