      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>PAINFUL TRUTHS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</head>
        <p>PAINFUL TRUTHS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</p>
        <p>An " amusing Tariff Reform comedy has been play- ing at Braintree, in Essex, the details of which can be gathered from recent numbers of the "Essex Weekly News." That paper, with a perhaps justifi-- able pride, recorded that things were looking up at Braintreethat, in fact, the little town was enjoying an era of distinct prosperity. "At six out of the eight large centres of industry in the town," said the original paragraph, " the men are working overtime," and it proceeded to detail the different manufacturing establishments Which were feeling this strain upon their- resources. One of "these was that of Messrs. Lake and Elliott, the chief partner in which happens also to be the " local hon. secretary of the Tariff Reform League. Mr. Lake evidently felt that something devolved upon him in a situation in which - anyone dared to. suggest that any trade was prosperous. Thereupon he, wrote to the. editor, suggesting that Free Trade must indeed be in a bad position when the prosperity of trade in Braintree was advertised, and adding that the trade boom existed only in the reporter's imagination. On being interviewed on the 'subject, however, Mr. Lake admitted some facts. "1 say we I are not feeling the boom," he said ; "we have only been working overtime on a few odd things. We work overtime more or less all the year round in seine - departments. Last week the whole shop worked over- time, but last night we only had. two men oil late" _ _</p>
        <p>A good deal oisympathy might well.be extended to a Tariff Reform Secretary who is burdened in the midst, of his. propaganda by a factory that " works overtime more or less all_the year round," and supplies his opponents with statistics. • The struggle between love of Protection and the duty of continuing to earn dividends must be painful. And it cannot be lightened by the -fact that the heads of the other firms in Braintree whose names were originally mentioned have, on being interviewed, testified:. "We are work- ing till ten o'clock every night," " Things are better than they were a year ago," " We have been and are still busy," and "We have about 150 men working overtime now." The situation of the Tariff .Reform propagandist in such circumstances cannot but be attended by heartrending.mlifficulties. Every fresh order Must be. a stab, every declaration that " over- time must be worked Ao-night " must appear like a base surrender of the. fortress. The perplexity and distress of such a position could only be adequately treated by the pencil of " Westminster • Gazette."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
