      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION</p>
        <p>Results of Saturday's Matches.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>G . T. P. C . T. P. WakefisldTrinity* 3 3 15 Leeds'. 3 2 12 Hunslet* Widnes* ' 3 4 IS Rochdale H Warrington* Halifax - 3 1 9 Dewsbury* Keighley* York* ... ...... .x... 6 527 HulIK. R 4 1 11 Hull* 3 521 IVigan _ _ _</p>
        <p>Oldham* , 63 21 Itulicora Broughton 14117. 3 4 18 Leigh* Huddersfield Batley</p>
        <p>CUINIBERLAND CUP.—FINAL. Egrement 0 1 3 Brookland Wy'rs 0 0 0</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. BARROW.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men had another easy home engage- ment c.n Satutday, when Barrow were the visitors. Fine weather prevailed, but there was only a small attendance of about 3,000. Both sides were well represented. TC,IIIIS • Warrington—Mclntyre, back; Fsh, Dicitimon, Taylor and Bradshaw, thiee-qnarter backs; Brooks and Jenkins, half backs; Thomas, Sh u gars, Bel ton,Bradshaw, Hdrmer, and Boardman, forwards. . Barrow—Gifford, back ; Woodburn, Dobson, Treloar, and Bamter, three-quarter backs; Haney and Burns, half backs; Thompson, Teasdale, Little, Morgan,Brown and Burns, forwards. _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. J. Priestley, Salford. Touch judges, Messrs. T. Foulkes, St: Holt us, and .1. Critchle) ,Widnes. In the early phases of the game, Barrow showed a bold fiont, and quick passing almost let in Bamber, Bradshaw accounting for the Barrow man inches short of the line. Mclntyre heat off the invaders, and Dickinson and Fish by individual efforts caused Barrow trouble, the attack eventually resulting in Thomas passing to Brooks, who completed the try: Barrow retaliated, and after Burns had made a capital run Gifford placed a fine goal from bear the quarter line flit.' Fish responded with a goal for Warrington, but 13-errow were rot long in arrears, for following some faulty passing near the home line Burns got well away, and passed to Brown, who scored in a good position, for Gifford to give his side the lead. Warrington rallied splendidly, and after Thomas bad placed the scores level nith a good penalty goal the Barrow defence fell twice in quick -accession, Shugars and Boardman scoring as the result of excellent forward work. Half- time: Warrington, 12 points; Barrow, 7 points. Turn;ng round Warrington immediately forced mitters, and it required all Gifford's skill to keep the opposition at I ay. Twice Bradshaw was held up inches insidP, but Fish put on the finishing touch to a brilliant round of passire.'. Barrow played pluckily, but were no match for the home side a fine concerted movement between Dickinson and Fish ending in a try to the home centre. Near the close Warrington pressed con- tinuously, and Fish concluded the afternoon's scoring a hen he raced in from good passing on the quarter line. Result:—Warrington, 3 goals. 6 tries (21 points); Barrow, 2 goals, try (7 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WIDNES v. ROCHDALE HORNETS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. ROCHDALE HORNETS.</p>
        <p>At Whines, before 1,000 spectators. Included in the home team were five" A" team players. In the first lnilf Widnes played with the wind, and did most of the attacking. A charged-down kick by Taylor ended in that player aczaing an unimproved try. Moran broke away, and kicked over Hollinrake's head, and McCarthy dashed up cod scored. Taylor failed at goal, the ball striking the upright. Half-time :—Widnes 2 tries (6 points), Rochdale nil. The second half wss all in favour of Widnes. When on ha own line Rob7nr:on knocked on, and Balmer pick- ing up gave McCarthy, who scored under the posts. Taylor improved. A fourth try was scored by Blong after a combined movement,r nd Taylor agsin improved. Johnson dropped a fine goal, and just before the whistle sounded Fitzsimons, one of the Rochdale forwards, broke away and scored under the post.. • Baxter iinproved Result :—Widoes, 3 goals, 4 tries (13 points), Rochdale 1 goal, 1 try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>OLDHAM v. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Only two telms in the Northern Union have bad the distinztion of 13, ating Oldham poiatless during the present season. One of these was 'Warrington and the other Runcorn. Six weeks ago, in the return match, Oldham beat Warrington pointless, and their supporters hoped that on Saturday they would do the same with Runcorn. They came very near to do'ng it, an 1 the only points scored by Runcorn, were scored as a result of a miskick by Anlezark, who, in endeavouring to cover a slip by the full back, puntel into the bands of Moran, who tran,ferred to ButterwottS, for the latter to score an easy try. Apart from this incident, it could scarcely be said that the visitors bad at any time a chance of winning. For the greater part, they were kept on the defensive, and at half-time Oldham led by 11 points to nothing. The score being com- prised of four goals and a try. Tn the second half, two Improved tries were added to the Oldham score, sand- wiched in between being a try by Butterworth.—Result Oldham, 6 goals, 3 tries (21 points) ; Runcorn 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>RUGBY UNION,</head>
        <p>RUGBY UNION,</p>
        <p>FRIFNDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH V. THE ALIENS (LIVERPOOI.)—The " Heathens" registered, a creditable victory in their encounter with the Aliens at Ciubmoor on Saturday. The visitors had the advantage of a stiff breeze in the first half. Midfield work was succeeded by several attacks on the visitors' line. Johnstone mulled a good Chance by knocking on, and a minute afterwards Skelton ran down the left wing and planted behind the Aliens' line. Warren improved the try. On resuming, the Aliens operated vigorously on the right. The 'Heathens," however, tackled in effective manner. A failure to clear by the home hick let in Stockton Heath. and Ferguson nipped in smartly and scored between the posts, Warren failing to improve. Nearing the interval Johnstone kicked a penalty goal for the Aliens. Half- time: Stockton Beath, eight points: Aliens, three points. In the second half the game was of a give•and- take nature, with no tangible point accruing. Final : Stockton Heath, one goal one try (eight points) ; Aliens, one penalty goal (three points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>rtri7</head>
        <p>rtri7</p>
        <p>A (tit f ,or bows— 54</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, April 10, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, April 10, 1909.</p>
        <p>do so in a rational manner. The 'majority of the Council had been against placing the bridge on the s;te proilosed. It would he better for the matter to' be left over, so that the Town Clerk 'could have an opportunity of securit:4 a piece of land lower down. The Maxoß : It has been left over fer two Or three years. Alderman IlcwroN : It will be a case of marrying in haste and repenting at leisure. (Laughter.) The Council would act wisely by waiting a bit. The TOWN CLERK said he had had a definite refusal of certain other lawl. Alderman Be.):NETT said be had never supported a motion with less enthusiasm, because he had a sort of sneaking feeling that this was not the bast site. In a sense they Nvou'd make a mistake by adopting it, hut he was absolutely sick and weary of the whole question ; the Council had acted like a lot or school children. The most segsi,de 'course would have been to have gone in .for a complete scheme—but they had not the enterprise —and have erected a b:idge that would not have been merely serviceable for nurses and perambulatirs, but for other purposes. (Hear, - hear.) But while the Council was making up its mind about building a per- manent bridge and acquiring other land, thousands of people were being shut out from the park.. Although this might not be an ideal site,it was better, having regard to the small am aunt to be spent, to-. rzive the peopie 'fitness to the Park. Having regard also to the almost hopeless attitude of the Council, he should sup- port the.present- scheme, in order to get something done. , Alderman ELLISON pointed out that the matter had been befOre the Council since 1903. (Cries of "shame") 'lie was proceeding to 'allude to the ques- tion of the provision of playgrounds hut was ruled out of order as the subject under discussion was the erec- tion of a bridge. There was no doubt that if the new bridge was not made in a convenient position, it would be necessary to go on with the provision of an open space for Howley Ward. Discessing Cuncitlor Roberts' proposals, Alderman Ellison said they woald want a road right through to Knutsford-road, and if it was to he a public bigbway it would liiv to be railed on both sides. It would also cut the park •in two. -Then again, people would have to walk 1,000 yards from Victoria Park Gate t a Howley. He con- sidered the other site which had been suggested v.as the more.convenient.</p>
        <p>Cottrelllor WILKINSON said dna while the majority of the Council said the halfpenny bridge site w;s the best, there were a few lagging behind and crying out for the site goggesled by Councillor Roberts. Coun- cillor Wilkinson explained the advantages ol tt.:e half- penny bridge site at some lenebth,and caused amusement by remarking that he was so desperately sick and tired of the rEater that he could not for the life of MID see why people could not see eye to eye with him. AD:d- ing to the proposed agreement, Councillor Wilkinson id the Corporation of a town with 73,000 inhabitants would tie itself so that the Ship Canal Company could say at any time that they wuld not allow the bridge to remain there. They would be placing themselves under an obligation to the Canal Company. The Borough Surveyor .was ailed Lr his opinion on this point, and said the agreement was a usual one among public companies, and it was 'not probable that it would be enforced. - Councillor Wmicncgos : If the time cams that the company dr ask us to clear the incline away, 'how are we to get to the top of the bridge ? Al&amp; rnrin TINNIoN Flying machines. (laughter). •</p>
        <p>„ . The Tows CLERK: ba—ve nisiny simi:ar agrze- ment,s with the Mam.hest.r Ship Canal Company and they with us.</p>
        <p>C.;alinCilior WILKINSON appealed to 1113 Council rot to vote f, r a scheme whereby any authority c-ud come down on them at a moment's notice.and say "close up 4.he bridge " when they had spent the money.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD said the mover of the resolution had not brought forward any new argument why they should gO back on the decision come to at the meeting of the General Purposes Committee. It would not be necessary to go in fur an Act of P.r:ian-.eat- to secure land for the s;to suggested by Councillor Wilkinson. They had bought land from Mrs. Hewitt without doing so. With little or no eNpanse they could compel owners of land to sell. In 'his opinion they Willsinson hid all the argument in favour of ha sheme. Councillor Roberts had certainly given them some figures for considerat:on, but figures couldthe made to prove-any- thing. Nothing had been said about the distance people would have to go across the fields before they reached their destination Councillor ROBERTS: My figures include the distance from the Old Warps to Victoria gates. Councillor LORD Said the agreement with the Ship Gloat Corupnv would be detrimental and the fact that under Councillor Wilkinsen's scheme the road would be available day and night was another argumettt in its favour.</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADTIURST said it appeilred to him that there Was " Much ado about nothing." They had nn doubt hem impressed by the fignrcs which Councillor Roberts had brought forward and which he believed to be absolutely correct. They were not sub- mitted at the meeting of the General Purposes Com- mittee and they, therefore, knew nothing about them. Cenncillor WILKINSON said they were thoriughly convers int with the figureu Counaillor Robeets hid brought forward that evening. Nothing fresh hid been adduced. , Councillor BROADHURST said, he had never missed-a meeting of the Council since he had been a ft,ernber, and the figures which had been brought forward that evening had not been given before. t` W course " con- tinued the speaker "we are not all Solomons, and even Solomon in all his glory never shone es Coneator Wilkinson has Shone this evening." (I.aughtei ). They could all do with a better site than the one proposed, but unfottimati-ly they. could not place bridg,!s exactly where they would like them. If they adopted Councillor Wilkinson's suggestion they would be put to s. great de ti of difficulty and losiof time in purch sing land. Councillor Dr. JOSEPH said he was in an awkward position, lie voted in favour of the site Councillor Roberts propo7ed at the last meeting of the conruit,tee now he wanted to recant. He felt it was not the best site, and he only voted for it the other day because he WGS under the impression that it was the only site avail- able. lie preferred to vote in favour of Councillnl Wilkinson a scheme.</p>
        <p>COUileillOr PENIBERTON was s•Jrry 1113:111;erS had beee so foolish as to mention large sums of money in con- necticn with the purchase of land: It did no good to the town. Should they not rather depreciate the value of land ? (Hear, hear.) He was sarprised that Alder- man Bennett should dare to take such an attit trie—to admit that the scleen was not the bast for the rate- payees and yet, in order to save treuhle, to vote fur it. They ought to erect a bridge that sviuld be the most serviceable to all. Were they gong to limit the use of the bridge to seven months in the year ?The peopha who were clamouring most for. the bridge ware nlt children. "As to your anxiety about the children" contiatied Councillor Pembereon, " You have had recreation grounds in your hands for years and yet have allowed the poor little beggars ta do without then,. It suits yott to-night to trot the arguramt out. What is it ? Piffle. That's 'what it is. There is no earnestness in your argument at all." Councillor Roberts's scheme would be quite right if their object w.:s only to pet- videa long walk in order that people might get an appetite for. Sunday's dinner, but it had been argued that the bridge was to benefit workmen in going to and from their employment. Alderman PIERPOINT said he agreed to the utmost extent with the terms of the resolution, and whatever members might think about Win, he did not think they could tax him fairly and squarely with doing anything to serve his own private interests, and he hoped that what he had to sv would be taken as words uttered by a man who had the courage of his convictions and was sincete in his ideas. Ho -harl examined a map of the borough and he found the convenience of a very large number of the people would I c served if the temporary bridge was placed in the position suggested. It cer- tainly was the best position for the 7,000 people resid- ing in Fa:rfield Ward. It would be the proper position , for say at least half of Howley Wad—or 3,400 persons. With reference to St. John's Ward, 4 .bridge in the position suggested would ba as convenient as one placed Dearer the weir. The people on the eastern side of the park in Latthford Werd (whom he would estimate at 4,000) desirous of proceeding to the north- Net- st part of the borough would be.well served by the Howley warehouse site. Taking these figures as being approximately correct—Fairfield Ward, 7,500; How:ey Ward, 3,400;, L ttchford Wad, 4,000 ; St. John's Ward, 10,300—there was a total of 25,b0a Surely that was a number sufficient to warrant the building of the bridge to give access to the park which was waiting to be used. With reference to the agreement to remove the bridge at three months' notice it was only-businesslike of the Canal Company to protect themselves in such a manner, but he did not expect there would be any occasion for them to exercise this right, for the ' tendency was for the Manchester Ship Canal to absol-b a large amount of traffic that was now being dealt with at Howley and so do away with the necessity for expansion there. He was living in hopes, now that They had almost settled the question of a ferro-cOncreto Warrington Bridge.' they - would find that bridge had been built on such economical lines in comparison ton steel one, that before many years had passed they would see their way to erect a permanent bridge to connect • Howley and Letchford near the site suggested by Councillor Wilkinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL. THE HOWLEY FOOTBRIDGE. Councillor Roberts's Scheme rejected.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL. THE HOWLEY FOOTBRIDGE. Councillor Roberts's Scheme rejected.</p>
        <p>A LIVELY SCENE.</p>
        <p>Our local rulers were in a talkative mood on Tuesday evening aril consequently the Town Council meeting dragged on for over three Lours. Towards tha elcse the proceedings were enlivened w:th a little" scene," Councillor Pemberton being threatened by the Mayor with summary ijection and daring anybody to lay hands on him. " Budget night" and a variety of debatable topics were no doubt responsible for a larger attendance th n usual. Not one alderman and only two councillors Were missing from their usual places. The Mayor (Alderman Forstmw) presided and tbere were also present :—The D,-puty-Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), Aldermen A. Bennett, W. Bolton, T. Burton, L. Ellison, John Evans, W. Pierpo'nt and D. Tmnion ;. Councillors T. H. Ashcroft, 'P. Barton, T. Birtles, Broadhurst, T. Chantler, J. G. Craik, E. Ciinningtan. J. Dickenson, James Evans, J. Halliwell, J. Hawthorn, Dr. Jcsepb, F. T. Lashford, J. Lord, J. Pemberton, W. B. Roberts, W. R00.6er:4, J. Sankey, H. Singleton, J. Skelland, T. S. Steel,J. Fulton-Smith, 0. Toole, J. Wilkinson .ani W. J. Wilson"; Messrs. J. L. Whittle (town clerk), A. T.-Haliaway (deputy town clerk), J. Fairhurst (I:orough treasurer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), J. Deas (water engineer), W. S. Haddock (gas engineer), F'. V. L. Mathias (electrical engineer) and J. M. Murray (director of education). DR. JOSEPH AND THE MEDICAL INSPECTION</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>COMMIT fEE.</head>
        <p>COMMIT fEE.</p>
        <p>The question of the medical inspection of school children has been very productive of discussion and it was -once more the first topic ta he mentioned. When Comte' llor Fulton Smith had proposed the confirmation of the Education Committee's minutes, Ceuncillor PEMBERTON expressed the opinicn that the Medical Inspection 'Committee, as newly-constituted, had not been selected with a duo regard to the Toni Council. There were eight cd-opted members and only three Councillors on it. They had a medical man on the Education Committee and one would have thought that his special knowledge would have made him par- ticularly suitable for membership and that the com. mittee would have gone - out of its way to retain his services. nere vas much" putting of round pegs :nto square " in public life. Councillor Dr. JOSEPH said it did not look as though the Education Committee were trying to form the best 'pcs,ible committee td deal with a difficult subject. In the interests of the children and of the medical profes- sion, both of whom- the medical inspection seriously affected, they should have the best committee obtain- able hut the w4ty in which this committee bad leen constituted had reminded him somewhat of the sarcasm used years ago as to the qualifications of the First Lord of the Admiralty—that he most never have-teen the sea. (Laughter.) Ile (the speAer) was not anxious for more public work but as a matter of pr:nciple he protested most strongly, and ha would protest still more strongly if he were not the only medical member of the Educa- tion Commit tee. Coureillor FULTON SMITH said he -sympathised with what Councillor Pemhcrton had sill and be would be only too pleased to tring forward Dr. Joseph's name at the earliest opportunity. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HOWLEY FOOTBRIDGE,</head>
        <p>THE HOWLEY FOOTBRIDGE,</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROB Liarrs.s. PROPOSAL REJECTED. The meet'ng then considered the -following‘proposals brought feraard by Councillor W. B. Roberts :—" (1) That the temporary footbridge be erected over the river on the eest side of Howley warehouses. (2) That an agreement be entered into with the Bridgewater Depart- ment of the Manchester Ship Canal Company with regard to the construction of the proposed footbridge, the Corporation agreeing to pay £2 per annum, and to remove FO much of the bridge as interferes with the access to their land, on receivingsthree mouths' notice. (3) That the tender of Messrs. David Rowell and Com- pady for the proposed foetbridgdto be constructed at Howleysat £390, be accepted." Conncillor Ronterrs gave the following information as toj the distances to the two alternative sites which have 'lately received consideration : Site No. I.—East side of Howley warebouse, near tanner:es, Naylor's and Bridgewater Navigation ware- house Taking the junction of Howley-lane ani Church- street as the starting peint for Howley, Fairfield and part of St. John's Wards, containing a population of about 23,000 the length down Howley-lane, which is a paved street, is a distance of 533 yenta From the junction of Church-street and Mereef-street as the starting point of St. John's nod part of Howley Wards, containing a population of about [9,000 the length down Napier-street, Parr-street and occupation road and footpath, Navigation-street to Howley-lane to No. 1 bridge is a distance of 1,233 yards. Site No. 2.—Near Mill Stream Bridge, commonly known as Halfpenny Bridge. From junction of Howley lane and Church-street down ilow:ey-latie' Egerton- street to footpath between the Bridgewater Navigation premises, thence by iivcr bank to bridge is a distance of 656 yards. From Church-street and Mersey-street junction down Napier-se reet, Parr-street, occupation j j road anialong river bank to the. bridge, the distance is J 1,133 yard,. j Another route which is not available at present, but J could he got by purchasing land from Lord Winmarleigh snd -Messrs. Faircleugh. is from Church-street and Mersey-street junction, down Napier-street. Parr-street, I-larboard-street, crossing lands of Lori Winmarleigh and Messrs. Fairclough to bridge a distance of 966 yards. From entrence of Victoria Park to junction of Howley-lane and Church-street by bridge east side of Howley Warehouse, 1,213 yards. From eatrance to Park to Warrington Bridge, 1,133 yards. From War- rington Fridge to junction of Howley-lane and Church. street, 933 yards. From entrance of Victoria Park to junction of Howley-lane and Church-street by Half- penny Bridge, 1,090 yards. Dealing with the eroposed agreement with the Man- chester Ship C mal Company, be said it was nothing more or lest than an acknowledgment of right of way and the Corporation would be quite safe in paying it. Even if the right were ultimately claimed by the com- pany, it Irculd not be necessary for the bridge to be taken down. With regard to the tender Which he asked the Council to accept, the cost to the teen 'would be a very small item. Victoria Park was bought for recreation and play ground purposes for children, and the great argument was used at the time that it would prevent the nccresity for the purchase of open spaces in St. John's Fairfield and Howley Wards,where the land cou'd be built upon and brought into ratable value. For seven months of the year the bridge would •be available for 'Messrs. Rylands' workmen—some hundreds of whom lived at Latchford.' His suggestion was, he contended, a reasonable one, and he commended it to the favourable coasideration of the Council. Opposition had Leen raised from various quarters, but he was sure they all wished to do the best they could to make a connection between the Howley and Latch- ford Wards. By his scheme there would be no land to purchase, as the budge wild he on Cerporation property entirely. (Hear, hear). A Iddrman SMETHURST seconded the motion, and said he had contended all along that this site was the nearest for the district which the bridge would served. The Alderman replied to several criticisms which had been urged against the scheme' notably that it would involve the purchase of from 300 to 400 yards of roils. The gentleman who made that statement would not have done so if he had known that not three yards ssould have to be purchased. By the present scheme there would be no turning, rind they would have a beautiful carriage drive. They wou'd only have to make a foot- way through one field. They had not taken a run and jump at this matter, as it had been most c irefully considered by the Town Clerk, the Borough Surveyor and by the majority of the members of the Bridges Committee. They were, as a Council, spending too much time in passing resolutions, tied then rescind:ng them. (Hear, hear, and laughter). Councillor ROGERS supported and said that when they purchased Victeria Park it was understood that no open spaces would have to be bought in Howley and Fairfield Wards. He contended that the proposed site of tbe bridge was a very appropriate •ene ' • it was impossib!e to have another site without the purchase of land and one of the owners was so unwilling to sell that they might have to cbtrtin an Act of Perihelion'. Alderman BoreroN expressed surprise that the motion bad been put on the agenda after the undertaking which had been given by the General Purposes-Com- mittee that anonher scheme would be considered and that a biidge would be erected in the most suitable place. It was proposed to erect the budge at the present suggested spot because that Could be done with- out leading to complications. The site was at the wrong end' of Victoria Park. If it was intended to be used for the benefit of workmen and children it should be put up at the other end. Personally, he did not care a here the site was, but it should suit the greatest number of people. if Councillor Roberts would with- draws the motion, the General Purposes Committee would consider the-matter at their nest meeting. Councillor ROBERTS : I shall certainly not with- draw. '(Hear, hear.) • Councillor SINGLETON in supporting the motion, said the committee had tried to shelve the mattee of the bridge, as thesebad tried to shelve other things lately. They would not answer his questions. (Cries of "Shame "and laughter.) • The committeea ignored the Council. He should do all in his power to help in this matter, because the Council were promisedsorne months ago that the question of the bridge should be properly brought forward.</p>
        <p>Alaerman BURTON said there had been a great deal of engineering about the Park. It was said at first that part of it would be 'disposed of for building purposes, , stud -that money would thus be raised to recoup them, and then they were told that the Cor- poration had no • power to dispose of the land. .(Laughter). ,He did not doubt what Councillor Roberts Lad said about the diAances. but he was sorry the matter had been brought forward in such a manner. He did not cast any reflection upon what Councillor Roberts had said, as he believed he was actuated by the best end purest motives, and wished to get rid of the business. So did he (Alderman Burton),but he wished to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>VOTING.</head>
        <p>THE VOTING.</p>
        <p>The voting resalted as follows Conncillor Robert's moti9n :—Alder,nen Smethurst, Piernoint, Bennett and Evan,, and Councllor,s Pcit-tn-Stnita, Craik, Wilson, Birtles, Lashford, Broadhurst, Singleton; Roberts, Rogers and Ih3 Mayor-14. Ag tinst the motion : —Aldermen Bolton, Ellison, Burton, and'Councillori Dr. Joseph, Sankey, Wilkinson, Evans, Barton, Skelland,, Ashcroft, Dickenson, Changer, Took, Haiti tvell,4Cunaington, Lord, Pemberton and Hawthorn--18. The MAYOR: The motion is lost. THE OLD PIN MILL. ALDERMAN BENNETT'S PROPOSAL.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement and Lighting Committee hid resolved that on the sale of the site of Edelsten's Old Pin Mill, the Corporation will, in consequence of the awkward site, 'assist the purchaser in the laying of the land.</p>
        <p>". Alderman BENNErr protested aglinst the Corporation expressing-willingness t.) break their own bye-laws .by allowing some builder to erect houses •on land which was nit suitable for that purpose ,and might with advantage be turned to Some other use.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK exprossed the view that the matter</p>
        <p>was not one for discussion as the I Ind was still for sale and the minute was withdrawn with that object in view.</p>
        <p>HOWARD PLACE.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETT AND THE COUNCIL'S</p>
        <p>"IGNORANCE." The Sanitary Works Committee brought forward a resolution that a urinal be erected on the centre of Howard Place in accordance with plan No. 4 prepared by the Borough Survesent, - Alderman LIENNF7rT moved that the minute he referred back. He -presented a petition signed by every shop- keeper and the principal property owners in the neigh- bourhood against the structure being placed en the site in quess.ion. He contended that twenty yards awey there was another site on land belonging to the Cor- poration, which would be absolutely adequate. The present proposal was a disgrace to a town which bad some claims to be a civilized:town.' A reference by the Alderman to .the Beaartiful Warrington Seciety was received with irreverent mirth by some members of the Council and the Alderman warmly protested against the "rank Philistinism and ' gross ignerence of some members of the Council who 'raised ignorant cheers against a- society which with the utmost possible public spirit, against every conceiv- able discouragement, is working to save these gentle- men from their ownignoranceernd his some little regard for the 70,000 people who have to lire in the town under a continual cloud of srnoke, many of theta in places where everything is ugly and calculatedato lower their ideals." He proceeded to read a letter from the executive committee of the Berutiful Warrington Society urging the Council not to proceed with the proposal on the ground that the • convenience was not required there and would be an eyesore to the neigh- bourhood and also that the inhabitants of the district and people continually passing by considered it was unnecessare and undesir able. When the Council Laden objection on aesthetic grounds, backed up by a practical protest from tradesmen and others, and when there was another site equally advantageous, they ought to reject this and unnecessary proposal. Cormeillor CRAM seconded. He.diel not think that any _town which preferred to be up-to-dare would ever dream of-putting a convenience in that particular place, unlees it was under-eround. The people taf the neigh- bourhood had suffered patiently long enough through the great nuisance and lois occasioned. by the space being left in such a condition that it was nothing but a playground for months. They had a right to ask the Council not to allow "this monstrosity " to be erected. Councillor Dr. JosEest in supporting the amendment, said it was a pity that a small open seace—the only One in the noighhourhood—should•be taken away so aeon after it was gime He also opposed the proposal on sanitary grounds. In the course of fur tiler discussion, it was stated that the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Commit tee bad under- taken to place trees and shrubs round the convenience, and also that the other site mentioned by Alderman Bennett was close to a dwelingbousee The cost of the structure would he about £3OO. _</p>
        <p>On a vote being taken, the amendment was defeated by one vote, 15 to MEDICAL OFFICER'S SALARY. -</p>
        <p>The Council approved a recommendation of the Ihalth Committee, that the salary of the Medical Officer of Health Le increased by the sum of £5O per r mum. on account of the services tendered by him to the Education Committee. The salary is now £5OO. THE STATE OF' HORSENTARKET-STELF!T.</p>
        <p>The Paame and Sewerage Committee resilvei that the Town Council be recommended to rescind the resolution, passed by this Committee on the 25th .E.nuarr, 1909 :—" That the Borough Surveyor be authorised to proceed with the re-paving of Horse- market-street, f.o.n Lyme-trcet t Market-gate, with wood blocks." . _</p>
        <p>..... Alderman SmEnizittr mo7ed that the minute be referred back, remarking that pressure had been brought to bear nion him by ratepayers in the street. In 1901 a petition was presented in favour of the street, being re-paverl, and if they could have got setts at anything like a reasonable price he believed the work would have been done,' The shopkeepers had therefore to put up with th; same pAying, wbh the exception of repairs %ere and there. He ostimated that they paid rates to the extent of .£I,OSI Per annum, and yet for the sate of a paltry £4OO they must be deprived of conducting their businesses in cnrafort.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROGERS secondel. Councillor BraTLE supported, and condemned the dilatoriness of the Council in .tackling improvements. Councillor HAWTITORN said the committee's estimates as originally prepared were £l,OOO in advance of last yesr s. The conniittee went through them again to see if there were any items wliich might reasonably be dsferred and thus reau •e the eatimates, old it was thought that the prop, sII to re-pave Iloriemarket- street might be put off for ansther yeir, ,Th) retssn was that in vieli of the project on foot for widening that part of the town, any money khey sprit on widee= ing or improving that street might prove to have been ‘‘ as' ed. . _ . _ . .</p>
        <p>Councillor SINGLETON said tile state of Buttar.narket street was a thoAsind times worse than Horsem. trket- st, eet. . . .</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNieN said the proposal was rejected simply to save the rates, and othsr sand still more stupid things were done by the committee with that object in view. The question was not comidered on its merits at all. The new. Chancellor of the Exchequer c'eme there in 1l his glory, pride and pomposity,to show that he was going to reduce the rates by fourpence. The Al,lermae contended that th 3 committee cur. down till estimates unnecessarily ant were only piling up obligations for the future. They actually reduced the estimnt3 for wages. They. did that last year, the ameuat allowed being neluced to £2,50) but it did not stop work from being d_ma and the committee spent ,E 3,000. Councillor PEMBERTON raid there was a real that the expenditure of the committee should not be increased by £l,OOO. Certain events were happening in the Street Improvement Committee which moved them to a vote for a reduction of the estimates. They might have to go on with the widening of Ho: semarket-s!reet 7,soner tkan they e7er anticipaled and it would be folly LO repave dr, street cad had to pull it up again in a short time. Councillor Pemberton wuxed indignant at tlie " sloppy, jobbing, jerry manner "' in which the Cogocil had done work in the past.' TbeNzi had bean waste in the manlgerreut which no butsine7s in the world could stand. He wished there 7; as some W-4 of surcharging Couracil lors who voted money away reck- lessly. Councillor Pemberton took Alderman Tinnion to task for his conduct of the proceedings at the recent committee meeting. Alderman TIKNION indignantly repudiated any SU tr,- gestion that be insulted or offended any member. He was in fact congeatnlated on the fact he had displayed in keeping a rough team in hand. j.aughter). Couacillor PBMBERToN; The committee are men who speak their minds and I will not be put down by : Alderman Tinnion. Councillor WILKINSON raid Alderman Tinnion "got his heck up" because he could not rule the Committee as he liked. There was a cutting down of the est7imates. Businessmen on the Committee said that certain parts of the department were not conducted on the best and seam lest principles or as economically as they might be. However, with some Councillors, it was a case of "Put your rate up and let the poor devls pay." ; The amendment was defeated by 14 votes to 12 and the minutes were approved. LIVELY SCENE.</p>
        <p>MAYOR THREArENS TO HAVE COUNCILLOR</p>
        <p>PE \MERTON RE MOVED. When the mintues of the Town 11411, Perlis and Baths Committee came up for approval, Councillor PEMBER- TON asked how the proposed howling greens were going on. He said it wa--; two years since they were passed and he had heard nothing of them: Alderman Bourox replied that the sub-committee hev'ng char,e of the work were getting on with it as fast as possible, and when they .had made up their minds what to do, a report would be submitted to 0.3 Council. The IMAYott put the minutes and declared them carried, and called upon Councillor Evans to move the adoption of the Markets Committee minutes. Councillor Pemberton rose to speak on the previous minutes, when the MAYOR said that Aldermen Bolton had replied on the minutes, and they had been passed and told Councillor Pemberton t,-) sit down.</p>
        <p>COUtleillOr PEMBERTON : It is a point of order. The MAYOR (sternly) : I will not allow you. Councillor PEMBERTON claimed to Le heard, and a heated passage-at-arms followed. The MAYOR : I shall have removed from the Council Chamber, Colfacillor Pemberton. Councillor PEMBERTON : Well, you won't. I would like to see anybody's hand on me. Alderman SMETHURST : Gentlemen ! gmtlemen ! Councillor PEMBERT.:N : If the Mayor is a gentle- man he will allow me- To MAvoit : You Sit down. Councillor PEMBERTON : I ask for an explanation. - The MAYOR: I tell you you will not. You are out of order. Councillor PEMBIZRTON : You d•.on't know yet. The MAyon : You are out of order. Councillor PEMBERTON : Excuse me, lam not. The MAYOR: Tto minutes have been confirmed. Councillor PEAIBERTON : They have been confirmed in such a way that you give me no opportunity of speaking. I shall 'ming forward a special motion. It is abominable, • . -</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemtorton thel sat down. WARRINGTON BRIDGE SCHEME AGAIN</p>
        <p>DEFERRED.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Coramittee approved the -recommendations of the Bridges Sub-committee that the new bridge to be erected at Bridge Foot be con- struoted of ferro-concrete ; that the Council be recom- mended to obtain tenders for a bridge 70 feet and 80 feet wide respectively, instead of CO feet4nd, 80 feet respectively ; that the lines of the proposed new bridge be in continuation of the lines of Bridge-street ; that training walls be constructed at each end of the bridge, as shown on the plan submitted; that a consulting engineer be appointed. On the proposition of the MAYOR, secondei by Alder- man SMETHURST, the minutes of the General Purposes Committee were confirmed with the exception of the resolutions referring to. Warrington Bridge. It was decided to hold a specid Council meeting on April 27 in order to have a full discussion.—On this understand- ing Councillor LORD withdrew his amendment relating to the recommendations of the Bridges Sub-Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>'NEEPING ECZEMA TWEl'fi'l YEARS</head>
        <p>'NEEPING ECZEMA TWEl'fi'l YEARS</p>
        <p>Never Without irritation —Was Told He Could Not be Cured—At One - Time Pain and Burning Barred Sleep.— Could Hardly Walk and was Becoming Quite Weak.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>" Ero CURED BY CUTCURA</head>
        <p>" Ero CURED BY CUTCURA</p>
        <p>"It 13 now ncari7 tv.-enty years since I was fir:3t attacked with weeping ec- zema on the leg, just above the ankl ,e which the doctors informed me I should never get rid of. During that time I have never known what it is to be with. out irritaticn. The last attack which' lasted for nearly three months was the worst of all. I could get no ease or sleep at night through the agony. / underwent treatment by a doctor but could get no relief, F.C. left oftgoing. The pain was so intense toward night that could scarcely walk home and the burn. ing sensation when in bed was intoler• able. I began to feel quite weak for</p>
        <p>- want ea- proper rest.: 'My daughter mentioned a case of a man who had an attack cf eczema on the chest and arms and after spending a lot with doctors, etc., was perfectly cured after using one set of the Cuticura Remedies. So I sent for a set (consist- ing cf Cuticura Soap, Ointment and Resolvent Pills) and after using them the first time I had a good night's sleep and from that time have entirely lost the pain. The sores healed up and ccntinued the Cuticura Ointment for about a fortnight longer and have taken the Cuticura Pills nightly till the bottle was emptied and the eczema has entirety disappeared. 1 have at least two thirds of the Ointment left. Lean from personal experience recommend the Cuticura, Remedies as an economical and effec- tive treatment.</p>
        <p>"My wife h alro used the Cuticura Soap and Ointment for chafing and two applications have entirely cured her. I cannot speak too highly of the Cuticura Remedies and shall not be without them in the house: S. C. Markquick, 90, Al- bert Rd., Leyton, London, N. E., Eng- land, June 10 and July 6, 1908." 6,</p>
