      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, APRIL 10TH, 1009.</p>
        <p>Before Parliament rose HONEST for the Easter holidays BUNGLERS AND on Wednesday an POLITICAL attempt was made in AGITATORS. the House of Commons</p>
        <p>to bring under discus- sion a speech made by Admiral SMITH" DORRIEN ,at Berkhampstead on March 31. As Admiral SMITH-DoRRIEN is not now on the active list this could not be done, but what the gallant admiral said is nevertheless worthy of some attention as an example of the lengths to which the naval controversy is being carried by extremists. . The admiral spoke of something that he would like to do. It might be wrong of him to say it,.he admitted, but if he had his way he would "take up CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN and hang him in Trafalgar-square for having betrayed his country." He may have been thinking of what was done in the case of OLIVER CROMWELL, whose remains were dragged out of the grave to be hung in chains at Tyburn. A picture of the gallant admiral forms itself in the mind as his words are read over. We are all familiar with the type, which, by the way, has been drawn for us by THACKERAY. "The service is going to the dogs, by Jove," is the historic form in which men of this class express their con- viction.</p>
        <p>Admiral SMITH-DORRIEN found a supporter in a Mr. ARNOLD WARD, who, however, would not confine himself to posthumous vengeance on the remains of Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN. He would, if the law permitted, do something very drastic to the living members of the Government. CHARLES LAMB, on a famous occasion, exclaimed to the ineffable Comptroller of Stamps, "Pray, sir, allow me to examine your phrenological development ! " Public speakers of the Admiral SMITH-DORRIEN and ARNOLD WARD type provoke in us a similar desire to feel their bumps. What can be the intellectual equipment of those who address their fellow countrymen,at what they believe to be a time of crisis, in language so far separated from that of common-sense, of good taste, and reasonable feeling? We grant that they may be sincere in their beliefs. Admiral SMITH-DORRIEN may be completely convinced that Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN was a traitor. Mr. ARNOLD WARD probably has an equally conscientious conviction that the present advisers of his MAJESTY are betray- ing their country. But what is the basis of these beliefs of theirs? What are the facts on which- they found their remarkable opinions? They have just as much to go upon as is commonly the case " with those who denounce things at large and at random," which is nothing at all. If the Government were really sacrificing the interests of the country, to say nothing of betraying them, and if there were the faintest proof of the fact, we should all be animated by .that same desire for an immediate change of Adminis- tration.</p>
        <p>Honest bunglers like Admiral SmITH- DORRIEN always believe that they are being</p>
        <p>betrayed when their own party is not ill power. They are much practised upon bY persons who are not so honest. The agit3- tion which is to be postponed until after Easter in not being engineered by bunglers but by electioneering experts, who have car vinced themselves that in politics honestY the worst policy. The task they have sel themselves is not to prove that the Govern- ment is neglecting precautions for till national safety. They are quite we aware that this is not capable of proof. TheY want to convince the people of the soundness of a set of entirely false propositions. We should have little faith in the good sense of enir countrymen if we believed that they woul" succeed in their object. Hitherto they We met with nothing but exposure. On the floor of the House of Coinmons they has.° not been able to sustain any part of their case. From Mr. BALFOUR downwards theY have all, if we may be permitted to use sucli, an expression, been floored by facts. VII' statements that hale been overwhelmed Parliament are put before the country' ill grosser forms and with more vehemence, just as if they had been proved to the hilt. Simple, fiery, unreasoning gentlemen likcie Admiral SMITH-DORRIEN believe them, all ' are led into making exhibitions of themselves in consequence.</p>
        <p>The Corporation ig £24,000 FOR anxious to push STREET private street improve' IMPROVEMENTS. ments in order to Pit vide work for t' unemployed, and at the inquiry on Thursdat a strong request was made to the Lee Len Government Board inspector to expedite tiw granting of a loan of £24,000 to further this object. The application, like ti' 00 recent one with respect to t_t" widening of the main thoroughfarn was allowed to pass without opposition, not only does this sensible method of Pll5 viding much-needed employment comnleill itself to the ratepayers, but they a'sci recognize the necessity, on the grounds 011 health and convenience, of proceeding witd the making and paving of streets an pas-Sages as early as possible. The sur gestion that ten years should b° allowed for the repayment of the 10all is a reasonable one, for in some eas,e!, •the expenditure presses heavily upon O.; property-owners and anything that e3i) be done, consistent with the public welfare' to meet their just requests should receive favourable consideration.</p>
        <p>Our forecast last wee THE RATES that the rates would he REDUCED, reduced by 4d. in £ has happily proved t.v be accurate. Councillor Wilkinson made /05 debut as Chancellor of the local ExchequeS on Tuesday under gratifying auspices, ana in the course of his first budget speech he ha the pleasure of announcing this welconle reduction of 4d. in the call upon the rate: payers. Various causes have contributed -it' this satisfactory result. The requirements of the Guardians and overseers are less than for the corresponding period, the rateable value of .the borough has been increased b, natural growth' and the operations of tny Assessment Committee, the various Cori poration Committees have carefully eiti.t their coats according to their cloth, and creel balances have taken the place of debits. Th! Improvement Rate (2s. 10d.) and the Sewer' Rate (2d.) remain the same, the reduction coming entirely from the Poor Rate (4s. 4(l' instead of 4s. -8d). With so many contribn: tory causes it is impossible to credit 011.,' department more than another and "here has the saving been effected." It],,. quite obvious that there has been a steal practice of economy throughout the year an our municipal administrators are entitled te praise for their prudent management. Aci4 Councillor Wilkinson pointed out the nee for economy is still urgent; for a rate °. 7s. 4d. cannot be considered a light imPosl- tion even for the privilege of being a "eitizeil:i of no mean city." Something under 7s. wotu, be a reasonable figure, and we hope eves ,care will be taken both in t productive and non-productive departmenli to ensure a further reduction next year. has been said that a town whose rates o;_r."s" not increasing is not progressive, but tplit, appears to us to be dangerous sophistry. ,0 moderately low rate is an encouragement 1/1 commercial enterprise and expansion. Vig rat v may give us municipal splendour hur they are not conducive to town-growth ° to productivity!</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett W 35 BHILISTINES one of the chief actelB AT HOME. in the various scenes humorous and other; wise—at the Warrington Town Council °, • Tuesday evening. He waxed very wroti" and his usually mild blue eyes.flashed nied. during the discussion on the not savoury or enlightened proposal to "ori ment " Howard-place with a public ci:2,11; venience. "It was," said the Alderman, ,0 disgrace to a town with some claims to va considered civilized," and it also illustratee "the rank Philistinism and crass ignoran,c,, of some of the members of the CouncY,*(l Had we possessed a voice entitled to be heai, at the time we should have said "110jV hear" as loudly as the " Philistine laughed in their derision. We do not dotil'a for a moment that such a convenience 1; required in the neighbourhood, but af,t,eo creating Howard Place in memory of tij - , great prison reformer and his local associtl,, tions, it is nothing short of desecration "0 "ornament !' the spot by an objectionab,! structure of this kind however ornate. design. The Sanitary Works Committe&lt; which creates as many nuisances as 4.10 abates—has been determined all along,: commit this outrage on good tasll:o' Much might be said against tj,;4 the scheme, on purely practical groun'd —Dr. Joseph objects to it for sanita", reasons, and the whole of the surroundil shop-keepers have petitioned against i"; an added nuisance—but the Council ls unrepentant, though it was only by 15 voted, to 14 that the scheme was sanctioneA, Alderman Bennett's tirade was fully justifiet'e If the Sanitary Committee cannot ab,a'A nuisances without making others they Priik better resign and leave room for men w" more enlightened ideas. Beautiful Warring' ton! Ye gods!</p>
        <p>1 i 5 Upon second thoug.l.o THE CHILDREN'S the trade appears ACT, have considered it be:, not to put into oPer‘;6 tion its schemes for evading the Childreoi, Act. The plans for "Children's Parlollr 1',5/10 with messengers to carry drink from .0 bar, and nurseries with special attendarl:t have not matured. We understand th;cl "private and confidential" circulars. issuat to publicans have advised that it is best or present not to risk a loss of prestige, 1,/ awaken the hostility of the public. In vie</p>
        <p>to</p>
        <p>Jig at tea at or )11</p>
        <p>Of the coming Budget and the plaintive cries which the " Beerophone " will send forth to the world, it is felt that the Trade must pre- ' erve its every virtue and not allow its emies to say that it cares more for its sales an for the welfare of the children. One Public-house in Catford tried the novel xPeriment of placing nurses outside its Two girls in light blue dresses and Yyith caps and aprons inscribed "Children's -Frotection" stoodoutside the door, and when Pdrents came to enter took charge of the :children. This is a venture which, we . should will not be persevered with very long.</p>
        <p>For the second time SALVAGE FROM this Session the House THE of Commons has been LICENSING BILL. engaged in discussing Bills which are really parts of the Licensing Bill of last year. A .lew Weeks ago a Sunday Closing Bill passed its second reading by a large majority and ;as sent forward to one of the Grand b°lnmittees. Last Friday Mr. Charles '°berts, who visited Warrington just a year and spoke at the Parr Hall demonstra- 'l°n, brought forward a Bill for the closing of :131iblic-houses on Parliamentary Election z lYs• This section of the late Licensing Bill le_,e,eived the suppo,rt of the Opposition when (Tore the House, but their enthusiasm has hinlinished and they are now manifestly- iTlStile- They had some difficulty in explain- away the speeches of their leaders, but Thyllslstency is not a political virtue to which Zilch importance is attached by the sup- l's of the Trade. -8- Wretched display was made by the "JPosition in the debate and the evidence 'Yen before the Royal Licensing Corn- withh its story of election fights :411enced by free drinks could not be VsPlained away or satisfactorily answered. o'Pe second reading was approved by a vote 199 votes against 46. It is better to have :te Licensing Bill piecemeal than not have it a all, and if we are to judge 1347 the interest biT enthusiasm_ which these fragments of the 11'1 I have aroused we may conclude that it is asQt, nearly so dead or so likely to be forgotten oPponents would have us believe.</p>
        <p>Th e Housing and ROUSINd AND Town-planning Bill, 'CIWN-PeANNING. the second reading of</p>
        <p>which Mr. John Burns ni°ved on Monday, is almost an exact leProduction of last year's Bill as amended on Grand Committee. It constitutes an vnorrnous advance in the methods of dealing Nytt:ll congestion and insanitary dwellings, vnile the part which deals with town- th7lining is a new departure in legislation in country. The essential principle of the tzlll is to put into force throughout the :1,11,1117 Part 111. of the Housing Act, 1890, hr,`".en enabled the authorities to provide new 41ses for the working clasfes, but which up Present has been only adoptive. -rohne pressure will be brought to bear upon ioltletant authorities in order to bring them of 4.9, line with more progressive bodies, one zi up chief deterrent influences, which has I.eacie many local authorities hang back, is c:ved by the proviso that loans can be tamed from the Public Works Loan Com- -Ltalssioners for periods up to eighty.years af a f,.te of interest which is the minimum both 4°lll the point of view of desirability and a,krrAhe evil of" back-to-back" houses is to tiushed. The Bill creates an implied n_dertaking on the part of the landlord in .1,7 contract of letting, not only that the af'll,se is reasonably fit for human habitation 'tie time of lettingand this provision is Xtended to houses of a higher rental than -13.1ne Within the present law—but that the _lnnse shall be kept reasonably fit for human bitation throughout the holding. Mr. -11rn8 claims that this provision will in itself, ligor I.ev °us.3r enforced, as it will be, create a olution in the minor conditions of housing. valuable addition to the Bill is the clause voro,„ . Teri P"lng local authorities to prepare and ew every five years a full register of VI, sing accommodation within their areas. 004,Tgether there can be not the slightest 0,;12t that the Bill makes a most important t,utribution towards the solving of "the undition of the people" problem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>- Solomon in all his glory never shone as Ilor Wilkinson has shone to-night," tii,,,q,Councillor Broadhurst on Tuesday. We terlit add that Mr. Broadhurst also shone a tiotlilling the evening, but his glory would uave made Solomon jealous. q:Ylle collection will now be taken" said the a;11,111an of a suffrage meeting in Manchester ,Ilszit.o Please let it be a silent collection.', The rh lence was a little puzzled until the chair- went on with gentle emphasis, "Some thillmake a good deal of noise." We slxould ari -0' the humble threepenny-bit would have PPortunity to shine here.</p>
        <p>* * W,ll,roPos of recent mine disasters, Mr. S )111:sn, M.P., has been telling the Wigan tee-„ers that mining inspection will have to Ile Pie a reality, instead of the sham which 'ieclares it is at the present time. They tie:t demand the State employment of prac- iiie':' Working miners as mine inspectors; Who had worked at the coal face and conversant with all grades of mining tha,i' He was confident that it was only by Ineans that they mould secure a real Dection.</p>
        <p>xt,,, 11,37 and heartfelt will be the good wishes Sai;licied to the "grand old man" of tlie te e%ation Army—General Booth—who will ti.iglitY years old to-day. The General's ' 'hdP</p>
        <p>Ar,„ -ay Will be celebrated by a Salvation tz,4 'l3r thanksgiving ,meeting in Albert Hall Ssei" 22nd, when 10,000 Salvationists will I'e UIQ to welcome him. Three deputations th'e'esenting the "old guard," the army of 'iiilsent day, and the rising generation, deli 'nen be presented, and the General will era birthday speech. Son * * * Xai,.,le, of the fallacious arguments and l'er -"Pies advanced b Protectionists are</p>
        <p>iy 'ast amusing. Speaking at Huddersfield rid, Mr. Wyndham, M.P., remarked 'Aro Ith a revenue such as Tariff Reform bring it would mean keeping a war liel!"et char 11P in times of peace." This is a ming plan on paper of fighting the with his own weapons and making do-e Day for them as well. The prospect uot please us any moie than it will thill/1 the foreigner. We still wonder how tor:iellormous revenue is to be obtained from 1)1011; goods, the exclusion_of which is to vide " work for all." rrhe L'* 0 iverpool Post" explains the phrase all, 'by suggesting that outside the xehange, Bristol, there are two iron 141.'1_8 with flattened tops, and on these the eocipaits placed their coins when they made few"B4 Purchase, or, at least, they placed a for ePresentative coins. Thus purchasing 11e.a4.8h became the regular arrangement, 1' was described as "paying on the nail."</p>
        <p>The proud people of Bristol keep the " nails " to this day as monuments of their methods of business and as indications of their world- famed integrity. But wherever the English language is spoken, the phrase "on the nail" is known. .</p>
        <p>* * There is a proposal on foot in Blackburn to make writing with chalk on the pave- ment and on the walls a police offence. We do not know whether this is directed against the suffragettes or not, but if it is adopted the ladies who go about at night, when they ought to be in bed, and ornament the sidewalks with their round-hand, only to their own delight, will have to adopt some other means of getting their message uttered. The proposal is quite reasonable. The purpose of the pavement-is not to pro- vide " reform " organisations with oppor- tunities for free advertisements, and it might in certain circumstances lead toa block in the traffic.</p>
        <p>* * * Two members of our Town Council were noticed on Saturday afternoon, apparently very much interested in Warrington Bridge. Of course it was only a coincidence that their inspection should take place at the time that.o number of ratepayers were passing over the bridge on their way to the football match. 'Whilst the two legislators were evidently considering the question of widening from the Mersey-street side, a football enthusiast was heard to remark: "What's .th' go6d o' looking at that side mon, tha' knows there's na pub on that side. Stop yer kidding and come over to t'other side." Do these wiseacres really mean business, or were they only "kidding '? "</p>
        <p>Mr. Russell Wakefield last week tried his hand at proving that compulsory military service was in reality volunteering. Here is how he did it: Compulsion was not the proper word to describe the acceptance by a nvtion deliberately of the universal preparedness for the defence of one's country. As son as a method became national it ceased to be compulsion. England expressed her- self by her Legislature, and if she decided upon _ _ . .</p>
        <p>national service It was then her voluntary act.</p>
        <p>On this showing France has a voluntary Army, and it is only a verbal fallacy to say that elementary education in this country is compulsory. If people can be got to believe Mr. Wakefield, then the cause of National Service will need no further advocacy.</p>
        <p>* * * The election of Mr. Hemmerde for East Denbighshire by practically the same majority as in 1906 shows that Wales, like Scotland, is still faithful to the Liberal cause. It should not be forgotten at the present moment that Mr. Hammerde is a strong advocate of the taxation of land values, and that he has always put this in the front of his political programme. When he says that his return is a message to the Government which means "Let us have a real democratic Budget," he apparently has in his view .mainly the taxation of land values. The East Denbigh .election will have a very sobering effect on the Tory party, and we shall probably hear less of the arrogant claim that they have the country behind them. It is certain that they have the support neither of Scotland nor Wales, and still less of Ireland. * * *</p>
        <p>One of the pictures that are certain to .attract attention at the forthcoming Academy exhibition is a three-quarter- length portrait of Mr. Lloyd George, by Sir Luke Fildes, best known, perhaps, as the painter of "The aktor," who received his early training at the Warrington School of Art. Mr. Lloyd George, clad in black-and- gold Chancellor's robes, is seen sitting at a table. The artist has given him an air of interrogation expressed in the alert glance and slightly raised eyebrows. "The change in pictures and taste for pictures has been very pronounced in the last thirty years," Sir Luke Fildes stated to a Press representa- tive. "To-day people live more in flats, hotels, and motor-cars, and there js less of home life. Partly for this reason people don't seem to care to buy pictures as they used to, and consequently fewer are being painted, and the painters depict, so to speak, more popular subjects."</p>
        <p>We haven't noticed any posters announc- ing the result of the Denbigh Election—and so near home too! Is this reticence out of respect to Mr. Hemtherde, who is no believer in bill-posting, and who won his election with, nothing' on the Posting Stations, which were practically covered with Blue, or are our Tory friends beginning to think this method is getting stale, and are waiting for Bewsey-street to give them some newer ideas? May-be they have merely a pre- judice against East Denbigh; at any rate the select little band of Tories who went there from Warrington are not very well pleased with themselves. They found an old friend (Mr. Howes) on the spot, and whether they were afraid he might ask thenn if they had found a man yet to debate Tariff Reform, or whetheir, their reception was not exactly what they would have liked, we cannot say. But this fact remains, they were glad to get back. * * *</p>
        <p>The Dutch in South Africa are now proud to be subjects of the greatest Empire of the World.—General Botha. There is no need for scare. I believe the Navy is equal to all the demands that are likely to be made on it to-day.—Captain Pretyman, M.P. Make ample security for national safety, and then apply your spare cash to more, pro- ductive ends than the building of unneces- sar Macnamara, M.P. My life is as open as a book, whatsoever good or harm I have done.—Mr. Will Crooke, M.l3* When the time comes for the Budget I hope patriots will not squeal.—Mr. Walter Runciman, M.P. If the worst came to the worst, the Labour leaders in this country and in Germany would be prepared to advise the working- classes to make war impossible by declaring that they would cut off supplies.—Mr. Keir Hardie, M.P.</p>
        <p>Much water is likely to pass down the river before it gets its new bridges. The long discussion at Tuesday's Council meeting did not bring the Howley footbridge much nearer materialisation, and it resulted in the question at issue concerning War- rington bridge being referred to a, special meeting to be held on Tues- day, the 27th. Councillor W. B. Roberts made a great effort to over-ride the decision of the General Purposes Committee and get the footbridge on the East side of the Howley Warehouses put in hand at once. There was a somewhat heated debate which, drew nearly every member of the Council to the vote, the resolutions being eventually defeated by 18 votes to 14. Speaking frankly we are not enamoured of Scheme INo. 1 as proposed by Councillor Roberts. ' A bridge right on the outskirts of the town and crossing the river at a point 1,200 yards from the entrance to Victoria Park cannot be considered the ideal connecting link between Latchford and Howley. Fortunately</p>
        <p>COAGULINE,' KLINX," TENASITINE.' Cements for mending all things.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Examiner, Saturdax April 10, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturdax April 10, 1909.</p>
        <p>the majority of the Council are convinced that to be of the greatest use the bridge must be built as near the Weir and Halfpenny Bridge as possible. Scheme No. 2is the one with most to recommend it, and if reason- ably direct access from the Howley side can be secured the Council need look no further for a site.</p>
        <p>* * * -There was an amusing episode at the Police. Court on Thursday, which tends to show how unfamiliar some people are with the custoths of the Post Office. A working- man summoned by his wife for arrears of maintenance swore he had sent her £5 by means of -a money order. "Here is the receipt !" he exclaimed triumphantly, and promptly produced a crumpled piece of paper. The magistrates' clerk straightened it out. "Why, this is a money order for £5," he informed the Court. Defendant explained that he had mistaken the order for a receipt, and was under the impression that the postal authorities would forward the money to his wife. However, the order now changed hands, the wife was satisfied, and the case happily closed. * * * The members of the St. Alban's Reading Guild showed considerable public spirit in promoting a lecture in aid of the Warrington District. Nursing Association, and it was pleasing to find that their effort called forth a cordial response from the public. They were fortunate in securing the services of such an eloquent lecturer as Mr. William Madden whose address on "Joan of Arc" was an oratorical achievement on which he is to be sincerely congratulated. It was evident that he had made an exhaustive study of the history of the period, and his remarks were characterized by his usual directness and fearlessness, criticism being meted out wherever it was due. Few people, we venture to think, have now anything but the greatest respect and admiration for the memory of "the Maid of France," and Mr. Madden's lecture helped his hearers the better to understand her motives and the secret of that wonderful spell which she exercised over the French soldiers.</p>
        <p>iLOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>The Infirmary and Dispensary. The Honorary Treasurer begs to acknowledge with thanks, the receipt of the sum..of £l7 15s. 6d. from Sir Gilbert Greenall Bart., being proceeds of the sale of fishing tickets for Walton Reservoir for season 1908-9.</p>
        <p>Chess, Match at Warrington. The Stockport and Rock Ferry teams journeyed to the Patten Arms Hotel, Warrington, on Saturday, to compete in the final for the Cheshire Cup, and after three and a half hours' play the Westerners won the match and the cup by five point,' to three. Will of the Late Mr. J. Podmore. The will of Mr. Joseph Podmore, of 37, Allen-street, Warrington, retired hairdresser, who died on the ult., has been proved by his son, Mr. Alfred Podmore, secretary, of 98, Manchester-road, Warrington, and Mr. Sycliiey Walter Jeffrey. of 404, Manchester-road, schoolmaster, at S.SO4 Bs. 7d. PREMIER CYCLES, the world's best value. From five guineas.—J. L. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Co operative Exhibition. A free exhibition of Co-operative productions, promoted by the Warrington Co-operative .Society,w ill be opened in the Parr Hall on Saturday next and con- tinue until the following Saturday. The Mayor (Alderman Forshaw) will perform the opening cere- mony and an address will be delivered by Mr. William Lauder (C.W.S. director, Manchester). The leading local brass bands have been engaged, and there will be many unique and pleasing attractions. On Saturday the 24th inst., at 7.0 p.m., Mr. F. thddison.,M.P., will give an address. .</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER TIIR CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Singular Forge Accident. Some excitement and alarm were created at the forge of Messrs Monks, Hall and Co., Liverpool-road, at abut 3.30 on Monday afternoon, when an iron chimney weighing several tons, and 60ft. in height, attached to a furnace broke in. two. The breakage cceutred through the corrosion of the plates inside, and the falling piec,o broke through several steam and water pipes. Thera was a strong wind blowing at the time. 4. large number of men were working in the forge, but fortunately no one was injured. The damage done is estimated at about £lOO.</p>
        <p>LAWN MOWERS at store prices for cash. Repairs by special plant.—J. S. °ARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge st.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Penny Popular Concerts. -/ So popular are "The Sonnets," of Liverpool, with the patrons of these concerts that on Saturday evening they appeared for the second week in succession. The schoolroom was filled to its utmost capacity and many were tumble to gain admission. The programme which consisted of songs, recitals and sketches, was greatly appreciated. Wee Jennie, a child vocalist, who has established herself as a great favourite at these concerts, was presented by Mr. E. Plinston, on behalf of the Concert Committee, with a handsome box of chocolates. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Shakespeare - Theatre, Liverpool. _</p>
        <p>This gaturday night, the new comedy by Mr. W. Somerset Maugham, entitled "Mrs. Dot" will begin a seven nights engagement at the Shakespeare Theatre, Liverpool, with one matinee on Saturday April 17th, with Miss May Palfrey (Mrs. Weedon Grossinith) in the title part, Mrs. Dot. it is the first appearance of Miss Palfrey in, Liverpool for seven years, her last appearance being in the first v:sit of her husband's play "The Night of the Party." "Ms. Dot " is a light and bright piece of. work and makes ready appeal to lovers of good comedy. The play is preceded each evening by "Money and the G irl ' an otiginal duolofitue by F. Kinsey Peile.</p>
        <p>GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, £5 55., £8 Bs., and £l2 12s. Others at higher prices. Fully guaranteed. Pianos tuned and repaired,—J. It. WooD, 41. London rd., Stockton Heath. Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Death 'of a Crimean Veteran.</head>
        <p>Death 'of a Crimean Veteran.</p>
        <p>A familiar figure has been removed by the death which took place at the Workhouse,on Saturday morn- ing, of Sergeant Harold W. Hardy. The deceased, who was 79 years of age had had a very varied career and served in the Royal Eloise Artillery during the Crimean war. He had been an inmate of the Work- house Eince January, 1904, and some 12 or 15 months ago, when the movement fcr providinr:, means of livelihood for military veterans was much to the fore, he was offered a pension if he would leave the house, but declined to do S 3, as" he liked the Master very much and was very comfortable in the Workhouse, where he hcped to remain until he died." The cause of death was bronchitis. The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Wednesday afternoon, with 'full military honours. The Rev. W. Bracecamp officiated. The band from Orford &amp;tracks headed the procession and a firing party and an escort of sergeants were also in attendance. LAWN MOWERS at store prices for cash. Repairs by special plant.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, -Bridge st.—Advt. Works Competition. The Rugby Football Club Works Cora Petition in aid of local charities.</p>
        <p>FIRST ROUND.—Thursday, April Bth. Kick-off at.O. Whitecross v. P. and K. Dall;am No. 2.</p>
        <p>SECOND ROUND. Friday, April 9th. Kick-off 10.30. Orford Tannery v. Rylands' Bros. Apprentices. Friday April 9th. Kick-off 11.30. Rylands' Bros. No. Iv. Richmonds. Saturday, April 10th. Kick-off 10.30. Rylands' Bros. Wet Shop v. Greenings. Saturday, April 10th. Kick-off 11.30. St. Helens Cable v. " Guardian " Office. Monday, April 12th. Kick-off 10.30. Firth Co. v.:P. and K. No. 2. Monday, April 12th. Kick-off 11.30. P. and K. No. Iv. P. and K. Dallain No. 2 or Whitecross. Tuesday, April 13th. Kick-off 0. Crosfields' v. Greenall, Whitley and Co.</p>
        <p>FIRST ROUND.—Wednesday, April 14th. Rick-off 6. Fletcher Russell v. Warrington Corporation. SECOND ROUND (continued). Friday, April 16th. Kick-off 6. Locos v. Fletcher Russell or Warrington Corporation.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs &amp; Colds. Relieved thma and difficult breathing. 91d., is. lid.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Tea Party at Penketh. The Penketh Wesleyan Sunday school tea party was announced to take place yesterday and about 250 scholars turned up as usual with the anticipation of a, ple-:sant tea and entertainment. They were not dis- appointed, for their hopes were fully realised when they were at the tables and making merry over the good things provided. After the tea the entertainment followed; ol'Yer, which the Rev. F. W. Kernick, M.A., presided: Reports were presented by Mr. F. Davies (secretary). Mr. H. Davies (treasurer), Mr. H. Carman (secretary sick club), Mr. Harold Carman (librarian), Mr. F. Dunabin (band of hope). The musical pro- gramme, which was much appreciated included items from the following artistes :—Misses C.H. Baker, Eva Ray, Nora Plumpton, Messrs. F. Davies, J: Loxley, selected children's choir, and a mixed clioir. After the'concert Mrs. Jam3s Dodds presented the prizes. DRESS BASKETS, TRAVELLING Taumcs, etc. Large stock, reasonable prices.—J. S..GARLA.ND, 51 to 55, Bridge s'.—Advt. Latchford Wesleyan United Bible Classes.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above a grand concert was held in. the schoolroom on Wednesday, the proceeds being , in., aid of the Outdoor Benevolent Fund. The following. artistes contributed to the splendid programme which had been arranged by the com- mittee :Mr. A. Greenough was heard to advantage in "Evening Song" and "Mary," Miss S. Silcock and Mr. W. Dutton delighted the audience with their duets " When the wind blows ii from the sea' and "In the 4)ringthne," Miss Silcock also sang " Forget me not" as a solo. Miss Mabel Henshall sang" The Little- Irish Girl" in her usual effective way. Mr. Herbert Plumpton is the possessor of a very fine voice and his singing of" The Bandolero " and" The Admiral's Broom" was greatly enjoyed by the audience. The Excelsior Glee Party gave two selections "On the Ramparts" and "Crossing the Plains" and their rendering was Tine. They were under the leadership of Mr, W. Wood Cock who is to be congratulated upon the success of their efforts. Mr. Balmford gave two selections On the cello, a " Dream- ing," b, " Danse Rustique," which were much enjoyed, and the humorous part of the programme was in the hands of Mr. G. Bennett who acqutita himself very well. Mr. B. Forster acted as accompanist. TALBOT INVINCIBLE CARS. Trials by appoint- ment.—Ageut, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge • st.-7-AdYt.</p>
        <p>Death Of an Ex-Soldier and Postman. We announce with regret the death of Mr. Robert William Cleminson .of Latchford, which occurred on April Ist. Deceased who was only 29 years CT age joined the Royal Horse Artillery eighteen months prior to the South African war. He went out to ISouth Africa with his regiment in September of 1900, lin the " Manchester Merchant,' and whilst there he was engaged in nearly all of the principal engagements. In some of his letters borne he dwelt upon the incidents relating to the chase of Da Wet, in which he had a Iconsiderable share. He received the Queen's medal ' with four bars, and the King's medal with two bars for his work in the campaign. The week following the declaration of peace he was drafted to In Ha, where he remained until his return to England in October 1907. He then accepted his discharge and commenced work at the ,gle ctrici ty We rks, whe re he gained tlia respect and confidence of the employers and etnplorSi. Fpr the past month or so he had been engaged as a carrier for the Post Office, his distri.t being Whitley and Stream. He left his duties only a fortnight ago suffering from a severe cold, and death ensuel from an attack of pneumonia. The funeral took place at the Warring- ton Cemetery on Monday afteraoon, Mr. A. Wright officiating. . Hundreds were gathered at the Ceme!ery, and full military honours were accor Jed deceased. The Post Office was well reprasented and the chief mourners were: Messrs. Thotn)s and John Clemins3n (brothers), Miss Elizabeth Oleminson (sister), Mr. and His. John Metcalf (uncle and aunt), Mr. and Mrs. S. Thompson (uncle and aunt), Mrs. E. Mete klf (grandmother). Much sympathy is felt with the relatives in their bereavement. The funeral arrange- ments were carried out by Mr. T.„Glassey. PUBLIC BENi.FIT.—UNDER TUE gLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCA.SAIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Railway AmbulanceCOmpetition. Warrington" first-aid " men were well to the fora at the sixteenth annual ambulance competition in conneetion with the Chestlire Lines Railway which was held at the Centr4l Station Liverpoll, yesterday week. The object was the section of a team to repreAnt the Cheshire Lines in tire inter-railway competition AO be held in London on the 25th inst. Eleven'teams entered for the examination, which con- sisted of stretcher work and questions on how to treat a child .who has swallowed scalding water, the signs and symptoms of a strain and a sprain, individual injuries &amp;c., the judge b_ing Captain George Ashton, M.D., of Manchester (examiner to the St. John Ambulance Association.) The tes's occupied the whole of the day, • and the teams were finally placed in the following order ofi merit Northwich, 2591 marks; 2, Warrington Station, 246 k; 3, Huskisson, 223-; 4, Warrington Engineers, 210 ; 5, Manchester Central, 201 ; 6, Birkenhead, 169 ; 7; Southport, 1671 ; 8, Blunswick, 165}; 9, Stockport, 154; 10, Livirpool (Central), 1511; and 11, Warrington (Stores), 144. The members of the local leading teams were : —Warrington Central Station : H. Holloway (leader), James Fogg, J. T. Robertson, IT. A. Roberts, W. Hulton and H. Ince (reserve). Warrington Engineers.: T. Downie (leadet), J. Lee, R. Brownbill, J. Smith, W. Clements, and W. Gibson (reserve,) Prizes were awarded to the members of these teams, Northwich becoming, in addition, the holders for another twelve months of the Cheshire Lines Challenge Cup. This is the third year in succession that the Northwich team has secured this coveted honour.—After the contests, the prizes were presented by Mrs. Pinion,the wife of the general manager. Captain Waring, chief superintendent of the Warrington Corps. was present and proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Pinionfor presiding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural District Cauncil was held in Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday afternoon. 'More were present: Dr.Murmy (vice-chairman), Messrs. R. Forshaw, G. Mather, A. Brooks, W. Furness, J. Ball, W. Houghton, J. C.Sutton (clerk), B. S. Newcombe (surveyor), H. H. Burngga (assistant surveyor), F. %V. Kerfoot (inspector), Dr. Sephton (medreal officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>SANKEY SANITARY QUESTION.</head>
        <p>SANKEY SANITARY QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said a tender hid been received from Mr. Whitfield last October undertaking the work of sanitary collection at Great Sankey for RHO. He had received a letter to the effect that the Sankey Parish Council had again discussed the question of sanitary colrection and at a special meeting it was resolved that a letter be written informing the District Council that Mr. Robert Woods had said he was willing to undertake the work that Mr. Whitfield h left. They considerad that Mr. Woods was a suitable man. _ .</p>
        <p>The CLERK then read a letter from Mr. Woods saying that he had been requested by a number of ratepayers to take up theNfork. He was fully equipped with the necessary articles to do the work, and would follow out the Inspector's instructions and comply w:th th3ir byelaws. He concluded 'by stating that he could start the work at once.</p>
        <p>The CLERK wrote to Mr. Woods saying that he did not mention any tender. Ile promised to stnd a tend3r, but he had not yet received a tender from him.</p>
        <p>The CLERK also said he had received a letter from Mr. John Hamill!, Sankey, complaining of the present system of collecting night soil. After some desultory discussion on the question Mr. BROOKS said: If Great Sankey Parish Council cant t wants rate, it is for them to obviate it. If they t us to do the work we must do it through the rate. He h en moved the following resolution: "That the Clerk be instructed to inform the Parish Council of :heat Sankey that unless satibf.ictory arrangements have been niacin by them for the collection of the whole )f the night soil of the parish on or before Wednesday 21st last, the Rural Distant, Council will take the necessa,y steps to undertake the work'and charge the cost to a special rate on the Parish." Mr. MATHER seconded. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. FURNESS said the resolution would not help the Parish Council any further. In the interests of all the ratepayers concerned, it would be more economical to levy a rata for the collection of nightsoil. The sooner the District Council recognised their responsibility in the matter the better. He said it would affect the farmers more than anybody else. The residents numbered eight or nine to one of the farming class. The amount which would be required for the purpose would be very small taking into consideration the fact that the farmers would only have to pay about a quarter of the rate levied. The resolution W3S unanimously passed. MEASLES AT WINWICK.</p>
        <p>The CLERK read a letter from the Winwick Parish council, which stated that there was an epidemic of measles, and that the schools had to be closed for three weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>MORE LIGHT WANTED.</head>
        <p>MORE LIGHT WANTED.</p>
        <p>The CLERK also read a letter from the Winwick Parish Council asking for an additional lamp to be fixed near to the top of Hollin's-lane and close to the western door of the Church.</p>
        <p>LINSEED CO NIPOUND with warm water is an cxeellent gargle for sore throat.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Mr. NEWCOMBE said it would involve a new main. It was decide 3 that Mr. Newcombe should report upon it. . • •</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK read a letter from the Clerk to the Lanclshire County Council saying that he had submitted their letter to the Main Roa is and Bridges Committee, when they decided not to take any action with regard to the proposed widening of the Sinkey Bridge until the Warrington Corporation decide to help them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>D ITC(' POLLUTION.</head>
        <p>D ITC(' POLLUTION.</p>
        <p>Inspentor KERFOOT said that he had complaints from ne Evans (Lord Lilford's agent) re3p3cting the pollu- tion of a ditch at Cuerdley. The Widnes Corporation let the effluent go into a ditch from Widnes.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the Surveyor and Inspector see the Surveyor of the Widnes CJrpDration.</p>
        <p>CHAIRMAN THANKED.</p>
        <p>Mr. BROOM, in proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman for his past year's work, said that it had been his privilege to propose D. Murray for the chairman- ship for two years. There was nothing that gave him more pleasure than t) submit the vote of thanks, con- sidering the servic3s the chairman had rendered during the past year. (Hear, hear). Mr. BALL, in seconding, said he had great pleasure in doing so. The Council had been well conducted daring thel past year, and their work had been very successful and satisfactory. Mr. MATHER,- in supporting, slid he had great envy for Dr. Murray. Considering the questions put to him by the Council on different occasions, he had thought to himself "How in the world is Dr. Murray going to gat out of this ? " but he always had an answer ready. A min like Dr. Murray only made it harder for his successor to satisfy the Council'. (Applause). Mr.FORSHAW also supported. Dr. MURRAY, in replying, said he had only tried to do his best for that Council and he would not have been able to do, but for the members' valuable help. He had always tried to uphold the dignity of that Council. Referring to the Council's work, Dr. Murray thought they did n sit long enougii ant he had noted that after an hour had gone of the sitting there was a great iideetiness. He sincerely hoped tint something would be done and arraingements mide to improve matters in this respect. (Applause). A vote of thanks to Mr. Forshaw,as deouty chairman concluded the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>MR. E. J. HALL.</head>
        <p>MR. E. J. HALL.</p>
        <p>Presentation From the Warrington ' Society.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. Hill, who recently resigned the offiae of joint hon.sacrettry of the Warrington Sozietv after tena years' enthusiastic and valuable service, was on Wed- nesday evening the recipient of a token of estesm from his fellow-members. The gift took the form of a handsome silver salver, the work of Messrs. Eustane and Co., which bore the following inscription":—" Pre- sented to E. James Hall by his friends and fellow- members on his retirement from the position of joint hon. secretary of the Warrington Society in recognition of his ten years' services and as a token of their personal affection and esteem, 7th April, 1909." .Mr. J. Longland, chairman of the Executive Committee,presided over a largo gathering at the Old Ac rde ny and numerous speakers bore testimony to Mr. Hall's geniality and to his tactful discharge of the secretarial duties. Letters and messages regretting insbility to attend and eulogising Mr. Hall's work were received from Mr. William Long (President), Mr. F. Monks, Rev. C mon Stevenson, Councillor Fulton Smith, Mr. J. W. Slack, Mr. J. H. Smethurst and Mr. Walter Crompton. Mr. F. Monks wrote :—" We owe very m rch to Mr. Hall's tact and judgment for the success in starting and working the society, but above all for the example of his steady, persevering w irk in all the institutions with which he is connect3d. Ais a red pleasure to MP, who have laid down the armour of public life,to see young men put it oa and do battle for the right as agrinst might in any feral. There is plenta. of real herd work iniront of the younger men, but not more than we had to face fifty to sixty years ago. Liberty and progress are still the watchwords in every department of thought and action and I rejoice with you in bearing testimony to one who has deserved well of his fellOw-townscaen."</p>
        <p>The CHAIRAIAN aaid no society or body ever had a more devoted and loyal officer than the Warrington Society bad had in Mr. Hall. His motto in carrying out the duties of his office, as in every position which he occupied, hid been "thorough" and those who had been privileged to work with him on the committee testified to the diligence and care with which ha had performed his work. But it was not so much that trait which had impressed itself upon them as the other qualities which he had exhibited—the unfailing tact and good temper which he had invariably shown in dealing with the many ani diverse questions which must of nscessity ariss among suoh a commuoity as theirs, the intelligent interest which he had displayed in their various works, fancies and tatstesiand last but not least the forbearance and patience he had exercised with their many little pet, tdiosvncracies. Mr. Hall had, in shert, endeared him- self to theta all and bad proved himself an ideal secre- tary. After a reference to the long personal friendship which had existed between Mr. Hall and himself, Mr. Longland handed the salver to Mr. Hall, amidst loud applanse. Alderman BENNETT, as Mr. Hall's colleague in the secretaryship, said Mr. Hall was one of his oldest and dearest friends. For nearly a quarter of a century they had been associated in all sorts of projects, private pleasures an 1 amosemeats, and to a large extent in public work. Alderman Bennett observed that soma years ago he penned some lines about Mr. Hall, Iron which he would quote the following . . _</p>
        <p>An honest Meld who failed me never Nor over asked a loan of me.</p>
        <p>The very existence of that society, continued the Alderman, was very largely due to Mr. Hall who bad been full of infinite enthusiasm for the best aims of the organization. Mr. John Fairhurst, Mr. L. Greening, and Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins each added a few eulogistic words. Mr. Henry ROBERTS slid that as a man who had passed through times of great tribulation he knew what it. was to have found in Mr. Hall, as he had found in others, what true personal friendship really meant. No man had ever been more blessed than he (the speaker) had been in regard to the friendship and affection he had received for so Tattly years at the hands of Mr. flag and his family. He never knew a man who had such a velvet glove oa such aa iron hand as Mr. Hall had. (14aughte.). Mr. HALL, in acknowledging the gift, said he valued most of all the spirit of friendliness which had prompted such a signal acknowledgment of serVicos which, looking backward, seemed to be very slight and fugitive. It was difficult to exaggerate for what the Warrington Society had stood during the last ten years. There was not at any time any public movement in the higher tone in Warrington but members of that society were in the forefront. The Society was formed in the first instance to preserve as a local monument that building which Mr. Pali() had so well described as "The most interesting house in Warring- ton." To do that was mucii in itself, but from the outset he felt the opportunity must be seized for more than that. Without effeminaoy,—for good fell owstlip was to be at the root of seemed to him that that was a grand ch ince to form a home for public spirit. It was his idea that public spirit should be encouraged and famed into flame from that centre and also that men of affairs in the town might there rest and relax from strenuous work in genial company. He still looked forward to great days for the Acaderny, both on its public and social side. (Applause.) A vote of thanks was accarded to the Ch iirman on the motion of Alderman BENETT, seconded by Mr. LOFT- HOUSE and supparted by Mr. F. R. ROBERTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Local Membars' Votes.</head>
        <p>Local Membars' Votes.</p>
        <p>The first part of the Parliamentary Session of 1009_ came to a close on Wednesday .evening, when the House of Commons rose for the Easter Recess. Since Parliament met, on February 16th, there have been 64 divisions, which compares with 78 last year. L'ical members took part in the following divisions :—Ur. A. H. Crosfield (Warrington), 23; Mr. A. J. King (Knutsford Division), 40; Mr. J. A. Seddon (Newton division), 52; •Sir J. T. Brunner (Northwich), 16; Mr. W. J.Crossley (Altrincham), 10; Mr. T.Glover (St. Helens), 31; Colonel W. Hall Walker (Widnes), 21; Hon. A. L. Stanley (Eddisbury division), 32,</p>
        <p>During the recent by-election at Chelmsford the Suffragettes chalked the place and time of their meet- ings on the pavements. To prevent this in future the Town Council has asked the Home Secretary to sanction a by-law the penalty for a breach of it being fixed at "a fine not exceeding .C 5.</p>
        <p>Arr. G. S. Bowles, M.P., was fined £5 awl costs on Wednesday at Bow-street Police Court, London for driving his motor-car in ,the Green Park at a greater speed than ten miles an hour. It was stated that in February last he was fined for a similar offence.</p>
        <p>The Berlin clrrelpondent of the+ " Morning Leader " says that one of the waaltLiest hall porters in Europe has just retired into privats life—that of the famous Lahmann Samtorium near Dresden. The porter, who has only had ten years' service, made an annual incame of £2,500 out of tips,</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Tennant, a nephew of Mrs. Asquith, was descending a hill near Wareham on a motor cycle when he collided with a cut. He was thrown to the ground insensible, and was found to be suffering from con- cussion of the brain. Mr. Tennant was onLWeinetthy progresing favourably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>GOOD FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>GOOD FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>How the Day was Observed at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Good Friday was an ideal spring day nd brilliant sunshine and a cooling breeze, made out-door life a pleasure. Many people left the town for seaside and rural resorts and the country roads were alive with cyclists and 'motorists- The religious aspect of the day received due observ- ance at many of the places of worship. At all the Anglican and Roman Catholic Churches there were services, one of the chief of these being the three hours' service with addresses on the Seven Last Words from the Cross. The Rev. Canon Stevenson conducted this devotion at the Parish Church and in the evening the choir song " The Way of the Cross " (Dr. Iliffe.) There was morning service at Wycliffe Congregational Church, the Rev. John Yonge preaching. At Bold- street Wesleyan Church the foreign missionary anniversary was continued, the Rev. E. Lloyd Jones,. of Manchester, preaching in the morning and deliver- ing a powerful lecture on "Christianity and other religions " in the evening. Football enthusiasts gathered in large numbers on the Wilderspool enclosure where the match between Warrington and 'Halifax—always a great attraction— was played. BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>VISIT OF TIIE REV. E. LLOYD-JONES. The Rev. E. Lloyd-Jones preached in B Ad-street Wesleyan Church yesterday morning, in connection with the Foreign Missions. He look his text from the 3rd Phillipians 9 and 10 verses, and slid that the chapter described St. Paul's conversion after 2) years had elapsed. No man, however soundly converted, knew all that it meant, and just when the conversion took place. It was after a stretch of years that he was quite able to understand his conversion. Paul gave up barbarism because he saw that it was in the lower nature and not in the higher. His old religion was the religion of the flesh, and the effect on his character wls detrimental. In Christ he saw a new and a better way of righteousness. Since the birth of Unitarianism they had been told that salvation by grace demoralised, and that it weakened men insteal of strengthened them. The best 'answer to that came from an unlikely sour, James Martineau, who said: "Considered logically s alvatiqba by gr ice demoralises and weakens; but conside-ed historically it has not been so." They should read the hiftoryof good men and women to see whether salvation by grace demora- lised. The man who did not believe in salvation by grace was, as a rule, a hard and nnsympathelic man. He also had a tremendous contempt for a weak willed man, because, he himself had a strong will. All Paul's belief he converted into fore and us .d them for the strengthenine of his character. The value of a creed was in its pulling power—the power which pulled them from despair and sin into' the region of gladness. In conclusion he said the value of a creed was not that it satisfied their intellectual curiosity but that it became to them a living power.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING. REV. E. LLOYD JONES ON "CHRISTIANITY AND OTHER RELIGIONS."</p>
        <p>In the evening the annual missionary meeting wait held, Mr. E. J. Guest presiding over a large attendance The Rev. THOMAS MILLER presented the connexiona report in the course of which he said that the total income of Wesleyan Methodist Foreign Missionary Society from all sources for the year was £200,668 and the total expenditure £201,116. They had 307 missionaries in immediate connection with the confer- ence. There were also 4,197 paid agents and 112,680 members in the mission field. The amount raised on the mission field as income by the native churches was £193,000. In presenting the report of the Warrington circuit the rev. gentleman siid that the gross income was £286 is. made up of the follow"ng items :—Bold street £3B 4s. 5d., Bewsey-road £33 103., Ssnyey Bridge £6 ss. Bcl., Latchford £9 9s. 9J., Ellesmere-stree £3 14s. 9 7d. Lymm £2O Os. 10d,. Penketh £5l 12s. 9d. Moore £2l 9s. 93., Whitley £7 18s. 2d., Antrobus £ll 125., Appleton £lO us. 6d., Padget() £36 17s. ld. Martinscreft £6 2sll 91., Stockton Heath £8 Bs. d. The Juvenile Foreign Missionary Society of the Bold- strest had collected £2l 3s. 42, being an increase of nearly £l4 on the previous year. Bewsey-road juvenils. had collected £l3 6s. 6d., which is an increase of Xis In addition to this the cengreea ion at Bold-street had raised £l6 4s. for Home Missions, and Bewsey-road £8 17s. 6d• In conclusion the rev, gentleman said that they aimed at £3OO for this year. The Rev. E. LLOYD-JONES delivered a lecture on "Christianity and other religions." He said that of all the studies in whi6h the Christian Charch could be employe,d, he knew of nothing more helpful and reassur- ing .to the Christian faith than the study of the success of missions at the preseut day. The story of the Acts of the Apostles could tilt be compared, as far as success was canc3rned, to the story of the madam missionary. That was true of all the work among all raeas of people. At the present time Japan was occupying much atten- tion. Thirty-seven ye trs ago death was the penalty of anyone who professed Christianity in Japan. Now one out of every ten of the mern'aers of the Japanese Parliament were Christians,±although Christians did not number more than one in five husdred of the population, that showed the great respect the Japanese people had for Christi tnity. Missioa work began in Japan in 1859, and in five years the result was oie convert; in tsvelvo years there were ten; in 1892 there were twenty-seven societies. There were 601 flreign missionaries, 365 churches giving a total me nbersbip of 35.000. The same proportion might le claimed of India and China. The present objection to missions was owing to the study of comparative religion. The questions were asked, why disturb other faiths? Why not leave every country to the care of its own religion? His answer was, because all countries having their own religions were deteriorating and not improving. None of the old Oriental creeds were as gool as they were at the beginning. Certainly a change had come over the Christian Church in reference to the possibility of salvation of the heathen, even before they heard of Christianity. The idea that irspivation was the exclusive property of one nation to he found only in one Book callel the Bible, was exploded owing to three things : The clearer conception of the love and justice of .God, archcelogical discoveries and the •</p>
        <p>publication of the text books Of Oriental religions. They could not reconcile with justice, or with love the idea of a man being lost because of the accident of his birth. It was very strange that our forefathers who held the exclusive salvation of Christians did not see the• Catholic element in the Old Testament and a vein of generosity to be found in many prophets of the Old Testameat in regard to the salvation of the nations. It was strange that such a verse as one found in Malachi did not occur to them and this was one out of the scores that might he adduced. " From the rising of the Sun even to the going down of the same My name is great among the Gentilea. and in every place incense is offered unto My name and a pure offering saith the Lord of Hosts." —a clear illustration of the fact that God accepts offerings made even. to other gods when made with sincerity, It was strange that from the New Testament they did hot') learn that any other nation that feared God and worked for righteousness was accepted of Him, but notwithstanding the good that was undoubtedly found even in other religions their salvation must be ultimately assured from Christian ttuth. in conclusion he said his belief W3S admirably put by an educated Hindu who wrote " I have given the question of social reform my best thought and attention these 12 months. My conviction is that the liberal education of women, and the consequent happinest of the homeis only possible in the Christian community. It is Christianity that permits the postponement of the marriage of girls ; it is Christianity that allows widows to re-marry ; it is Christianity that gives to fallen women a chance of reclaiming themselves from evil ways; it is Christianity that teaches the dignity of labour ; it is Christianity that allows facilities for being rich, wise, and philanthropic ; it is Christianity that gives salvation, not laborious and multifarious ceremonies. If ever the Hindu are to rise in the scale os nations it must, be by Christianity and by Christianity only."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>FORTHCOIIING BIG MEETING. The Lord Advocate to Visit Warrington.</head>
        <p>FORTHCOIIING BIG MEETING. The Lord Advocate to Visit Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. Alexander Ure, 1..L.8., K.C., the Lord Advo- cate of Scotland, will visit Warrington on Saturday, May Bth, to address a great meeting in the Parr Hall promoted by the Warrington Liberal Association in Support ,of land refonn. Mr. Ure is reputed to be one of the finest speakers of the day and he has made a special study .of this pressing question. He has represented Linlithgowshire in the Liberal interest since 1893, having contested Perthshire in the previous year. Mr. Ure, who was born in 1853, was educated at Glasgow and Edinburgh Uni- versities and was called to the -Scotch Bar in 1878. From 1878. to 1888 he was a lecturer on constitutional law and history at Glasgow Univerity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>BLUE-GREY CATTLE.</head>
        <p>BLUE-GREY CATTLE.</p>
        <p>There is no class of commercial beef cattle which command so high a price in the market as the blue- greys. This is particularly the case when they are at a growing stage. In large auction marts where all breeds are sold publicly to the highest bidder, yearlings and two year old steers of this sort realise about 4s. to ss. more per cwt., liye weight, than equally well-bred specimens of other breeds. This is mainly due to their early-maturing qualitie3; but it is also to be attributed to the fact that they have become very fashionable, and are extensively purchased to be grazed in the parks of noblemen and gentlemen.—" Country Life."</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND 'for Coughs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. 01 Chemists only. 91d.,18.10,28.8d.-Advt</p>
      </div>
