      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Warrington Woman Sent to the Assizes. REMARKABLE CASE,</head>
        <p>Warrington Woman Sent to the Assizes. REMARKABLE CASE,</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Wednesday, before Mr. J. W. Hayward (chairman), Aldermen Ellison and Smethurst, and Councillor Dr. Joseph, Annie Smith, Garibaldi-street, Warrington, was charged with feloniously forging certain receipts for money purporting to be received by Joseph Smith 11 re for payment of £l4 from his account in the arringto Savings Bank, with intent to -defraud. Mr. Lindon Riley prosecuted on behalf of the trustees of the Warrington Savings Bank, who he said had no feelings of vindictiveness, bat on the contrary there was a feeling of sympathy. The Bank had a very large constituency. The open accounts for the year ending in November last were no less than £15,000, 'affecting between a queeter and half a million depositors. There had born irregularities prectieed by a depositor or some ,ne affecting to be acting on behalf of a depositor thus depleting the funds of the Bank by an amount of £lBO. The view of the trustees was that there nets only one course open to them—to bring the parson suspected before a proper tribunal. The facts were that the pr'sonerle husband. Joseph Smith, opened an account with the Bank in the year 1891, and the credit standing to that account on 17th /enlist, 1907, was £177 ss. 11d: On the 2nd May, 908, the whole accouut plus interest and a dividend on Consols which had accrued in the meantime, makiug a total of £lB3, had been withdrawn by 22 separate accounts of withdrawal in sums ranging from £4 to £lO. On one occasion in the month of February there were withdrawals at the rate of £lO a week. The whole account had been depleted in that eves,. The whole of that tie:emit had been withdraen and all the rnachieery in connection with those 22 different withdrawals had been fraudulently set up by the prisoner. The rules of the bank provided that a depositor must either attend himself to withdraw, or in case of inconvenience, he could send the btobk ebb en authority to receive the money on his behalf. Ihe prisoner herself attended with what purported to be the authority of her husband. She also presented a form that was professed to to signed by her husband, witnessed by a fictitious person. The signatures of these witnesses were all in the hand- writing of the prisoner. On those facts coming to the knowledge of the Committee_, and having regard to the feet that the accused was the wife of the depositor, the Savings Bank did what the Act of Parliament pro- vided when a dispute arose. They arbitrated with the Secretary of the Friendly Societies where the lo.ss was to fall and the Registrar saidlit was to fall on the b,nk. They lad restored the money to Mr. Smith. The loss therefore had fallen on the bank. In conclusion he said the evidence was very simple and direct. sWiliiam A. Proud examined by Mr. Riley said be hr d 'rem connected w ith the hank for 21 years. He had been 5 years the actuary. He produced the rules. On the 29th May 1891 Joseph Smith 23 Welltleld-street opened an eceonnt with the bank. He was therein described as a taker. On the 17th August 1907 there ' stood to his credit £177 ss. 11d. On that die, the prisoner called at the Bank and saw witness and handed three documents to him. No. 2 eves a letter that pur- ported to come from Joseph Smith asking for £4 as he was not able to-come. Witness knew that she was the wife of the deposit. r. Joseph Smith was formerly a baker but at tlat time he was it labourer. He referred to the signature of the depositor written in 1891. He accepted the documents from her and got her to rep' her name at the foot. Edmund Osborne, cashier, paid her .£4 on August 17th 1907 in his presence. On the 17th. December 1908 the prisoner and her husband appeared at the Ben k. Fram the 17th August 1907 there had been 22 withdiawals from the account. On December 17th 1908 he produced all the documents releting to the account end all the documents relating to the withdrawals including the document relating to the withdrawal of £6 cn the 31st August 1007. Joseph Smith repudiated any responsibility for these withdraeals. He said the signatures on the forms were not his. Witness at that interview asked prisoner if she had signed the top receipt, 17th- August, 1907, and the said "1 did." He tented over several withdrawal forms and asked her if she had signed the lot, and she answered in the affirmative. She ridded that she had only spent the money on the household, and had not -spent a half-penny on herself. Afterwards she said she bad given ber sister £4 and she bad also paid two lures to Newcastle. Witness asked her who Fred Bolton, who eurperted to be a witness, was and she said "There is no Mr. Boltoe, I wrote it." The trustees hod restored £lB3 and interest to Mr. Smith. Mr. Browne (for the prisoner) then cross-examined witness who said that be had known Mr. Joseph Smith as a bard working and saving man for many years. Up to the time of his marriage he either attended the bank himself or sent hie Mother. He believed at the time that the signatures on the documents were genuine There was a similarity between the three signatures. .4ehe did not get the money so easily, for he had the signature examined, lie admitted that on the 2nd December last he knew that -the money bad been fradulently d awn. Mr. Browne: My contention is that the admission obtained at the interview was got in consequence of the inducement held out by Mr. Proud. Witness: That is not so. Mr. Browner Did you promise to report to the managers the offer of Joseph Smith to shere half the loss ? Witness: No. I repudiated liability. I told him I would report it to the Committee of Management. Mr. Browne: If you did not make terms with Mr. Smith, why didn't you issue a warrant? With( ss : The facts were not in the hands of the Committee.. Mr. Browne Why were four mouths allowed to elapse? Witness : Because the whole facts of the case were not before the Committee. Mr. Browne : Why not before the Committee. Witness: Because it was not possible to present the whole facts to the Committee. liMr. Browner Why wasn't a warrant issued ?- • The facts were not in the hands of the Committee. Witness centimilug his evidence said that a warrant was issued for the prisoner's arrest, en the Ist April, 1909. Answering Mr. Browne he said it was not for Lim to state whether any proceedings would have been taken if the Registrar General had favoured the bank. They were not awaiting the result Of the award to see what they would do. In answer to Mr. Riley witness said he wrote to Messrs. Browne and Co., solicitors, stating that the matter would be laid before the Committee at their next meeting. Answering the bench he said he did not lead Mrs. Smith to believe that-the decision of the Registrar General would settle everything. Edmund Osborne, cashier at the Warrington Savings Bank, said he was present on the 17th August, 1007, when the prisoner came. She presented both documents purporting to be signed by Mr. Smith. He paid her £6 on the strength of that, and she signed for it. Ile was present at the interview between Mr. Proud and Mr.' and Mrs. Smith on the 17th December last. He heard Joseph Smith repudiate the signatures and the prisoner admitted that she had written them. In answer to Mr. Browne, witness said it was not his duty to examine the signatures that had been-passed by the counter clerk. Herbert Hardy said he lived at 14, Lloyd-strect, ' Warrington. Since June 1897 until that time no one of the name of Bolton had lived there. Detective Sergeant Meacock stated that in company ith Detective Iteddlngton on Thursday, lit April, at about 7.50 in the evening, he saw prisoner at the dwelling-house, 14, Garibaldi-street. He read the warrant over to her and cautioned her. She made no reply but sent for her husband, and on his coming he asked her if she had drawn £4 and she replied" Yes, at first." Witness took her to the Police Statioh and again read the warrant and she said "I drew the I money." Prisoner's husband stated to witness that he thought the matter was settled and at an end. Frank Hatton, a clerk at the Warrington Savings Bank, said he was present in the bank on the 31st August; 1007, along with Mr: Osborne, when the prisoner presented the withdrawal form of that date. The form was accompanied by is letter asking</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Elnrder Charge at Warrington.</head>
        <p>Elnrder Charge at Warrington.</p>
        <p>CASE DISMISSED.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court before Mr. 11. Roberts (Chairman end other magistrates), on Tuesday, Beatrice Betley (19), a single woman,4l, Parr-street, was charged with the wilful murder of her child on FebruarylSth.— Mr. F. G. Frayling prscee3nted on behalf of the Director of Public Prosecutions,and MrsT.S.Steel,defended.—Mr. Fr-ruling stated that an ierqusst ,sas.held en the body of the child, but the jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," not knoe lag how' the injuries, which consisted of a wound so the right eye, fracture of the skull, and scratches on the breast, shoulder and cheek were caused. On the morning of the 16th of February, the prisoner did not go to work and about dinner time her mother had occasion to go out for a few minutes. On arriving back she was suddenly called upstairs by the prisoner and told to look iri the front room where she would find a baby. The mother did so and found a child lying by the fenfler, with a skirt thrown over it. The strange part of the case was -that althopgh the girl was residing with her mother in-Parr- street until Fequary 15th and the moth-:r swore that she did not kiviw of her daughter's condition. The ' prisoner used some serious threats both to herself and the child. When charged the prisoner made no reply.- -A mass of evidence sir-railer to that tendered at the inquest was then heard.—Ellen Hunter, midwife, Mersey-street, stated thst she was called to the prisonsr. When she picked up the child it began to cry, and prisoner said Put your hand over its mouth or my dadda will hear it." She also said " Give it to rue and I will throw it out of the wineow." The child was suffering from the injuries as described.—ln answer to Mr. Steel, witness said prisoner did net appear to be in a frenzy when she seoke.—Mr. Steel (to witness) : Did you tell Sergeant Stuart thst the prisoner looked wild when you visited her the first. day ?—Witness: I cannot tell you.—Mr. Steel : Did you tell him that the girl appeared to he out of her right mind ?—I cannot tell you.—Did you tell the Coroner that ?—She 'MIS wild in her tilk.—You were under the impression that she was out of her mind ?—Wou are trying to make me tell filet of lies.—Did you not tell the coroner that she talked in a crazy way ?—Yes.—Do you attach an importance to the girl's statement as .to her wanting to throw the child out of the window ?—No.—Dr. Manson described the child's injuries and stated that in his opinion the cause of death was a fracture of the skull. He did not think the injuries could have been caused accidentally; he consider..rd violence must have been used. The injuries were very extensive and the force applied must have been great.—ln answer to Mr. Steel witncss said he was of opinion that prisoner had had an epileptic fit before he reached the house, and it was probable that she did no know what she was doing or saying. Witeess thought the girl had been in a frenzied state since the child was born, and would have no recollection of what took place.—The Bench then discussed the matter "in private, and on their return the Chairman said there wftS not sufficient evidence to justify them to convict the girl on such a serious charge.—Mr.Steel : therefore apply to you for her discharge.—The Chairman: Yes, she is discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>SAT-I:RDA:Y.—Before Messrs. J. W. • Hayward (chair- man), H. T. Fairclough, T. Pemberton and Dr. Joseph. SHOP TIERET.—Peter Carey, Back 1/Illarn-lane, and Thomas Hickman, Hamilton-street, were charged with stealing four pails of stockings, value 4s. 4d., f,orn the shop of Mr. H. J. Pearee. Buttermarket-street, the previous Day.—P.C. Newsy stated that he was on duty at Market-gate when he was informed of a quarrel going on in Battermarket-street. He proceeded there and the two prisoners ran away up Academy-street. A young man brought them back, and said they had been stealing some stockings. Witness took them into custody and they were locked up. The young man who helped the police stated that he saw Carey with the stockings protruding from his pocket. Witness ran after him up Acaden.ystreet, brought him back, gave him into the custody of Newey, and captured Hickman in Bank-street. —Carey said he stole the goods because he wanted a drop of drink.—Hickman stated that be was at the back of the " Cross Keys "in Buttermarket- street and met Carey. - The stockings dropped from Carey's pocket and Carey told him that he could keep thera.—The Chief Constable stated that the previous night he heard a convers tion between the tw o prisoners, in which one prisoner was saying that he would take all the blame, but he could not tell which priseter it was. Carey had been eight times previously before the court -and Hickman five times.—They were each sentenced to a month's imprisonment. _ - _ -</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON THE POLICE—John Delooze, Ship-yard, Warriugton's only " blacidister," WAS charged with being drunk and disorderly and also with assaulting P.C.'s Claike (10) and Cuombs the previous day. He pleaded guilty.—P.C. Clarke stated that about 9 p.m. he was on duty in Pinners' Brow when he saw the prisoner behaving in a disorderly manner. He remon- strated with him and subsequently hsd to tbke him into custody, thereupon prisoner became very violent and kicked witness several times.—P.C. Coombs went to his assistance and he also received kicks from the prisoner. They succeeded with difficulty in getting prisoner to the Police Station but on the way he became exceedingly violent and P.C. Gibbs bad to come to their help. The three conktables had to carry prisoner the remaining distance to the station. A large crowd gathered round but no one offered to help the police.— P.C. Coombs corroborated and said that prisoner bit Mtn several times on the finger and thumb of the right hand.—Prisoner had 45 previous convictions.—The Chief Constable stated that the police ought to be pro- tected while in the execution of their duty. Last year there %%ere fifty, per cent. more assaults than the present year and police officers bad been laid up for</p>
      </div>
