        <p>2,144' ...-:, • ..• ~.„ ~,,, i ~,,...t. -z-,--- •:,...L,,, ...,,,,te-4- • Zr•t7,... , , ....„. : . .:.,,,,..„: ..1:k„.:;4.: . .14,.....,,,?....i0,..*:_44, •.- et;'t'r•e'''j,i: t. ap4i.... . ...,;.a.-. 7_,,!..4.:, ....,... ... --...k5.1....:-.4.;*•:-;f1t,%., /i 4 ,t 5 *xl,. .;-..;Ri.;.;t '.r",,,,A, •-•.'` .., te-:i.;:izt, '!.....:::•%4-... -44",.rf:' ...., -.44:-.-:.*Plt. ~.;.-...i „i.1444f. ~i VI A A". ... , ..;.- - •.• •!-• • . /;.;. ..... - ...t, ..r. Psi •:. 1.,:: '''''''',.'"'t.- 13.:: . .e I ..,1740 •- • .., 4,4 A, ',../tiV Zi.';'• sitEr.g.: ;.•,,,C.V:.••• ..iii, 647:: ...,..:4 . • ...... ... ......:,.. ......§i.rer:.: .. J.-.„,..4, .......•,, W. 4.:, . , i.4i. .....11.•,`' ot• .7 .41. ..-...` •-.-....,,-,.. ~,r,„. -..-•,:: 'tt.'",-.., •-,--; .v, .1...,;-, S-;:sz.i ~....,1t1,1,_ .. 1 ,• • • ..k-/,, - e.,;-• -...--.7 icria." = -. • - :"..,.. . - -,74`i . ' ...:11" . :,:f... ~..•;.,.. .....7.1,?, .4,,,,,t-;.,-„, ... s ~...,., ........., .7., ,),...4.. ,7.4,...ir,.3-...... . •,;-2:-...,', ---::... ''••• • • . . ..• 1, .. -..:5:„...,..„.., , ~ . "-t7•4= kz'l,--...:. t':,*::,t.., ' .- .-"e,*•• ~.&lt;o'f t*.".. ~ . _ 4 .5.'42ft.,..,t•_.-- , ..,..e:-- -,-,'•l-:41.7...,;:,- . --,..' - ..,.. ....,.. -. -.: .;:•' .."-- •• . •••••_ .--VI 1. --t2,-.--2--,:ii: . 4-...., ..:. .:.•,-.::: : -35* --,..-,:••• •-5 -:-.-- ?:t.is:. tv:.-••..44,, . - ,' :.: i--hlk 0 ....2#4:- I, • -1?-, .c. --w7:-:it.• .. ', • .___. .--. • , • .... z •,..-.-.1. -....„ -•- . .. .... .- _-- - ... • .....4..,,,- ~,-..e........ ,:.1.,•--- ---- 4''''P . ~• i.....• • . •::„t.,,, _ ...., ..-.._ r. • 5.-- ~ . ...,... ...- r. ... -,.., -- ' • ',:, .P.;;F.i."...-Er ,--='" .. . - . --. :..., ...... - .-......., - '.'</p>
        <p>case of the Highway Department consider Able reductions were made in con mittee from the figm es originally submitted. I mentioned befOre that tho majority of departments had kept well within their estimutes but I can assure you that ECONOMY IS STILL MORE NECESSARY in future owing to big schemes in hand, such as War- rington Bridge and Buttermarket street widening • (near, hear)-</p>
        <p>THANKS TO OFFICIALS. In ccnclusioo, gentlemen; may I ask for your charit- able judgment upon my first budget speech ?- In your kindness, put (1, wn to my newness to the office, rtlY shortcomings you luny have discovered. (Hear, hear.) I have to express my great obligations to the skill awl guidance •of your officials, Air. Fairhurst and Mr. Woodcock.; the g rent pains they have t4en, the wonder- ful accuracy of their work, their deep devotion to duty deserve the highest commendation. (Applause.) That</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON STANDS SO WELL . to-day compared with other towns, that it can show El diminished rate as compared with last year, that its expenditure i 5 relatively so low are matters for sincere congratulation to all, end are a high triliute to tbe knowledge and skill of your officials. (Applause). I thank you for the kindly attention you have paid te what I have said, and trust that- whatever the future, hes in store for us all, you may never have to lists n to a lesa favourable financial statement than it las Leen ' my happy privilege to make to-night. (Applause). Councillor FULTON SM iTir secondsd the proposition. Alderman BreeNserr congratulated Councillor Wilkin- son 'en the reduction he had been able to make in the rates, which would he good nsws for the public generally. There was just one thing, approaching the general question, and that was the undue oscillation in the rates—bow they swung about from one sum to the other. They depsndel - too much en the fluctuating bank balances. When expenditure was pretty normal the rates ought to be pretty normal. They ought to • have some continuity and steadfastness in the ratee- When the estimates fluctuated so little, there ought not to be such a fall or rise in the rates as often occurred. There were two essentials necessary for all to observe -- to fight anything which savoured of extravagance and not to run into the danger of undue cheese-paring- As Councillor Pemberton bad put it. they ought to fell fell value for their money. It was not so must the amount they spent but whether they got full value for . it. That ought to be the fundamental principle. BY , superficial economy they spoiled , great improvements , and sometimes they shrank from obvious duties. Ile thought if they exercised reasonable care they would be - able to carry oat some of thous reasonable improve- ments. Councillor PEMP.F.RTON rattle great mistake in Corpor- ation work was that there was not sufficient time or thought given to it. He thought some of the officials took too much upon themselves. It was their duty as members to interfere at times hut not unduly with the management of different departments. Ho was convinced the rates could he kept down with good business management. They in Warrington stood' in a utique position, as they bad the water- ways and the railways at their door. It was a town which ought to increase rapidly in prosperity if its affairs were properly managed. Councillor JAM F:S EVANS said he was not going to complain of Mr. Wilkinson's speech, which in one part referred to the estimates of the Hospital Committee- He thought they had done very well. Whilst other departments were credited with the earnings the liestrital Committee was not credited with any. lie only wanted the Council to get a correct impression of what bad been done. The general body of the Council did not understand the pasition. They had had an exceptionally heavy year in hospital work, yet whilst they had overspent their estimates that money was nnt actually lost. They bad earned .£5OO and not run the department at a loss. Councillor' FULTON-SMITH seconded and said the accounts required and deserve d a great deal of scrutiny. He had been at them for three or four days and he had not quite comprehended them yet. In regard to the carting question he thought they ought to adopt steant traction. The saving w ould not only be in time but also in the cart"ng of large quantities of material. He thought that with more prudent management the rates, would come down to is. and he did not see anything le hinder that idea if the chairmen of committees would take greet interest in the duties. They had been over- burdened with work by the Local Goverumeet Beard and the Town Planning scheme was a very serious matter. He was perfectly sure some of those enact- ments if allowed to be dealt cilth by local authorities. -- could be worked on much cheaper lines than by the Local Government Board. As an accountant himself, he congratulated the officials on the clearness with 1 which they had presented the accounts. The estimates were approved. .</p>
