        <head>Exariath.er, Saturday, April 10, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Exariath.er, Saturday, April 10, 1909.</p>
        <p>for payment and he put the date stamp upon it. He days togetheic—Prisoner was sentenced to three months' l acting as counter clerk on that day, and compared the hard labour. signature with that of Joseph Smith's of 1691. He also BEGGING.—AIbert Edward Allen, Friars' Green, was 1 initialled the form. Within the month preceding the sentenced to 14 days' hard labour for begging in 1 17th March, 1008, there had been a good n.any with- Palmyra-square and also for threatening Mr. Cann who. d rascal s. had remonstrated with him. Mr. Browns: Didn't it strike you at once at a glance MoNDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Deputy- that the whole of the receipts were in one handwritings, Mayor (Alderman Smethuret), Alderman Tinnion, —No. Messrs. S. Wright, W. Roberts, S. Furness and G. Mr. Browne : On neither occasion ?—No. Fred Smert, expert in handwriting, Manchester, Was Music LICENCE EXTENSION.—Mr. Satinet Hassell next called, and he said he bed examined the signature applied for a of Joseph Smith on the exhibits, and there was no n extersion of the music licence of the -*local skating rink until midnight on Easter Monday.-- attempt to disguise. They were certainly not in the hands riling of Joseph Smith, and in his opinion the Mr. Roberts :'ls there any drink sold ?—The Chief,Con- seme person who wrote " Annie Smith" on certain of , staple : No, sir.—The licence was granted. PORA ELY AUTHORITY.—Mr. David Ray was the exhibits wrote the name "Joseph Smith" on each ranted temporary authority to sell at. the Golden Ball, of the six exhibits., re The whole body of documents had f, nutsferd-road. been written by the same person. ;MILITARY ABSENTEE.—EdNard Nelson, private in the SPEECH FOR THE DEFENCE. King's Liverpool Regiment, stationed at Orford Mr. Browne said that both Mr. and Mrs. Smith Barracks, was charged with being an absentee from his thought that the matter had been settled long ago, until regiment since Ist Ap; pleaded guilty.—Detec- the warrant was issued. There was no shadow of a• rive ReddOigton stated that in consequence of a corn- doubt that some discussion did take place about the plaint received from Liverpool of an alleged theft by re entire settlement between the parties. Mr. Smith woman he made inquiries, and ascertained that prisoner said one thing, and Mr. Proud said ;mother. was in cammunic.ten with the woman. He proceeded As a matter of fact the whole matter did go to the Barracks aid found that the prieoner was air to ariaration. The Registrar-General found for absentee. Ile was found living at a chip potatee shep,the them and the bank had taken those proceedings. woman passing as his wife. Witness took them both Seeing how easy it was for the woman to get the firet lute custody, and they were locked up.—The Chief Con- £4—assuming she did get it—what were the cense- stable stated that there was an escort, in attsedenogjand quences ? It only the slightest enquiry had been made also that the officer was deserving of a reward for the on the 17th August, 1907, or the slightest care taken the arrest.—Reddington teas awardeflOs., and the pri'soner bank would have been richer by £179. The woman had was handed over to the escott. been encouraged by the negligence of the bank. Did LEFT HIS WIFE.—John Henry Beckett, Margamte- they.think that any jury in the face of the admissions street, was charged with running away from and In the face of seeing those documents would bring the Borough leaving his wife in charge of in a verdict of guilty? In the face of all those clecu- the IVarrington Union since 7th February. meats, in the face of the accounts of 1891 when Joseph Mr. Shearer stated that the wife and children, aged 11, Smith give his signature to the bank, 18 years agn.when 8. 6,3, were admitted into the Warrington Union on 7th constant t-arsact ions were taking place, did they think February. Prisoner returned to town that morning and that the jury would ever say that the bank did not was apprehended at 6 o'clock. His family had assist hi the most reateeial way possible in snaking the e„se the Union about £9. Prisoner said he could way casy for her tonbtain the money ? Did they not find work, but he would have started work that think the jury would not say that the woman had been morning.—The Guardians had so many oases that they helped and in a sense encouraged. That was a case of were determined to take proceedinge. The prisoner had spite brought by the bank—he was instructed to seyso.The not commtmiceted with them or his wife since going lank authorities knew the evidence on the 2nd De :ember. away.—He was sentenced to one month hard la, our. Meetings must leave been held in January,Februery,and ~00TBALL IN THE STREEF.—Arthur Clare, Webster's- March, so what was to prevent a special meeting being yard, was fined Is. for playing football in Dial-street on called?Theexcuse was that Mr.Long,the chalrman,coald the 28th February. not attend. Had Mr. Long everything to do with the A re ..,EFF,NDANT'S COMPLAINT.—Martin Mann ion, bank ?If the award had been in favour of the hank,there Bridge-street, was charged with making use of obscene would never have been a word said about those pro- language on the 13th March.—Mr. Carter Inspector of ceaings. It was spite and simple revenge. Because the Tramways. said the defendant went up they had been " licked " those proceedings were to him at the Rylands'-street terminus and taken. In conclusion he said that on the ft 4,3 of that made use of some most disensting language the case oughtsto be dismissed. saying that the Chief Constable and De' esti veßedd ng- The prisoner was committed to the Assizes. ton were no good. A number of ladies were ahout Prisoner was granted bail in the sum of £l9. at the time.—Mannion said that Detective Reidingtoa was continually after him and he was enough to make a man coapnit suicide (Latiehter). The Chief Con- stable stated that .the defendant was only before the court the previous Tuesday for plying a ca' for hire without a licrece.—He was orc'ered to pay 10s. and costs. A PITIFUL CASE -e-Ann Jane Holbro of no settled abode a young woman of respectable appearance, was ' charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge. street the previous niehr. Sergeant Barlow statad thtt he -saw the prisoner drunk and disorderly at a qnarter, to Ii the previous night. For the past fortnight she had been a perfect pest to the towns--The Chief Con- stable strteed. it would be very clOsirsble if the woman would go to a home. The clerk (to defendant) : Will you consent to going to a home ? —Prisoner -: -I will see Mr. Firth later on the .subject.—The matter .vres egain brought forward at the end of the court. Prisoner : My case has been heard once this morning.—The Chief. C instable stated that the defendant nppearedlo be baldly right in the bead.—Mr. Firth stated that he had interviewed the woman and could make nothing of her. —Prisoner was remanded to the Wtsrkhouae in order that her tnental condition might be inquired into. TUESDAY.—Before Mrs 11. Roberts (in the chair), Alderm Bolton, and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, TRAVELLING WITHOUT A TICKET.—James Ford, a labourer from Ditton, was charged w:th travelling froin Liverpool to Warrington by the London and North- Weste.n Railway, without paying the fare.—John James Wood, a porter, stated that he was on deity collecting tickets from the 5.27 train fiorn Liverpool low level when the prisoner came up to him. Witness asked him for his ticket and prisoner replied " I have not g,it one. I have lost it." Witness then asked him to pay his fare and prisoner replied "That be had no money." Witness then told him that he would have to pay his fare. The prisoner thereupon set off to run across the line to the other side of the station. Witness followed, and caught tie; with prisoner who then became very abusive and wanted to fight. With the assistance of Tnomas Leather, prisoner was taken to the station- master at Bank Quay Station.—Detective Woollacott spoke to arresting prisoner who was slightly under the influence of drink.—Prisoner was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days., WEDNESDA v.—Before Mr. JsteW. Hayward (chairniaa), Aldermen Smethurst and Ellison, and Councillor Dr. Joseph. CONSTABLE ASSAULTED.—Patrick Burke, Pinner's Brow, WAS charged with assaulting P.C. Macdonald whilst on duty the day previous.—The prisoner pleaded " not guilty." P.C. Macdonald said that et a quarter- past eleven be was on duty on Pinner's Brow when he saw the prisoner fighting with. his wife. Prisoners wife asked w;tness to assist her end before he could stop him he threw off his Coat and cup and rushed at him like a mad dog. Ho tore his coat down the front in several places and hit him on the bead. , Witness asked the assistance of two people and they would not help him. Somebody tore him away from the prisoner and Sergeant Barlow went to his house and arrested him.—Sergeaut Barlow said thst at a quarter te twelve the night previoua h 3 was on duty in Pinner's Brow and Saw the previous witness who appeared to hare been very badly treate:T. On the way down to the Police Station the prisoner said it was all through his wife.—Prisoner who had bern before the magistrates 65 times, was sentenced to three months hard labour.</p>
        <p>Tut:asnAv.—Before Messrs. John FArclough (in the chair), Charles White, C. J. Burgesl,. W. Roberts' A. J. Jolley and Dr. Naden. AGGRAVATED ASSAULT.—Mary Broderick, Philip- street, was charged on remand with an aggravated assault on Annie Bibby on Saturday last. The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner was before .the court on Monday last cud remanded for inquiries. It appeared there had been a rowin the neighbourhood between the two families which had continued for smile time. About eleven o'clock on Saturday the poisoner was sec-n in the street only partially dressed, and it was alleged she strta:k proeecutrix in the eye with a small table knife. :.z.he was taken to the infirmary, where the,wound was dressed.—Several witnesses were called to corroborate the complainant's story, and it was further alleged that a poker was used. The prisoner denied that she struck a blow uith the knife: The bother would not have been caused but for the row uhich the complainant and her family were making. The Chief Constable said there was a certain. amount of truth in the prisoner's story.—The magistrates dis- missed the case. •</p>
        <p>STREF.T BETTING.—WiIIiam Terry, Lovely-lane, was charged with loitering in Priestley-street for the purposes of betting.—Detective Clough stated that in consequence " of numerous complainta he in company with Detective Holland were detailed to keep special observation on the locality. Prisoner was seen at the rear of A itkin-street, near Priestly-street, to receive six slips of paper containing money from six different men, between the hours of 12.30 and 2 o'clock. After unwrapping the papers and reading them he was observed to put the money in his pocket. Subsequantly he handed over the papers and money to another man. On seeing the officers the prisoner ran, away but was caught by Detective Holland in Manley-street.-Detective Holland corroborated.—The prisoner said he did not receive any bets. He was only keeping "nix ". whilst another man made the book.—The magistrates imposed a fine of £lO and costs or two months. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—WiIIiam McDermott was fined ss. and costs for the above offence in Bank-street on Wednesday night. WIPE DESERTION.—Henry Jackson, Bright-street, Wrexham, was summoned by his wife Elizabeth Jack- son for desertion.—Mr. T. S. Steel who. appeared for the complainant, said it was an extraordinary case of desertion. The defendant was a hardware dealer carry- ing on business in Warrington. He seemed to have had an affection for a young married woman named Sankey who resided in Slater-street. The complainant had several times complained 'about his conduct and there had been quarrels between them. The defendant carried on business in the market. and the woman Sankey wps also a stallholder. The complainant's suspicions -being aroused the defendant was watched and it was dis- covered he was living with the woman in Wrexham.— The magistrates granted a warrant for his arrest. '</p>
        <p>AN HABITUAL DRUNKAII.D.—Ann Traynor, 317, Well- field-street, applied for a separation order holt her husband Peter Traynor, on the ground of him being an habitual drunkard. The complainant alleged that her husband was continuilly drunk at tho week-ends. After hearing the evidence the mag;strates diimitsed the application. .</p>
        <p>-THE STORY OF A MONEY ORDER.—William Richards of Northwich, was summoned by his wife, Mary Ellen Richards, 6, Spencers-yard, for wife desertion. The defendant said he had not neglected to support her as he had sent recently a money for £5. The complainant said the had not received it, whereupon defendant searched his pockets end h3nded to the Clerk what he called a receipt. The Clerk discovered that the so- called receipt was the genuine money order., Amidst laughter the defendant said he thought the post- office authorities forwarded the note. The Chairman: You ought to have sent it'by p:)st. The money order was thereupon handed over to the complainant. and the defendant promised to pay 10s. -weekly to,ards his wife's maintenance. He left the Com t smiling with Latisfaction that the lost money order had been found.</p>
