      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Evangelistic Mission.</head>
        <p>Evangelistic Mission.</p>
        <p>Another evangelistic service was Leld at Bold-street Wesleyan Clauch on Sunday evening, alter the ordinary service, when an addiess was given by Mr. M. H. Llewellyn, subject "It is finished." He asked his hearers what those words rceint in this century to each The Rev. T. M iller conduoted the service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Buckley-stredt P.S A.</head>
        <p>Buckley-stredt P.S A.</p>
        <p>A well-attended even- meeting of the Buckley-street P.S.A. was hold on &amp;inlay and proved interesting and helpful. Mr. James tYrrmten presided and gave an address on his experience in P.S.A. w.irk. Mr. Tipping, of Liverpool, delivered an address on "Hercules." Mr. Alfred Bates treated his hearers to a onuple of well rendered violin solos, whilst Mr. James Ryder was very successful with a tenor solo. Miss M. Piinstcn acted as accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Bewsey-yoad Wesley Guild</head>
        <p>Bewsey-yoad Wesley Guild</p>
        <p>After n successful season which extended o'er a period of ,about six months tin committee of the •Bewsey-road Wesley Guiid thoughtit desirable that, there should be a soCial on the last night of the session. This took place last Monday evening in the Lecture Hall adjoining the church and a good number assembled. Mr. William Lowe entertained the gt,thering with some humorous selections, and a god supply of gimes helped to make the evening a very pleasant one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Wycliffe Congregational .Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational .Church.</p>
        <p>At this Church on Sun-day-evening the choir sing the late Sir John Stainer's beautiful work "The Crucifixion." Thera was a good congregation. Aii (ha .chorases and solos .were sympathetically rendered and the service .wss deeply impressive. The soloists were Messrs. -Arthur Griffiths, Frank Brown and Bowman -Mr. W.. 11. Payton, Mn. Bae„ played the or.an with his usual taste and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Holy Trinity Church</head>
        <p>Holy Trinity Church</p>
        <p>"Holy Trinity Parish INefigazine," alluding to the new vestry and porch, slys : "This woik is now in progrcss, and we hope will be completed ins few weeks. The porch cannot. be commenced till the new vestry ready for use. The cost of the work was underestimited in the fir.t iustance,.so that the churchwardens must k the members of the congregation t 3 make special effort if debt is to be avoided. Certain Sandays will have to be set aside for special collections, when we trust the same support given to the Church in the past will•be again extended. Mr. Johnson, who is bearing all the expense of the porch, also finds it cannot be completed without the expenditure of a very consider- able sum. Bat with his accustomed generosity he cheerfully acquiesce. Our leaders are alroady nwire that Mr. Johnson is also bearing considarab'y the greater part of the cost for the decorating of St. Luke's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at the in;etieg of this Society on Sunday. Mr. 0. Smiley presided and spoke upon" Their works do follow them." He referred to Wesley and Luther the influence of whose work was even greater to-day than when they were alive. Mr. T. Prince gave a good stirring, Gospel addreqs upon "The Gospel of Salvation: What it is, and how to attain it, cind u hat the possession of it is." Religion, he said, was not a belief of the bead only, but a belief of the heart. Just as the child believed in its mother and the mother in the child, so it Was scitli our dealings and belief in Christ. His great Heart appealed to our hearts and thus we lived in Him. Mr. NV. H. Payton rEnlered an organ solo which was greatly appreciated. Mr. McEachern and Rev. John Yonge also took part in the meeting. Three new merubers received a most cordial welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Bold-street Weslesan Church. Foreign Missions.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Weslesan Church. Foreign Missions.</p>
        <p>The Foreign Mission7rry anniversary was commenced last Sunday at Bold-street Wesleyan Church. In the morning the choir rendered the anthem"o Saviour of the world,"and in theevening,"The word of the Lord endureth for ever." Rev. E. B. Keeling preached in the moreing and took his text from 2 Corinthiens, ii. pt. 14, "Now thanks he unto God which always leadeth us in triumph in prayer." He said the word used for triumph wea very singular one and was only used in two passages in the New Testament, It was a technical woid with a technical meaning. if they looked in a classical dictionary they would flail that the word triumph was a I rather elaborate business. 11 meant a solemn Procession in which a victorious General entered into the city in a chariot followed by his troops A recognised tribmph was the cherished ambit:on of every Roman General. It mult be a war against a foreign nation, in which at least 5,000 people were slauphtered,before it could be a triumph. When a triumph was secured nothing that the city of Rome could do for him was too much. Was not there something peculiarly unsuitable in speaking of the Cross as a triumph and using the technical word in connection with it? There was the sharpest contrast between the magnificence, display end splendour of a _Roman triumph and the gloom and darkness of the shadow of the Cross. St.Paul,however, fearlessly claimed that the Cross of Jesus Christ was a triumph and in the other text he spoke of himself as being one of the captives in the triumph of the days in which Christ was- living. Christ r.ever spoke of the Cross es a shameful thing. He. never spoke of His humiliation. We did. The rev, gentle- man was not quite sure that they mere right in speak- ing of the humiliation of Jesus Chript ; nor in speaking of condescension of God. 'Was such a thing possible that God could stoop to any low thing? All God's ways were upward, and nothing was downward. Through the Cross we bad mankind at its best. St. Paul did not hesitate to show, that the triumph was man's triumph. In conclusion the preacher said that in the days of the Ronutus they counted by the number slaughtered,now we counted by the number aaved.Christ was willing to help us on to our triumph. _ The Rev. Ttromas MILLER preached in the evening and took as his text "The field is the world" (St. Matthew xiii. part of the 38th verse),and" Go ye there- fore and teach all taltions " (St. Matthew xxviii. part of the 19th verse). He said that those words made an appeal to the imagination with the light of history upon them, but they still seemed incredible when they took into account the conditions under which they were spoken. It, s .emed inconceivable that anyone should give expression to sneh an aim and ideal. Those words were spoken to a few simple men possessed of few social advantages, with one or two exceptions uneducated, without the prestige which came from semi influence, face to face with entrenched wrong. Those men were urged to go forth and.. teach all nations the Gospel of Christ. Wherever there were human hearts, ihere were hearts which mus,t be won for Jesus Christ. Alluding to the conditions to-day, the preacher said Ensland was not christirin because its Government was nominally Chris- tian. We saw the victy which lucked behind the scenes and that which made human life a fomenting vat of all that was vicious, and yet we believed that in business, home and pleasure, Christ should be laster. As to the outlook in the world, there were 5110 millions who received Buddhism as the only message in their infancy. Buddhism had a wonderful fascination. It enjoined a beautiful moral life. It appealed to culture. its moral examples were as beautiful as the Sermon on the Mount. In India the greater part of the race found consolation in Buddhism. Still in Buddhism there was an inability to comfort. The Kingdom of Christ would be founded</p>
        <p>upon love. Out of 110 languages spoken in India the Bible was only translated into 42. The work of missionaries had only been done by sacrifice and suffering. In India, they bad millions who were illterate ; and they had men who had the mastery of the finest philosophy, and they bad .men who had all modern accomplishments. They had also men IA ho e ere keen students and who came to our Universities and carried away the prizes, and yet they were separated from Jesus Christ. If they were trust- lug only only to human strength and ingenuity they would fail, but they would succeed bee ,use they were relying upon the power cf God. Wherever men were nourished on the Bible they could stand persecution. The glory of the Christian Church was in its miss:onary history, for only a century bad elapsed since the work was began, bnd hunireds of thousands had been saved. Some of the grandest men and women, of shorn the world was not worthy, had been enrolled in that work In conclusion, the rev, gentleman asked why Christianlty would win. They believed that just as in the seed and in the ground,so in Christianity there were mN ster ious forces at work and through that the desired end would be accomplished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WARRINGTON RATES.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RATES.</p>
        <p>/I REDUCTION OF 4d. -- Councillor Wilkinson's First Budget Speech.</p>
        <p>Tuesday was Budget night at the Warrington Town Council, when the estimates of expenditure by tile various Connuittees were submitted and approved,and the rates fixed for . the ensuing year. Councillor Wilkinson, the new Chairman of the Finance Com- mittee, made his first budget speech. and had the gratification of ,being able to announce a substantial reduction of.4d. in the in the Call upon the rate- payers. _ _</p>
        <p>- The rates for the year just concluded and those for the ensuing Year are as follows :- 1908-9 1909-10 S. d. S. d. Improvement Rate Poor Rate - ‘4, 4 8 1 4 Sewers Rate _</p>
        <p>78 7 4 Councillor WlLitissoN submitted the following re=olutions:—</p>
        <p>(1) That the Connell hereby estimate the amount of £33,347 will, in addition to the borough fund, be required and suilicient for the purposes to which the borough fund is applicable, under the Municipal Cor- porations Act, 1882 or otherwise by law, for the year to end 31st March, 1910.</p>
        <p>(2) That the'Council do hereby order a borough rate to Le made in pursuance of the Municipal Corporations Act, 18S2, in order to raise the said amount.</p>
        <p>(3) That the overseers of the township of Warring- ton Ic and they am hereby ordered to pry to`the Treasurer of the borough, by instalments, and on or berore such dates as may be fixed by the Finance and Estate Committee, the contribution of the sum of £38,667 out of the poor rate made or to Le made for the said tounship of Warringtor, as provided by the Municipal Corporations Act, 1882. (4) That the estimate for an improvement rate as recommended by the Finance and Estate Committee be confirmed, and that the Finance and Estate Committee do make an improvement rate accordingly at two shillings and tenuence in the pound, to be collected by two half-yearly instAlments, and be-authorized to affix the common seal of the Corporation thereto. (5) That the estimate of the general sewers rate as recom- mended by the Finance and Estate Committee be con- Or !led, and that the Finance and Estate Committee do make a general sewers rate accordingly at twopence in the pound, to be collected by two half-yearly instal- ments, and be authorized to affix the common seal of the C,rporation thereto. COUNCILLOR WILKINSON AND BOROUGH FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON said :—I can conceive the position of a chairman of the Finance Committee being an unenviable one. The tendency taf local burdens upon the ratepayers is to increase, and the unhappy man who has to make an announcement of an additional burden is no doubt, for a time at any rate, one of the most unpopular members of the Council. Upon this my first appearance before you as the introducer of the local Budget, I am glad to say, however, that I shall ercape the unmerited odium which ro often attaches itself to the Chairman of your Finance Committee wit( n the rate of the past year fails to meet the expenditure of the coming year. (Hear, hear.) The statement which I shall have the pleasure of submitting to you is gratifying to myself, and I hope will prove acceptable alike to you and the ratepayers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>IMPROVEMENT RATE.</head>
        <p>IMPROVEMENT RATE.</p>
        <p>We will take the Improvemeet -Rate first. The departments which have exceeded their estimates are as follows: Hospital deportment, £504, caused, I am sorry to say, by the excessive number of patients; scavenging department, £53, removal of snow; markets department, £5B, decree:, ed receipts; weights and tr ftlsures department, £l2, sed receipts. Note ithstanding the above lcsses ott the rates, there is a small balance in hand which has been kept in reserve to meet outstanding accounts. The depart- ments which have effected savings upon their estimates and brought e.out this result are the highway, Finance and Nightsoil. INCREASED. ASSESSABLE VALUE.</p>
        <p>The amount required for the ensuing year is £32,980, as against £1,978 for 1C084909, or, .Cl,OOO more, but owing to thP increase in the asseEsatde value in the Borough it will not be necessary to increase the rate which will stand at 2a. 10d. in the R. This must be considered very satisfacto-y when the growth of the town is taken into account, and especially Er) eceing ti at last year's estimates included £2,000 from elec- tricity profits and £l,OOO from sale of surplus lands, while this year ti-ese amounts are excluded from the estimates. (Hear. hear).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>12,000 FROM THE GAS COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>12,000 FROM THE GAS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The estimated amount to be received from the Gas Department is £12,000, as against £11,530 in 1908-9, when the department made a record profit of £15,599, out of which a sum of £4,095 was held in reserve to meet repairs of a special nature. This year the price of gas has been reduced by 2d. per 1,000 cubic feet which represents the sum of about £3,000, and consequently the profit is not expected to be more than &lt;£12,000. It is to be hoped that the figure will he materially increased in future years, as the profits from the gas works constitute an important factor affecting the rates, and low rites are very effective in inducing manufacturers and others to come into the town, as in sr•veral recent oases the only question asked, when inquiries have been made as to sites for works in War- riugton is" What are theates?" (Hear, hear.) GENERAL nWERS RATE.</p>
        <p>The amonnts requirei under this rate is .0,023 as against £2,419 in 1908-9, but it has not been thought advisable to increase the rate of 2d. although at the end of the finaucal year there will be a deficit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EOFtOUG If 12 ATE.</head>
        <p>EOFtOUG If 12 ATE.</p>
        <p>The year was Commenced with a considerable over- drAft, but the majority of the departments have kept well within their estimates. In all cases where depart- ments have exceeded their estimates I am glad to say the amounts over-expended are too trivial to particu- larise. It was thought advisable to withhold £l,OOO from the amount allocated for educational purposes last year. With the help of the foregoing departments, particularly the Watch, a sum of £l,OOO is carried forward to the assistance of the ensuing year's rate, and at the same time a sufficient balance has been kept in reserve to meet outstanding liabilities incurred in the year 1908-9. Turning to the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>ESTIMATE'S FOR THE ENSUING YEAR</head>
        <p>ESTIMATE'S FOR THE ENSUING YEAR</p>
        <p>it will be seen that, although we shall not have the advantage of last year of receiving .C 3,000 from the Ship Canal Company for costs, the amount required is slightly less than 1908-9, principally owing to the fact that last year we had a debit balance of nearly £1,300 to commence the year with, and this year we are fortunate enough to begin the bear with a credit balance of £l,OOO. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>POOR RATE REDUCED</head>
        <p>POOR RATE REDUCED</p>
        <p>The sum of £i8,667 will he required from the Over- seers, which, with the fact that the requirements of the Guardians and the Overseers will be less than last yesr, will enable a poor rate of 49. 4d. to be levied, as against 4e. Bd. last year, a reduction of 44. in the R. (Applause). The decrease in the rates has been obtained without revising or cutting down the estimates in any way after the approval by the committee, although in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" Held in Bondage ''</p>
        <p>Warrington playgoers should have a fairly vivid impression of life in Russia and Siberia for it has formed the groundwork of thrilling dramas submitted at the Royal Court Theatre for two successive weeks. The author of .` Held in Bondage " has made clever use of a theme which affords plenty of scope for highly dramatic situations and emotional acting and the result is a play which commands unflaggiuX interest. The story is of a young sculptor who becomes involved in Nihilism and pays the penalty by - years of suffering in Siberia. At times the excite- ment reaches fever heat. especially in the -duel scene which is • most realistic. The company is n good one, Miss Constance Ifedwyn • infus- ing much power and feeling into her portrayal of " Zara." Mr. George Gaisford displays undoubted talent as the much-wronged Ivan Dronski, and Mr. Richard Vane takes the unpopular part Of Prince Mazaroff in a whole-hearted fashion which works upon the indignation of the gallery. Miss Aimee Grattan Clyndes as Leonie Devigne is responsible for a really excellent piece of characterismion which meets with a full measure of approval, and Mr. W. Dewhurst is a- dashing Paul. Although the tragic element predominates there is plenty of good fun in the play, but the music hall turns in the street of St. Petersburg seemed some- what incongruous. The humour is chiefly supplied by Mr.. Matt Russell, Mr. Fred Wilberforce, Miss Helen Camel on and' Miss F. Ferina, who enter into their parts with considerable spirit, and prove favourite4 with the audience Miss Patty Delahay, is a winsome • PPrinceSs Sonia. The mounting of the piece is most effective. .</p>
        <p>The theatre orchestra has been improved and Mr. Norman Latham has taken over the conductorship. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. A popular attraction is announced for next week. when Messrs. Sidney Rennef and G. Al. Copping will present the great domestic drama,' Love Rules the World." There will no doubt be large audiences :0 the play, which tells the story of a mother's love an a son's devotion, was well received on the occasion of its last visit. An excellent company will appear and the stage effects will be on a ,pleasing scale.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' (Aniseed, Senega, Squil &amp;a.), for Coughs. Of Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
