      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAPTERS.</p>
        <p>WARRI'T'N FAIR.</p>
        <p>The Oldest Dialect Ballad Extant.</p>
        <p>Few of our townspeople, we imagine, are aware that the oldest dialect poem we possess belongs to Warrington. This is the quaint old ballad " WarriTn Fair." Those who have studied Harland's " Ballads of Lancashire" may remember that a version of it appears under the title " Warrikin Fair," but the reridering there given does not seem to us to be the best or even the true one. Though the old song has no historical interest we have judged it worthy of some effort to discover its original form, and to place it here in the hope of preserving a very curious bit of local verse.</p>
        <p>A generation reveals itself in its recreations, and in the crude humour of the poem we may find a clue to the tastes of our forbears three and a half centuries ago. • The date of the ballad is fixed by its reference to "Rondle Shay." This gentle- man Randle Shay or Shaw was the bailiff to Sir Thomas Butler in the second year of the reign of Edward VI. at a date which may be approximately given as 1548.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>NARRATIVE OF THE BALLAD.</head>
        <p>THE NARRATIVE OF THE BALLAD.</p>
        <p>If our readers will forgive the unusual proceeding we will tell them the story before they read the ballad. Somewhere outside Warrington there lived a couple, Gilbert Scott and his wife Grace. That Grace waS pre-eminently the "better half" is proved beyond doubt by the sequel of the story. One Warrington Fair Gilbert went .to town to sell his mare Berry. A sharper took him in hand• and arranged to buy the mare for " neenteen good groats"' or in modern terms 6s. 4d., a trifling sum as we should measure things, but a respectable and probably the full market price in those days. That it was a fair offer for the mare is proved by the fact that it was finally accepted by the shrewd Grace herself.. The fine appearance of the purchaser, his courtesy, his loving shake of the hand touch the tender heart of Gilbert Scott, and he finds himself unable to press for immediate pay- ment. The stranger completes his conquest by inviting Gilbert to a feast of " snig poy," and by the gift of twopence to spend no doubt upon Warrington ale.</p>
        <p>-That such a fine gentleman—he wore a grey coat and green breeches—could be any -less good than his clothes no country farmer could ever be expected to believe, and Gilbert allowed him to take away the mare without even asking his name. True he gave copious promises of payment on some future market day, and would meet him for the purpose at " Rondle Shays." Leaving the fair Gilbert appears to have met " Rondle Shay" himself, and communi- cates the fact that he has sold his mare. The bailiff's suspicions are awakened and he asks whether he has received the money, to which Gilbert can only answer that a promise is quite as good as cash from such a magnifi- cent gentleman. Arriving home he finds his muscular wife engaged in culinary operations, and tells her of his bargain. He had let his mare go for a plate of eel pie and twopence. Flying into a temper she chastises him with her ladle, and lays him in a battered heap. She pro- tests that such a story makes her " moore madder" than the innkeeper's good ale. In reply to enquiries Gilbert has to confess that he had been so impressed by the address and cajolery of the stranger that he was ashamed to ask his name lest by so doing he should have insulted him by his suspicions. Grace was not a woman to be easily beaten. If she did not know the sharper she could at least identify the mare and for five successive Wednesdays she located herself at the hostelry of Randle Shaw. Her patience was at last rewarded. A stranger appeared leading a mare and sure enough it was Berry. So wild was her excitement that she nearly leaped through the casement to grapple with the rogue. He dismounted to refresh just as Grace reached the door. Suppressing her feelings she asks for the money for the mare. The sharper, with a masked oath very common at the period, replied that he did not know her. Retorting with the same expletive she gave him to understand that she was " Owd Gilbert-o- wife Scott" or in modern language Mrs. Gilbert Scott. He evidently feels that he has an uncommon type of woman to deal with for he makes no attempt to dispute her identity and replies that he has not the money. Grace instantly says that she will have the mare and makes good her words in a scene which gives the,poet rare opportunity to exercise his descriptive powers. She doffs her cap,thereby releasing the " snood " which bound her hair. Seizing the adversary by the hair she pitches him against the water- ing trough. The noise attracts Randle Shaw who comes to separate them. Grace disposes of him by a remark which is more effec- tive than polite and declares that she will have satisfaction either in money or revenge. The innkeeper suggests that she takes either the mare or the money and the matter is settled by her receiving the latter. • Grace kept the money for herself and most readers will agree that she deserved it. But it is time to get to the ballad itself. WARRI'T'N FAIR.</p>
        <p>Ladies and gentlemen, if yo win bo' tarry, Awl' tell yo 'heaw Gilbert o' Scott sowd his mare Berry; Ile sowd her for neenteen good groats, one Warri't'n</p>
        <p>Fair, But he didno' whether he must be paid ewer or newer</p>
        <p>Os he're gooin' toart whom, he met his neme Ron'el; " Neme Ron'el " quo he " aw've sowd my mare Berry " Whey, then yo'an money beloike?" "Nay aw newer o penny-- But money's os sure os it're oather yoar bond or mine Or else awd newer trust th' cnvkert mon ogaine."</p>
        <p>So when he went whom he towd his .vroife Grace, Hoo up wi' th' ladle an swat him i'th face: Theaw tells me sich an unmanner7 tale, It mais me moore madder nur Tummas's good ale.</p>
        <p>"Hoo piched him o'th hillock, an' he faw'd wt a whack, That he theawt would welly ha' brocken his back, " 0 ! woife, quo' he, if thou'll le'nnue but rise, I'll gie thee aw' th' leet, wench, imme 't lies," Tho udgit' quo' hoo 'but wheer does he dwell?" "By lakin, quo' he 't I conno' tell."</p>
        <p>"Pray what were his' parel, or what were his noom?" "Un faith woman ! aw never troubrd mon as mich</p>
        <p>as t' ax him his noom ; But he'd‘o good thrum hat, an aw quickly spied that, An reawnd be middle he wore a girdle, 'At seemed be o' th' better sort o leather.</p>
        <p>"His cooat were grey, an his breeches wer'n green He would ha' done good for t' ony mon ha seen; An' he gnu me a great huncheon o' denty snig poy, An shak'd me by th' hond most lovingly.</p>
        <p>"He act'd just bike an honest mon's son, An spent two pence on me, when he had done; An promis'd he'd meet me at my neme Rondle Shay's" Next Wen's'day Grace went t' teawn, an for five market days, But ne'er eouM spy Berry conuniul to Roudle Shays.</p>
        <p>One day: as Grace were sit restin' her in a reawm, Hon spied th' mon wi' Berry commin deawn to th'</p>
        <p>teawn, Then her heart gen o beawnce an Grace were so gloppent, As though th' winder casement hoo'd bike to loppent.</p>
        <p>Tit' mon were no sanner at th' hitch But .Grace're ready t hayve up th' catch,— Cu faith, mon ! my husban's sowd his mare ye An' desires 'at yoan send him th' mony for Berry."</p>
        <p>" Marry ! " quoth he," but aw know yo not," " Marry ! " quoth hop," but awn' owd Gilbert-o'-wife Scott.</p>
        <p>" Marry ! " quoth he, spare," " Marry ! " quoth hoo, " Why, then, aw'll ha ' ,th mare.</p>
        <p>"but tit' money aw eonna</p>
        <p>Grace lop an' hoo stroode, as if hoo'd lin woode</p>
        <p>1100 off.wi 'her cap, ' dea'wn fell her snood ; Hoo geet on him by th ' weaufT, an thrust him again, th' treawf ; Wi squealin ' and squalin 'they made skit a din, That to rid urn my neme Rondle Shay he cooria</p>
        <p>“ Fye Nannty Grace fye !” " Fye, aye, o the de'il ! Done yo' think 'at its oather fit, farrantly, or wee! 'At mon should ha' boath money an th mare ? Aw'll mak' him an example, aw'llheaw ' him a groat; An' if he doesno' pay me awl' poo' eawt his throat."</p>
        <p>"Come, fye, Nannty Grace, come, lye, an ha done ! Yo'ast be th ' mare or Money, whether yo' won," So Grace has gotten th' money, and whomarts boo's</p>
        <p>gone: Hoo's kept ow hersel an gen Gilbert Scott noan. The language of the old ballad will present few difficulties to those who are schooled in the Lancashire dialect. A curious feature is the recurrence of Neme and Naunty as terms of address. These are the old words for Uncle and Aunt. Randle Shaw is always Neme (Uncle) and Grace is Naunty (Aunt). It seems quite plain that the terms were used as familiar appellations or courtesy titles, and that they did not describe any real relationship. The word We'n'sday 7 reveals the interesting fact that Warrington market day remains the same after three and a half centuries, and we gather that a horse fair flourished in some form or other in the reign of Edward VI. But it is difficult to know where the market would centre and we shall probably enquire in vain if we seek to locate the hostelry of Randal Shaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>[IiiISCNYI</head>
        <p>[IiiISCNYI</p>
        <p>The Life Stories which have appeared upon this page have already revealed the wealth of interest that resides in the memories of our older inhabitants and we trust that many more rich veins of remembrance will be laid bare.</p>
        <p>* * * -*</p>
        <p>The citizens of Rod/dale celebrated last Saturday the bi-centenary of the birth of John Collier, better known as "Tim Bobbin," the earliest of our dialect writers. Tim Bobbin is buried at Rochdale and a quaintly- epitaphed stone marks his resting place. He was not however a native of the town. His father was the curate and schoolmaster of Urmston, and he began his poverty-touched career in December,l7oB. Dislikin the busi- ness of weaving to which he was apprenticed, he became a schoolmaster and lived "passing rich" on twenty pounds a year at Milnrow. He died there in his seventy-eighth year.</p>
        <p>* * *_ *</p>
        <p>Tim Bobbin tried his hand at many things. Literature he may have loved, but he doubt- less told the truth in his couplet. Some write to please, 801110 do't fir spite, But want of money makeki me write.</p>
        <p>Not the thought of fame but sheer poverty stirred his genius, and he tried his hand at poetry and carica- ture. It is, however, only as a writer of dialect that he is remembered. His "Turn mas and Mary" published in 1746 is the first Lancashire sketch preserved to us and was the first of many such efforts on the: part of Tim Bobbin.</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>At the celebration on Saturday some interesting references were made to the Lancashire dialect and its preservation.Dr.:W. E. A. Axon, an authority upon that subject, disagreed with the suggestion that education would obliterate dialect and pointed out that Germany, the most highly educated country in Europe,was the country in which dialect literature flourished most. Another speaker in eulogy of the dialect called atten- tion to the Lancashire adjective " gradely," and stated that it appeared in a poem "Joseph of Ariomatheea " written about the end of the thirteeth century, being applied there to the Virgin Mary.</p>
        <p>* * *-*</p>
        <p>An old print has come into our handsl which was sent forth a century and a hvif ago to extol .the virtues of Cheshire cheese. We are not sure about its historical refer- ences, but we venture to give one of them. The antiquity of the famous,Cheshire pro- duct is traced back as far as the twelfth century. It is said that the Countess of Chester as far back as A.D. 1100 was in the habit of making superb cheese, so famous as to be sought for by printes. She is said to have sent to the Archbishop as a' choic,e gift three samples of her rich cheese.</p>
        <p>* * *</p>
        <p>More interesting than the history is a poem, dated 1746, and set to rollicking. music. The poem gains meaning from the fact ,that it was published during the Spanish war made by George 11. The cheese in those days was made up in circular form, arid it would seem to the poor Spaniard a tiuge pumpkin-like fruit.</p>
        <p>A Cheshire man sailed into Spain To trade for merchandise, When he arriVed from the main A Spaniard him espies,</p>
        <p>Who said" You English rogue, look here,. What fruits and spices fine Our land produces twice a year; Thou hast not such in thine." The Cheshire man ran to his hold And fetched a Cheshire cheese, And said "Look here, you dog! behold We have such fruit as these."</p>
        <p>"Your fruits are ripe but twice a year, As you yourself do say; But such as I present you here Our land brings twice a day."</p>
        <p>The Spaniard in a pacsion flew, - And his rapier took in hand; The Cheshire man kicked up his heels, Saying, " Thou'rt at my command."</p>
        <p>So never let a Spaniard boast While Cheshire men abound, Lest they should teach hint to his cost To dance a Cheshire round.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>"THE STITCH IN TIME."</head>
        <p>"THE STITCH IN TIME."</p>
        <p>At the Aigburth Parish Hall, on Saturday nil 'ht this "play with a purpose," by Miss T. H. Crostleld ; urd Mr. B. S. Ton roe, was performed by local amateurs before a large audience. The "Liverpool Daily Po:st and Mercury" thus refers to the performance :—The play is an earnest attempt to present the aims tor the National Service League in dialogue. It is more cam- mentative and narratory than dramatic; and perhaps the audience—though on the whole, sympatb etically dis- posed and at times enthusiastic—laughed occasionally when they were supposed to be seriously ivrpressed, as, for instance, in the case of the wounded Yew lan and that of the explosion, for which the stage eqs ipment was necessarily inadequate. The acting, on the whole, was good, though histrionic art was subserviei st somewhat ; to obvious exigencies of propaganda, "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>AT THE TOP OF SNOWDON.</head>
        <p>AT THE TOP OF SNOWDON.</p>
        <p>The first sicent of Snowdon this year; was made on Saturday by Mr. J. R. Owen, stitionmaster, Llanberis. A large number of workmen are at present employed in clearing away the snow from the cuttings through which the Snowdon mountain railway pusses, and it is expected that a se ry ice of trains to and from the summit %lint have been re-established by Easter Monday. There ,still remains a great cl,pth of mow—as much as twelve or fourteen feet in some places. Qn reaching the summit Mr. Owen was surprised to find two sheep— which }rive often been noticed by summer visitors, as they walk in and out of the summit buildings, and take food from the hands of strangt•rs-13ing under the shelter of one of.the buildingi. How they managed to exist through the severe weather is remarkable, for tilers was not a blade of grass to be seen for miles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>T3OY KILLED'IN A FOOTBALL CROWD.</head>
        <p>T3OY KILLED'IN A FOOTBALL CROWD.</p>
        <p>A boy named Alfred Deans died in the Sunderland Infirmary tai Sunday from injuries received in the rush bp the crowd on the occasion of the. Cup-tie hetwcen Sunderland and Newcastle United. Eight persons, including a policeman, were injurel, 501120 sustaining broken ribs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MY LIFE STORY.</head>
        <p>MY LIFE STORY.</p>
        <p>..• • •• , , ' ~d,4,tjAs • •-.A • • Zilr4 s' •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MR. S. KENYON.</head>
        <p>MR. S. KENYON.</p>
        <p>I was born at Mount Pleasant about ten miles from Preston and half-a-mile from Brock Bottoms which lies in the beautiful</p>
        <p>valley of the river Brock.</p>
        <p>My father _and</p>
        <p>mother lived in the house where I was .born</p>
        <p>more than fifty years. A married couple, friends of my parents, had tea and supper with them on Christmas Day for nearly fifty years, almost, I believe, if not entirely with- out a break. They all lived_ to be over</p>
        <p>eighty years oT age and died within a short time of each other. I may mention that my father's father died at the age of ninety-four. The house in which we lived was two miles</p>
        <p>from White Chapel School, the only school at which I was educated. At the age of fifteen I was apprenticed to a chemist and druggist in Preston, a few yards from where the Town Hall and Library now stand. Soon after I was apprenticed the porter and.myself upset a cask contain- ing a white powder. The porter, who knew more about the stock than I did, ran out of the warehouse and I was beginning to get it up when he called out, "Come away or you'll be poisoned, it's arsenic." After the dust had subsided we did the best we could with it, but soon afterwards I was very ill, and my skin became very yellow. I went to a doctor who told me I had been poisoned, but under his treatment I gradually recdvered. A while after this-a circumstance occurred so remarkable that I scarcely like to mention it. The errand boy and myself had to grind pepper in a room in which gunpowder was kept in wooden kegs. This of course is not permitted now. Darkness coming on before we finished, we lighted a candle and placed it on an opened keg of gunpowder, in ignor- ance of what it contained. We were called down to the shop and left the lighted candle there and it was not until the candle had been removed that we saw how narrowly we had escaped an awful disaster. Sometime afterwards, at my father's request, my indentures were cancelled and I com- pleted my apprenticeship in Lancaster. Until I went to Preston I had scarcely ever been a night away from home and the change was very great. I soon learnt that the ideal and the r 1 were very different. I was very often told by those very -much older than myself that I could not get on in business, or make a living unless I disregarded my love of the beautiful and• renounced my poetic ideas, and nothing ever vexed me more. I knew it was an error and hated it as a cursed lie.</p>
        <p>When in Lancaster I became acquainted with the Rev. Henry Solly, who afterwards became so interested in Social Science in which, I think, Lord Shaftesbury took such a prominent part. I and two friends waited on Mr. Solly in reference to the for- mation of an improvement and debating class. Mr. Solly was always ready for any good that he could do and with his help we formed an Improvement and Debating Society in connection with the Mechanics' Institute. It became largely attended and I think through the influence of Mr. Solly led to the formation of some of the first working men's clubs in the country. I left Lancaster at the age of twenty and took a situation in a Midland town. It was the hardest post I ever had. We, the other assistant and I, opened the shop at seven o'clock in the morning and did not close until ten af night and twelve o'clock on Satur- days, and we had to stay in all days on alternate Sundays. We lived on the premises, and were never away from them except on our Sunday out. I asked the proprietor if we ever got a night out and he said "No; do you see that clock My young men go to bed to get wound up." Then I asked if we could do as we liked 6n our Sunday out. "Yes," said he, "you can go to hell and back for anything I care." Our greatest relief was to get into the ware- house together, from which place we were occasionally, whilst blessing the governor, energetically called. On one occasion I was going to make black draught when the governor called out "Bring that bottle to me.". I did so. He</p>
        <p>told me to fill it up so far with Epsom salts and water which I did. He then shook it saying "If they take an ounce of this won't it be a draught ?" " Yes " I replied "a draught of Epsom salts and water." He then coloured it with ink and said, "that's good enough for the black country." So I thought it was time for me to get out of it. I was not there long but did not get away until, with standing so long, the veins of my leg became varicose, and since I. was twenty- one years of age I have borne the indelible marks of that slavery. My very friendly companion in the shop died soon after I left. On leaving the black country I went to Bowers Brothers,Chester,and found it a happy change, for they were most estimable men. The hours were reasonable and life was enjoyable. I was afterwards manager of a business in Whitehaven, and found that Cumberland people wefe humorous, sociable and friendly. Whitehaven has many pleasant associations, and after leaving it I often heard in dreams the wavelets breaking on its rocky shore. The last post I held was that of dispenser at Bolton Infirmary. Perhaps the following circumstance is worth mentioning. The Committee wished to know the exact time a certain patient was admitted. On asking the porter, he said quite positively that it was between five minutes to nine and nine o'clock in the morning. When asked how he knew the time, he said that when he opened the door he saw Mr. Kenyon coming up Nelson-square and that he never came before five minutes to nine and never after nine o'clock. I am inclined to think they believed him for after leaving Bolton Infirmary, the Chairman, the Rev. Canon Powell in a testimonial he gave me said: "Mr. Kenyon was considered an excellent dispenser and performed his duties with amazing punctuality and diligence." Whilst at Bolton I became acquainted and friendly with Mr, James Dodds to whom the</p>
        <p>pleasing memories of far years extend, and it was a pleasure for me to work with him in Warrington for what we believed to be the welfare of the town. It is very nearly forty years since I was induced to leave Bolton and take a chemist's and druggist's business in Warrington. I soon found that the busi- ness was not what I expected it to be. Looking round I began to think I might do something that would induce manufacturers to. do business with me and I invented dipping wire varnish. It was a long time before I could get people to use it. One gentleman said he knew I thought it would do, but they or he, I forget which, thought I had varnish on the brain. That gentleman is not living now but he was so nice a man that I almost thanked him for telling me. I have to thank Mr. Edwin Woods, with whose firm I did such a long and satisfactory business, for being one of the first to bring it into use. He told me he had decided to ascertain with certainty whether it would or would not do. The result was quite satisfactory and others soon adopted it. Of course I have to thank all the wire manufacturers for their confidence and for the upright, honour- able, -and manly way they treated me. I never tried to sell a customer any article unless I thought it would answer his purpose. On one occasion a gentleman who is now dead gave me an order for three barrels of an article which I told hint would not answer his purpose, but he told me to let hint have it at once. I went away telling him I would do so, but turned back again to tell him what I felt sure the result would be. Still he persisted and I let\ him have the three barrels. Early the following morning he came to tell me it had turned out just as I expected and told me to let him have what I thought would be best. I sent him wire varnish and I think he used it for that pur- pose ever after. Sometime after the varnish had been generally used, a large firm in Germany had two sample barrels. After they had tried it I received a letter from them stating that it was quite satisfactory and inquiring price for a large quantity, but in another day or two I had another letter informing me that the duty on my varnish was so very great that it would end ,our business. Afterwards the passing of tile McKinley Tariff Bill in America was a great injury to my varnish business. A little before this occurred I had the misfortune to be bitten On the thumb by a dog, on which a veterinary sergeon made a post mortem examination and gave to the town authori- ties a certificate of rabies. So that although I was in some ways fortunate I was in others' very unfortunate. The incubation period for hydrophobia is sometimes two years, 'and it was a long and anxious time to live through. One day whilst at tea the thumb began to twitch just where the dog had bitten me, which is one of the premoni- tory symptons of hydrophobia, and I began to thira my mortal hour had come. Not sinking in old age by slow decay But fiercely snatched in manhood s prime away.</p>
        <p>After enduring this for about fifteen minutes it occurred to me that I got the thumb hurt on the day previous. So I sat in an easy chair, smoked a cigar and fell asleep. During the incubation period of hydrophobia I invented other articles some of which I patented. I am thankful that during my business career no one was injured in my employ. I told them to be careful of themselves and I was glad that with us long hours were not a necessity. These are some of the most eventful occur- rences of my life, and I am inclined to think that some of the most important events of life are frequently brought about by circumstances over which we have little control. The guilt of actions only God can know Who gives to each its best corrective woe. In my humble way I have written and spoken against many public evils without the least personal animosity, because I believe that the welfare of the public is above all other things for ever sacred, and that disinterestedness in public Life is a very sacred duty, for it is by far the most important factor in promoting the common weal, in making the world happier and "working out the purposes of God. Nearly ten years ago I wrote some lines on my native hills, the last verse of which by your permission I, will quote :</p>
        <p>In eally years by fate compelled to stray And wander from loy childhood's home away, Through life forever must the wish remain To live beside my native 1 ills again. I am nearly 70 years of age and it is time to leave generally to younger men the strife that is caused by the evils of the world and for me to live in peace beside my native hills again.</p>
        <p>S. KENYON,</p>
        <p>Ribbleton. April sth, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CURIOUS THEORY</head>
        <p>CURIOUS THEORY</p>
        <p>About</p>
        <p>EARTHQUAKES.</p>
        <p>"The cause of earthquakes is sin," said a famous preacher. How he reasoned it out is not easy for the average sinner to see. The suggestion that the destruction of Messini and Reggio resulted from the violation of moral law, would be considered rank nonsense by modern scientists.</p>
        <p>Hannah More, the great authoress, came nearer the etruth when the said : " The cause of a deal of sin is bile." That bit of wisdom we can understand. Bile poisons the brain, and the brain is the organ of the mind and-senses. All the earthquakes that ever shook this wicked world never did as much harm to human beings as i 3 done every year by sleepy stomachs and lazy livers. •</p>
        <p>Here is an example "Before my arduous, task waa quite completed I began to suffer from biliousness and indigestion." The writer is Mr. James Seymour Morton, an author and editor living at 233, Lymington- avenue, Lordship-lane, London, N. His "arduous task" was the correcting of the proofs of a classical work, "about half of it in Greek. Writing further, he'says : "My sight was affected and the lines of type seemed blurred and confused." After spending a.. month at a health resort in Yorkshire, ha returned to London and resumed his labours. "But not for long," he says. • " About six or eight weeks after- wards I brae down again, the biliousness and indigestion . being as severe as ever." Just then a friend advised him to try Mother Seigel 's Syrup, which he did.. "Before I had taken half of the second bottle," he writes, "the pain after eating became less severe. By the time 1 had taken eight small bottles, my ailments had completely left me. , Indigestion is now no more to me ,than a painful memory." There is no magic about the cure of Mr. Morton. Mother Seigel s Syrup cleanses and invigorates the stomach, liver and bowels. That is the secret of its success in curing biliousness and indigestion. Take it daily after meals. The 28. (Id. bottle contains three times as much as the is. 14d. size,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>ST. HELENS SALARIES.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS SALARIES.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Town Conncil will have at the May meeting to &amp;al with several recommendations for increases in the salaries of various officials, and the education authority have added one more to the list. The Secretary for Education (Mr. Sharp) having applitd for an increrve, the Council are recommended to increase his salary, at present £4OO, to £6OO per annum by two instalments of £4O, and four succeeding instalments of £25 each, on the understanding that the increases are not to be considered as coming under the preaeut oour. domed ouperannuat* eobeme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>, _ ( . ' _ V. • , ,</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XII.</p>
        <p>[Henry Gastonard tells of a visit to his cousin, the Rev. Arthur Warrington, at Lowestoft.]</p>
        <p>Hotel Metropole, Lowestoft, July 31§t, 1905. Dear Paddy,—The late Douglas Jerrold remarked that he doted upon the sea—from the beach. It seemed yesterday that the sea doted upon me when it rolled me like a barrel in my bunk and moved me to appeal to high heaven for immediate annihilation. The passage across was about as dirty as a Channel passage can be and that, as you know, you who are fond of singing the glories of the deep (when you are on shore) is a shade which blackest night cannot surpass. I made no stay in London save to dine and to sleep at the. Carlton. The hansom which -drove me across Trafalgar Square showed me no amazing novelty, nor was I long enough in the city to find the place much changed. It is true that there is now a fine bit of life and colour where. once the dingy old Pavilion stood—for I visited the new building after dinner—and Leicester Square seems less shabby than formerly; but a man who comes over from Paris is rarely amazed by anything that London can show him, and admits her later day claims reluctantly. In one matter alone do I find a real advance, and that is the newer hotels, which, •1 yen-tufo to think, are just about as good as any in Europe. You did not answer my last letter—possibly because of your indignation; it may be be- cause of your want of interest. A man who is playing golf by the seashore (for I am con- vinced you are there) cares little for the fact that his neighbour is in a bunker,, and less for the means by which he may extract himself therefrom. Nor do I expect the Paddy of old time to be changed very much from that Hec- tor who has washed his hands of me upon more than one occasion, and is quite ready to do so again when my letters have made him angry enough. You think that I am playing a fool's game, Paddy, and your silence beers witness to the fact. So be it—until we gather scalps together at Portmarnock, and 1 play you for your boots, which, most likely, are unpaid for. You should know that Jules Farman's in- formation sent nrSe to London, and from Lon- don pell-mell to the East Coast of England, where I am to find Mimi the Simpleton among a company of clowns, and to withdraw her immediately from that humorous if unwashed society. If Farman is to be believed, the girl left us deliberately at Poissy, met an old com- rade of the Fetes upon the outekirts of Paris, joined his troupe immediately; and having acquired the distinction of dancing a Spanish -dance which is not Spanish, and of playing upon a guitar which is no guitar at all, set out -with' certain vagrants of the city to amuse the desperadoes of the outskirts and their obliging families. This company appears to have prospered for a little while, and then to have been drowned, partly by drink and partly by the winds of adversity. It' broke up at Rheims, sent straggling members on to 13russels,• there fell in with an Englishman of enterprise, Was re-organised by him and wafted over to our native country where upon the candy shore or the less accommodating shingle it amuses tha prosperous of suburbia and bears Isitness once more to the smallness of this terrestial globe and the fertile resource of its in habitants. Admit with me, my dear Paddy, that there is something wonderfully fine in this superb independence. Reflect upon the homes you know, the motherhood there, the gregarious %instincts of childhood, the bonds binding even the most wretched—do this, and then put side by side with :it the life and actions of such a Child as Mimi the Simpleton. She has not known a home these many years. She cannot have the remotest idea of the meaning of motherhood. The streets of a city have taught her the great lessons of self-reliance and of self-help. She ie not afraid to be alone. All the terrors which inflict the man of sub- stance, bills payable and bills due, the rise or fall of values, doubts _concerning the future, the perils of ambition; the bitterness of lass, these have no meaning for Mimi the Simple- ton. Let the sun shine and she will laugh. Give her bread and coffee and she has the riches of Crcesus. Take her to a cnfe, -where the lights dance and the fiddles are busy, and you are opening to her the floodgates of Paradise. Fortune is powerless against armour such as this. What matters it if the bread be lacking to-day—will- not to-morrow be more generous? Who shall complain that the sun acts in a cloud, when he will rise in splendour at dawn? You may ask me, Paddy, how it comes to be, if this be Mimi's creed of life, that I would intrude upon it with a more exacting philosophy or a friendship that is critical? I shall not attempt to answer these questions. I am drawn to this child, I know not by what spell. It is not love, as men commonly em- ploy that word. I do not seek, be sure of it, to put shame upon her, or to ask her to be the instrument of passion. But she has be- come necessary to my life. The vagrancy of the years has brought me to this, that there is just one other vagrant upon the road with whom I would share the wigwam, just one other little comrade who must help me to light the camp fire and to watch by it when the sun has set. To this end I am pursuing Mimi upon this Eastern strand. To this sel- fish purpose I am about to command that she shall cast off the Spaniards and remember the lessons of yesterday. She may refuse or she may consent—but i shall pursue the issue if necessary through the years. Accompany me, then, to this "gay" resort; follow me to the sandy shore of Lowestoft; but particularly, my dear Paddy, to the tem- porary home of na cousin, Arthur Warring- ton, who is here i a locum tenons, and has - already fascinated a large. number of females and a smaller (a much smaller) number of pious males. Arthur, I must admit, was not as pleased as he might have been to see me. The exclamation that he uttered was not alto- gether ecclesiastical, nor do I choose to re- member it; but it did not imply welcome, not as you and I understand the word. When he had recovered the - shock, he</p>
        <p>confessed to me that he believed me to be</p>
        <p>dying in Paris, and was natura;ly much re- lieved to find that his alarms were groundless.</p>
        <p>"I think you wrote me to that effed, Arthur," said I. "If I did not reply to your letter, pray forgive me." says lie, blushing to the roots of his beautiful auburn hair, "I do not think that I wrote, Henry—l had not your address—but we were both distressed, greatly distressed, I will say." "Well," said I, "you, teem to have been worrying, Arthur—but say no more about it; for here I am as sound as a smacksman and as hungry. What's more, I have come to stop :with you for a week or two if you will have me, which, if I remember your invitations correctly, is a pleasure you have been looking forward to for a long • time. Now, isn't it, Arthur? or am I mistaken?" Well, he stammered and stuttered again, and was in the middle of a parable about the green room and the pink room, when in comes cousin Martha, who is one of the jolliest little women in Suffolk and as clever a flirt as ever was yoked to a parson's cas- sock. Be sure Martha had her lord and master down in a minute and was trampling upon him in two. What! to turn their own flesh and blood into the streets who ever heard of such a thing! Of course I must stay with them. And wouldn't Ihe useful, tco She thought of that ia a minute.</p>
        <p>. ";.:: 47:,-,-4-t'..,',;:a.:• i',/,' T, of- • : 1 ~ -- , . - 4..*:(..... ,_. ;,,-.Z•- • .. .leil -t.l .. :.-- 4...- 4, '` BY --.4. --, - - , ~--,- .....5........_ ,4,4, .....-...- ,-.4. ...•,, ..,,,__ ••}l.- k . _ - .0.... ...?..1.-__ ~‘,...0......., . .....,.,..„ ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>MAX PEFIBERTON</head>
        <p>MAX PEFIBERTON</p>
        <p>Author of "The Iron Pirate " "The</p>
        <p>Hundred Days," "A Puritan's Wif:,"</p>
        <p>etc., etc.</p>
        <p>' "Don't you know we're to have a. Pageant here," says she, "of course you paj, Henry?" "Of course," said I, "anything that you tell me to do is done immediately, Martha. Shall we paj now or await a more solitary oc- casion?"</p>
        <p>• She expressed some confusion at this, and hastened to explain that they were about to have a Pageant at 11owestoft in which they 'would Celebrate the early arrival of the Danes in England and the glorious victories of Queen Boadicea. There were to be real Nors;men and real ships-,-to say nothing of bloody fights on the • foreshore and the gathering of the clans upon such heights as this land of marsh and marigolds can com- mand.</p>
        <p>"Arthur is to be a monk," says she. "Of course he will be a Proteatant monk, but he will wear sandals and shave his hair. I am to -play a British maiden, Harry. I - shall wear,._9l bearskin upon my shoulders and dye my hair red—now don't you think it will look beautiful?"</p>
        <p>. I assured her that nothing could be finer; and "as for the -reputation of the late Mrs. Astley of glorious memory, whose hair was to be wrapped about her feet whenever she stooped to the earth to do it, that," said I, "is already perished." This flattery was by no means unwelcome to cousin Martha. She told me that they were habing great trouble with the towns- folk, who had entered into the fray almost with too much spirit—especially a local ven- dor of wines, who wanted to play a modern monk Roger and to roll kegs of beer down the hills to the sea. There were many can- didates, I discovered, for the mtidens' parts, especially such maidens as were to be car- ried in the arms of the barbarians. Not less popular vvas the office of Druid, who would emit the mistletoe and by the Army and Navy Stores an generally cenduct the sacred rites as tradition and Christmas have sanctioned them.</p>
        <p>"And what ao you do, Arthur?" I asked the parson, "and what, if you please, is a Protestant monk? Forgive the ignorance which • remembers so little history. Of course it is a showy part, or they would not have asked you to play it. You were always a bit of an actor, weren't you, cousin? Don't you remember when you came out to us at Bordeaux,- that little Mademoiselle Charcot who—" He exclaimed "Hush, hush.! " it is astonishing how rarely a cleric is tolerant of reminiscences—and when my cousin little Martha implored me to tell her the whole story, Arthur silenced her immediately by an answer to my previous question. "A Protestant monk is one who carried the evangels before the days of the Papacy "Then against what did he protest, Arthur?"</p>
        <p>"Against the pagan intolerance of his day —just as we -protest in our own time against the pagan intolerance of the s2cial world. 1 intend to show the people that their vices are not changed from those of fifteen hun- dred years ago—" "'What a lively .business. Do you have e band?"..</p>
        <p>"It is not seemly to jest upon such a sub- jFct, Henry." "Oh, I know it—pray forgive me. Of course you are quite right. The old ~.gods are far from done with yet. Venus, I think, still gets an engagement occasionally, and Janus is. often looked in the face .by the morning papers. I admit that there is still something to be said for bearskins, while caves should be a godsend to the man who has just come down from Carey-ctreet. Why don't some of you parsons set us ,an example? Sell that thou hast—especially your brewery shares, Arthur—and live in a cave. I'll bet you what you like that cousin Martha in a bearskin would fill your church every Sun- day though half the bishops. in England were at the shop opposite." "The shop, Henry! Has Paris taught you to call a church a shop?" "In Paris, my dear Arthur, there are no monks. The Government has done the pro- testing."</p>
        <p>' "POI- the good of Prance, undoubtedly. The pure religion." "Tour 'religion, Arthur—but look here, I'm not out for a theological argument. Let us talk of the Pageant. What does Martha suggest that I should play?" "Would von like to be a knight in armour, Harry, and buy your own armour?" "That's a generous proposal, Martha. Did knights in armour come over with the Danes?"</p>
        <p>"Oh, dear no—l had forgotten that. Sup- po6e you were a Norsbman with beautiful long hair—" "To match yours, Martha? Arthur wouldn't like that."</p>
        <p>"But a Noreeman was such a splendid creature. You would have to speak in a guttural voice, Harry, and carry a scimitar?: . _</p>
        <p>"It sounds well, Martha; Ltt I should prefer the cellarer's part. I could look after the wine vats very well. If that's not it, how would you like me to play Henry the Eighth on the Field of the Cloth of Gold? I could get the costumes from Fox's; and I tell you what, if you want any humour, I'll drive on to the course in my motor-car." Arthur raised his eyes to heaven at this, while Martha seemed not a little affronted. They were both very serious about this busi- ness, poor dears, and not a little concerned for its success. To pacify them, I fell in with the Norseman idea, and then sat down to tea.</p>
        <p>You can have no idea, Paddy, of the meaning of these pageants to country towns, or of the enthusiasm they excite. People are in and out of this house every ten minutes to consult the "Master." Young girls, who would have blushed to show their / ankles yosterday, are popping themselves in skins and sandals with the glee of children. There in an eloquent Free Church Minister staying here who preaches twice every quarter against the theatre, ,And is now about -to ap- pear as a Druid priest with a sickle. He re- hearses his part like any actor at the Hay- market. Frivolity is immediately resented. When I suggested that a fleet of motor-beats should bring the Norseman to the shore, just to contrast the old ideas with the new, shocked looks met me, and an open protest. The most trivial mistake ceets its maker a reproof. I have just heard Arthur deliver a sound rating to a wretch4d tailor who thought that a Dane might very well carry a musket, and prcduced one left to him by his great - grandfather. The majesty of the Pageant_ brooks no levity.</p>
        <p>It will be apparent to you that I had little opportunity for any really private talk with my beloved cousin during these first days of my arrival, nor does there appear any possi- bility of my finding one until this mum- mery is over. For that matter, 4am very doubtful of the utility of such a rrocceding, nor do I think that I shall profit by it. A. sportsman would consent to my plan imme- diately; but Arthur is not a sportsman. I shall propose to him that we divide the in- heritance, and have done with it; but I know from the outset he will find some shifty plea upon which he may excuse him- self. If he does, I am what the world calls a ruined man—which is to say. Paddy, that I must work for my bread like any other decent fellow, and not complain if the loaf is yesterday's. Sometimes I admit that the change will be stupendous. I have wanted for nothing, as you know, since I was a little Child. My poor father indulged me in every way, so that now, at the rae,ture agc. at tweuty-faur,</p>
      </div>
