      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>"What! to turn their own flesh rind blood into the street?"</head>
        <p>"What! to turn their own flesh rind blood into the street?"</p>
        <p>I am as bles6 as many a man of forty. All that any sane bachelor cAn necd is to be purchased by seven thousand pounds a year. I can run a motor-car, keep a small sailing yacht, hire a shoot, travel. I need no house. for the finest hotels are open to me, with all their luxuries. From that to abject poverty is to be a swift descent. Paddy, I shall have a subsistence, but nothing more. The rest will go to dear cousin Arthur—to the glory of God and the purchase of a manor-house he .has his eye upon. Meanwhile, there is always Mimi. Would she come to me, I wonder, if I were poor? It might be so; in winch case one of the Beatitudes would again be justified, and Harry Gastonard awakened in an instant from his lethargy. I shall ask her this ques- tion when I find her—to-night or to-morrow, as the case may be, Paddy. Meanwhile, waft me your blessing • across the emerald seas, and find me, as always, Your friend,</p>
        <p>HARRY GASTONARD.</p>
        <p>or :iaving goou-nye to sucn jovial companions. My dear Harry, yours as of old, • PADDY O'CONNELL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>CHAPTER XIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII.</p>
        <p>[The Reverend Arthur Warrington writes in ,all haste to his Solicitor, Mr. James Fogg, of Serjeant's Inn, Strand.] St. Philip's, Lowestoft. Feast of St. Alphonsus.</p>
        <p>James Fogg, Esq. Dear Mr. Fogg,—l am -writing in much haste to inform you that 'my cousin, Henry Gastona.rd, has returned from Paris; and has had the effrontery to coma here. I trust I am not lacking in charity, nor harder than my fellows, but the life this young man has led in Paris makes him no fit companion tor my wife, who, I regret to say, appears to have taken a fancy to him, and insists upon his remaining with us. I write, therefore, to ask you if this will imperil in any way my, hopes under the will of Martha's uncle; are we doing. right to have Henry here, and is there any possi- bility of the judges construing this as a con- sent upon my part to any division of the valuable property?' Please inform me at once that. I may convince Mrs. Warrington of her folly, and put an end to this foolish in- fatuation.—Dear Mr. Fogg, yours very faith- fully, ARTHUR WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CHAPTER XIV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XIV.</p>
        <p>[Paddy O'Connell apologises for his silence.] The Donny House, Portmarnock. August 3rd, 1905. Dear Harry,—l should have told you be- fore that you did wisely to keys Paris, and, please Go, to see that "ilieant faymale " no more. 'Tis many a man that goes to the devil when he mighL have gone any other road for the asking of a• cheap ticket--and you, I am glad to see, are now restored to your senses and safely back in the land of the Sassenach, where I wish you much pros- perity. You have wits of your own, man, and a lively presence. Let me implore you to use them in some 'honourable occupation, if it is only to spite that long-legged beggar of a parson, who would drive the very saints out of heaven should he by any chance arrive in the neighbourhood. of that highly praised Meanwhile, Harry, I am waiting for your news of Mimi. What a droll little witch she was to be sure—and, man, 'tis lucky for ye that I am your friend,- or the Lord knows where she would have landed me. Seek her out by all means and restore her to civilisa- tion. Ye'll never need to be ashamed of her in any company. There are women who are born to be the light of men's lives; and if ever I saw one of the kind, the little lady whom Greuze should have painted is one of the company. Find her, I say, and play a gentleman's part towards her. You'll never regret* it, my boy. I am writing you but a brief letter, for the golfers here hf,ve been playing games upon this old bird and ruffling his plumage exces- sively. Yesterday;., young Willie Jackson made me a bet of a sovereign that he'd drive a ball off the face of his watch, and I took him immediately. Well, he goes out to the tee as cool as a martyr at the stake, Of ;ifs watch down on the nand, sticks a ball on the top of it, and smashes the whole lot to blazes. You could have heard me laughing two holes 0:7 as. I paid the money and chaffed him. _ _ - -</p>
        <p>"'Tie to 'she watchmaker ye'll be taking that same," says I, "and asking him what's wrong with the works? "—for there wasn't a ha'p'orth a the watch left, not enough to put in a teaspoon. To which h 3 answered, as impudent as anything: "I think not, Paddy; it was a penny It atch I bought 'ln Dublin three days -ago." Ye Basi7 think that I didn't show my_ nose in the cub-house any more that day, Henry. And, as if this wasn't enough, young Phil. pots persuaded me to play him with one of those pneumatic balls to-day, and. a fine game he had with me._ The harder I hit it— and you know that driving is my pride, being able to outdrive any man in Ireland when I hit one the harder I hit it the shorter, so to spe.ak, it went, until some of my finest brasses weren't travelling twenty yards, and my eleeks not ten. Be hanged to the ball!"- says I at last, "I believe it's bewitched." _ _</p>
        <p>"Oh, come," says young Philpote, "noth- inc of the kind, Paddy; 'tis your precious bad driving. Now, what did ye drink with your dinner last night?" "Not more than half a pint of whisky, and peThaps not so much." Men your eyesight must be going, Paddy. I'd see a doctor if I were you when 1 got back to town."</p>
        <p>"There's no better eyes in Ireland," says I; and I picked the ball up-and put it in my pocket. After that I knocked young Phil- pots all to blazes, BO I knew it was the ball, and would have said so if the Archangel Gabriel himself had come along and denied me. When I got back to the club-house, I took young Martin, the pro., aside, and asked him a few questions. "Why did ye sell me such a thing of a ball as that, Martin?" I asked him. • ,</p>
        <p>_ He professed great astonishment. "There's nay a, better baa' made," says he. "Will ye play a round with it yourself, Martin?"</p>
        <p>"Ay, when there's wind in it." "Wind!" cried I, beginning to undcr6tand. "True," says he, "when there's wind in it; but no' when a gentleman hag pricked her wi' a noedle before the oth2r gentleman ganged oot."</p>
        <p>Henry—l saw it in a moment ! That young devil of a PhilpOts had let all the air out of the ball before - I began to play with. it. The' story's .all over the place—l'll never show my face here for a month. unless it be to pull grxei el ilwr a! Mewur too ift;08.4.02•0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>CHAPTER XV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XV.</p>
        <p>{"The Chimes" at Yarmouth, and what Henry Gastonard learned of them.] Thf..s New Vicarage, Lowestoft. August 6th, 005. Dear Paddy,—Byron told us that: "Christians have burned each other, quite persuaded ; That all thc Apostles would have done ati they did."</p>
        <p>I am not quite sure that my dear cousin, Arthur, would not put me immediately to the stake were it not for this worldly— little wife of his., who leaps thfough the hoop of his philosophy lik.3 a clown at the circus, and is never ,so pleased as when her antics move him to paroxysms of jealousy. Let me,- none the less, postpone for the moment a narration of this particular tragedy, and thank you for your letter. I am sorry to hear that they let the wind cut of your pneumatic golf ball, and so provoked you to expressions not found in the cate- chism—but, my dear Paddy, is not half the world flogging balls so treated, and are not the fortunate few those, who can command a euperfluity of that necessary gas by which mankind achieves success?</p>
        <p>I give you this for. consolation. Would to heaven you could console me as effectually. For, to be candid, Paddy, I am as hard driven by my doubts as' ever a man was in this world. Ytsterday I saw Mimi for the second time. My first visit :cgs paid to her almost immediately after 1 had written to you; - and a sorry enough pilgrimage it was so full of drab shades and mournful har- monies that I write of it with reluctance, and do not speak of it at all. Recollect that I had traced the child to the old town of Yarmouth, and was determined to seek her there. Of course, my car is here, and serves me well at *these times. A fast journey in the famous "forty," which has carried us together upon many a merry venture, brought me to that fishing village they call Gorleston; then to an even more crabbed street the main thoroughfare of Yarmouth to wit. I know nothing of these places, but the approach to _them depressed me greatly, and left me but ill prepared for the really superb sea-front which .a side street„ of Yarmouth presently disclosed to me.</p>
        <p>It -is inconceivable, Paddy, that such a parade as this should be so little known to the children of civilization. Depict a won- derful strand of the purest golden sand, a gentle sea with many ships in a narrow strait, a wide thoroughfare abutting upon the promenade, and a mile of houses as a background •to it all. Do this, I say, and you will still have the poorest idea of Yar- mouth—for its glory lies in the booths they have erected upon the sand, in its entertain- ments, its wide piers, its floral halls, its orderly gardens; and superabundant band- stands. Such a city upon a seashore I have never seen in all my life. I drove my car to a decent hotel on the. front, and I descended presently, feeling as lost as an African set down suddenly at Ludgate-circus. This would have been about a quarter to eight o'clock of a splendid summer evening. Thousands of lights were now blazing upon the promenade, lights large and small, and of all the hues of the rainbow. Turn your ears where you would, music pleased or offended them. And what music, Paddy ! now that of a fine military band, again of a hurdy-gurdy, and upon that the tinny notes of a worn piano, laboriously thumped by some child of the academies. Nor was this sufficient, for youths passed raving of Jenny or Sarah, and here and there a woman screeched some incoherent lines which tue music-hall or the sea beach had taught her. I crossed the street, and ventured upon the golden sands. The barbarity of the scene inipressed me strangely. That such an ar- tist, such a born child of all that is really the fruit of genius as Mimi the Simpleton, should have sunk to this, inflicted me with an intolerable melancholy which nothing could relieve.</p>
        <p>Here, Paddy, here I must find Mimi the Simpleton. To this Ultima Thule some in- spiration of the nomad's life had wafted her. You can have little idea of the emotions which followed me to the quest of her as I threaded the human lanes, and would have closed my ears to their voices, but could not. But I will not weary you with a recital to so little purpose. Let it be sufficient to say that when I discovered Mimi at last, it was not upon the lower sands where the meaner booths are set, but in a considerable wooden structure built almost against the prome- nade, and promising at least a better atmos- phere and a better company. Here a bill at the door informed that "The Chimes" were performing, and that for the inconsiderable sum of sixpence I might be privileged to hear the famous singer Wat Urling in his famous song "Bonny Bill," and also to witness the gyrations of the Spanish dancer "Alphonsine. Other lines accorded notoriety to a certain Jack Bends]!, and to a person by the name of Bertie Idden, who, it 'appears, had played. the banjo before the crowned heads of Europe, and was still alive to . tell the tale. These promises I read swiftly, and, paying a shilling for the front row, I passed into the place, and saw Mimi again. (To be Continued.) •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>£ S D OF FLOWERS.</head>
        <p>£ S D OF FLOWERS.</p>
        <p>The takings of several ccsters who hawk flowers round the beet London neighbourhoods top £2O a week each. At a big florist's in the West End £4O is taken most mornings for buttonholes. A nobleman in Scot- land orders from a London firm several times a year three dozen bouquets at £5 each. They are enclosed in hermetically-sealed tubes before being despatched on their journey. This ensures them arriving perfectly fresh. Many clergymen cultivate and sell choice flowers. A vicar in Carmarthen, for instance, draws £5OO a year from a London dealer for lilies of the valley. A vanlead of Marechal Neil roses arrives in London once a week during the season from an old lady's estate in Berkshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>HOME CURE FOR CATARRH.</head>
        <p>HOME CURE FOR CATARRH.</p>
        <p>Special Offer of Trial Treatment.</p>
        <p>Those who are suffering from any form of Catarrh of the nose or throat should Investi(Y'ate the simple home treatment advised by Mr. Munyon, the medical philanthropist. Full parti- culars, with a special offer of trial treatment and a Manual of Home Remedies, will be sent post free to anyone (mentioning the Warrington ." Examiner ") by Munyon, 01, New Oxford Street, London, W.C,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WHAT IS PROTECTION?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS PROTECTION?</p>
        <p>[Br MR. H. WIIIMAM.]</p>
        <p>Let us answer the above question straight off and in a few words. "That which protects" is as near like a dictionary answer as anything I can think of. That it does not fill the -order is made quite clear in Germany, or why the many thousands parading the streets of Berlin and labelling themselves the unemployed, and crying - out in the bitterness of their poverty, " Give us work, or find us bread ? " Herr Calwer, one of Germany's best authorities on the labour question, says : "Hardly a day passes in which it is not reported from some part of the country that working hours have been reduced or workmen dismissed. Trade unions are holding conferences all over the country to discuss what had best be done in face of hard times, both present and to come." • The only good Protection in Germany, or greater Germany, does is to make the rich richer, and also to make wider and wider the gulf that separates the worker from the idler. THE EXPERIENCE OF CONTINENTAL NATIONS.</p>
        <p>It is money that votes in Germany, .and it is the rich who maintain the protective system there. At the last election the Socialists polled by far the largest number of votes—nearly 21000,000—yet there is not a single Socialist in the Prussian Diet. It is quite evident that Protection does not give to the German workman the wherewithal to buy bread and butter,nor does it prevent him from rioting in the streets. • Is it possible any of the friends of Protection can give us an encouraging sample of its benefits to the poor of Holy Russia? What it most certainly does is to foster the growth of a rich, heartless and tyrannical class, and it also 'builds to scatter a mass of whom Swinburne wrote :</p>
        <p>"Help is n.-me in heaven, hope sees no gentler star; E srtb is bell, and hell bo Ns down before the Tsar."</p>
        <p>Tolstoy, speaking of his fellow countrymen says: "It was true, that for a bare subsistence, people, con- sidering themselves free men, thought it necessary to give themselves up to work such as, in the days of serfdom, not one slave-owner, however cruel, would have sent his slave to."</p>
        <p>Shall we see a better state of things in Italy. A recent writer in describing a situation that was typical says : " We saw families last winter in a town where there had been no meat for over a month, and if they had their portion of cornmeal. polenta at noon it meant they could have no supper at night." Speaking•of the peasant class the winter says : "His condition was such that in order to get enough black bread for his family he must accept the most grinding terms, vaying in wheat for the little patch of exhausted soil which required the combined labour of man, wife and children to eke out a miserable existence," CURSE OF LANDLORDISM.</p>
        <p>In Russia, Italy and Germany, landlordism is the first curse, as it is in England, to stand as a lien on the- labour of the worker. In France things are much different. A land system, which, though not perfect, gives a living to the worker and enables him to give a decent, though not luxurious home to the small family he has been wise enough to gather around him. At the present time one of those periodic spasmodic fits of tariff revision has the two protectionist nations of France and America in its grip, -while it the same time the two nations are wondering what Canada thinks of it all.</p>
        <p>PROTECTION NO BENEFIT TO THE WORKER. As to the United States and Protection I have this to say, Protection does not benefit, not one iota, the common labourer who is a married man, and who is bringing up a family. This man has to meet the cheapest of labour from the poorest districts of Europe and Asia. He not only has this difficulty before him, but he has also the almost impossible task of placing his foot on the first rung of the ladder that leads up to what is known as the American standard of living and has been built up by the highly-paid skilled workmen who, in too many cases, form rings quite as corrupt as any of the Trusts. Not a city in the United States to-day but has its out-of-work problem to solve. Protection does not and cannot, and it is foolish to expect it to solve it. The system of Protection, as it works in the United states, is a fearful and wonderful thing. It keeps the employer at home with his nose to ,the grindstone, while his wife, sons and daughters trot the globe, seeking what they may devour,- and scheming how beet to cheat the Custom House and hush up the perjured conscience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MR. CARNEGIE'S ADVICE.</head>
        <p>MR. CARNEGIE'S ADVICE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Carnegie recently wrote an article for one of, our magazines in which he declared that the steel industry needs no protection. More recently and before the Ways and Means Committee he re-affirmed this opinion,but "on examinationhe proved so disrespectful and disingenuous that it was thought best to disregard his evidence." While Carnegie was in business he as hated by his competitors beyond all other human beings. Of course one can well understand that this library-giving Scotch gentleman is steadily increasing the number of his enemies. Those who are not yet over the rushing stream that leads to that which perishes rather than to eternal life, hate him worse than sin, and all because he is wining to advise the United States Government to kick away, and send floating down the stream the bridge called Protection. Some months ago my readers and I visited the packing houses and there learned of some of the toothsome morsels that the rich man gives we poor mortals to eat. Let us now visit the Pittsburg workers and see how sweetly, they glide along the primrose path so well prepared by the benevolent Scotch Laird. A few years ago one of our rich men, Russell Sage, was gathered to his fathers, and left an immense sum of money to his wife. He must have known she was a pretty sensible woman, as indeed she was, for she partly financed what is now known as the "Pittsburg Survey." The group of men forming this Survey Company is made up of some thirty expert sociologists who have been investigating Pittsburg for a year, finding out things the city itself did not know.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>CONDITIONS IN PITTSBURG.</head>
        <p>CONDITIONS IN PITTSBURG.</p>
        <p>Pittsburg is a city of Slays, Italians, Poles, Negroes, Irish, Scotch, English, Germans, Jews, Syrians, Bohemians, Japanese, Indians and Americans. All these peoples are in Pittsburg to work, and they rarely go short of it. They work twelve hours a day, and as the report says, their work leaves them so exhausted that they care for neither reading, recreation, religion, or home life. One man after-many years of such work, said he would have been far happier in the penetentiary. It was found that as high as 50 per cent. of all young foreigners who come to Pittsburg contract typhoid fever within two years of their arrival. I cannot deal with the employment agencies. Their work is described as "infamous." In one part of Homestead, near the Carnegie Steel Works, it was found that one baby in every three died before its second birthday. A Japanese veteran of the recent war told one of the investigators that "lie looks upon his experience upon battle-fields as quite commonplace compared with his experience in the steel mills." Over five hundred men are killed every year in the course of their work, and an unknown number seriously injured. The victims are usually the pick of the men ; they are the young men, half of them are native born, 51 per cent. have families, 30 per cent. more are single men who partly or wholly support their families. A short time ago a rev. gentleman who had been in the States a little over twenty minutes said it was a lie to talk of Americans worshipping a god of gold.l think he was wrong. With 500 men dying each of typhoid fever, the movement to clear the water-supply was blocked and exploited at every turn. So it is in San Francisco to-day and in pretty well every city from Pacific to Atlantic. Pittsburg is a city which does not know the number of its children of school age, nor the physical status of the children of its classes, it is a town which for five years did not demand a report from its heath department. The report goes on to say that Pittsburg is, for richer, for poorer, in sickness and in health, for vigour, waste, and optimism is rampantly American, I could give to my. readers samples of cases of the benevolence of the men who pose as philanthropists and who support Protection because of their intense love of their employers. One briefly given must suffice. A Ruthenian age 30 and his wife of 28,newly married and looking forward with hope. The husband work- ing on a platform over a lime-stone mill fell and so ended his working life. His wife within a month of confinement, into this grim situation entered the baby, The husband in the 'hospital, the wife lived on thirty dollars a month the company paid her, then the com- pany cut off the allowance and she demanded a settle- ment. They offered her £l2O, and she had to pay all hospital bills. At last accounts, the matter was still unsettled. How many Slays, Lithuanians, and Italians are injured in one year in Pittsburg is not known. No reliable statistics are compiled. If the widow of a man killed in a mine or mill in Pensyl- vania lives in 'Europe, she can not recover damages, although the accident may be entirely due to the neglect of the company. Because of this ruling, certain strong companies in the Pittsburg district seldom pay a cent to the relatives of the deceased if they dwell beyond th? Efectg.</p>
        <p>There is no evidence in the above that Proteicti° helps the common labourer. As I have already isa makes the rich richer. It enables them to bulfo palaces in London and to pose as philanthropists. a word, Protection means robbery. Mr. Carnegie.%i Infant industries no longer need protection ; t the steel and other industries have now grown l'eY°o the need of tariff protection." So long as a fel' o,‘ 3 the land and all that is in and on it and so logthe few' own the outcome of other men's brains, Patent Laws, neither Free Trade and still leo teetion will find work for the unskilled, nor ennsb3"</p>
        <p>work for the skilled workers,</p>
        <p>H. WHITIIA3I.</p>
        <p>San Francisco</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
