        <p>lips. w. . . . . The dangh.er of God ascended into the House of Her Father. 0 of Mr. Madden quoted a number of eulogies of Josorf Arc. The testimony of all, he said, was in her fa! 00$ and England to-day, amongst all the nations, wits lot ready to wipe out the stain and to honour gi°l.l Joan of Arc. As far as Catholics were concerllo,,b 0 criticism was at an end and Protestants had, "001 noble feeling of generosity, endorsed what. Roine,oo# doing to-day. Rome had spoken. The cause a of Arc was ended. (Applause). The Rev. Father lltroorss, who presided, paid. oc tribute to the work of the District Nursing As 05; tion. He said the priests had to live and work tone! he sick and they constantly came across the 51)0...i-fel work done by the nurses, whose visits were as visits. Father Buggins also spoke highly of erica Madden whose name was a household word for elocill for in Warrington, cordially thanked Mr. Macnaghteliol lending the Hippodrome for that deserving cause;ool acknowledged the courtesy and kindness of the manager (Mr. Longden).</p>
