        <p>',PENDENT 3 ISTHOD CE! APEL.—The usual tlcs Y Pleas.int Stuidar afternoon in connection with ov'e-PaPel was held on Sturlay afternoin last, presided was " the superintendent, r. G.Fenton.A short address g, iven M to the children by Mr. W. Kenyon. Solos renlestl,ng by Mr. H. Overton who gave an excellent rendering of "The Children's Home." Miss E. Renyon Y pearl red with much feeling " Someons will enter the glte," and Mr. J. R. Fletcher was equally _rzce sent in the rendering of " The Silver Li&amp;ng "end c.l Ben -•ate of Pearl." A duet entitled "The Harbour Was also very ably sung by Miss E. Rancorn and Col Barton. Mr. H. Griffiths was the organist. fuudeetions_ wens t, *ken in aid of the missionary</p>
        <p>Padgate. Z..‘PGATE WESLEY GUILD.—The monthly devotional Of the Guild was held on Monday evening when aPer was given by the late pastor, the Rev. W. Mellor so the subjeat "Who and what are saints." In his open- hvgprenisrlts Mr. Mellor said he was glad once more to was° the opportunitv of speaking to the members and obt,,l°fry he was nimble to congratulate the guild on the shield,-but trusted they might be more another year. Mr. , Mellor then went on to the roeining of the "Word " saints " and what re,,in out of its meaning. He said it was impossible to aho'r,et the word to those in the glorified state, and the Wed that it could be applied to persons living on He said saints are those who are. sine:Med t51.14‘ is separated from all that is worldly and devoted which are not sinful. All true Christians to ,?1•01 to God are saints and whoever offels himself he'lls service of God ancearistiaoity becomes a saint. vilelttli:ged the members to be very careful in judging triai,uer a man be a saint or rot as we cannot tell what kr. others may have to contend with. In conclusion tr, :0411or said that in order to be a true saint we must fettand reproduce in a marked degree the pure and per- ,erhameter of our Lord Jesus Christ. A solo entitled 144'; „shiest Redeemer" was very expressively sung by ai Dr. Bennett and the meeting was very ably pre- tile:°ver by Mr. F. Wright. Miss Edith Bennett was "ec9tnpanist.</p>
