- Ha said r'ar(l '1"-1 U.trter 03 board had he key.. He went ezird• ad "lid good-night to the watchman and went 1/4 .nr 941.1eftm I found the boy Carter, on hoard and ha. ahiP's kit including a sou'wester,hootF, etc., nli In answt•r to 'Ur. Gould, prisoner said that nt ore ' f raining the ai • a)ird with the intention o j 4gitt Pector Pearse asked for a further remand of '43" as a mats of the ship, the chief witness, was eranda "`"'. and *ould not be track until the following eirilittand r Y*---Theed atoPPA: l'tcraitnicohnarwna.s grantol and the prisoner tl IX DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTION. .4,l.sti!sreos,'lt or the Booths Hill Ward fight on which ..tic,ll.ls' contested seat 111 the District Council raucla interest was focussed, was declared on</p>
        <p>Saturday night from the Town Hall steps by Mr Mulhird, clerk to the Council, the result being F. G. Moston W. Cowper</p>
        <p>Majority Mr. Moston, in returning thanks, said that the elec- tion bad been one of the keenest ever fought in Lynn°. He hoped to justify the continued confidence they had reposed in him, and he took the meanin., of the result to be that they api•reciated his work on the Council during the past nine years. (Appleuse.) Mr. T. IT. Warburton also addressed the crowd, thank- ing them for allowing him to go in unopposed. He supposed he had satisfied the electors of Heatley Watd, but if requested to do so was quite prepared-to hold a public meeting to give an account of his stewardship, and to discuss any item that might be brought before the Council.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. R. H. Gotobed, also returned unoppored, thanking the electors for the honour they had done him, and expressing his sorrow at not being with them owing to indisposition. THE CROS3.—Great indignation is being expressed in the village at an act of vandalism by the Government Telephone Department. The ancient crops is "being spoiled by the placing of huge, unsightly telephone poles from which the wires are to stretch right across the top of the cross, almost touching the weather vane on the top. The Postmaster General has been written to, and an indignation meeting is to be held by the inhabitants of the village.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 10, 1909.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. Garnett : Is there any promise when we shall have it flagged? The Chairman: Probably some time this year. Mr. Garnett: In the meantime can we have cinders put on ? The Chairman: I will mention it to the authorities. Mr. Ambler contended .that they were flagging the wrong footpath. It was the Stores side that wanted flagging. Mainley : That is the side that I live on. (Laughter). Mr. Ambler stated that the other side was in good condition and the stores side should be atttended to. The Chairman indicated that he would bring the matter before the authorities. SPEED OF MOTOR CARS THROUGH THE VILLA-GE:</p>
        <p>Mr. Ambler explained that in accordance with a proposition made by Mr. Woodward at a Council meeting he thought something should be done in reglrd to having this excessive speed checked. It was a dis- grace and sooner or later there would be a serious accident. 'Mr. Woodward had proposed warning notices to be erected, but up to the present time he had not seen them. Mr. Garnett staled that ten miles an hour speed limit was absurd. The road was not a children's play- ground. It might not be generally known, but a car travelling at the into of 20 miles an hour was more under control than a horse and trap travelling at ten miles an hour. _ _ _ . .</p>
        <p>....—., Mr. Ambler proposed the adoption of a resolution to have warning notices at each brot,k (the two extremities of the village). Mr. Ditcbfield seconded. Mr. Witbinshaw proposed as an amendment, and this was seconded by Mr. Garnett, that white circular discs be fixed by Mr. Ambler's house and by the brook (Widnes end of the village), and the triangles fixed by the residences of Mr. Gilbert and Mr. Parsonage. The amendment was caned by a large majority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
