      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Penketh and Sankey.</head>
        <p>Penketh and Sankey.</p>
        <p>MEN'S BIBLE CLASS.—The open Sunday of the Men's Bible Class was held on Sand4s, in the We3leyan Chapel. The address which was given by Mr. A. Monateld, of Warrington, was one which will be long remembered. The subject was" Marriage customs in the East." The address was most edifying, and many expressed the wish for Mr. Mounfleld to come to Ponketh again. Mr. C. E. Parker presided and the Bible Class choir rendered the anthem." Immortal love." Mrs. Parker officiatell at the organ.</p>
        <p>BAND OF HorE.—The last entertainment of the Penketh Band of Hope was held in the Wesleyan school on Friday night. Mr. Murphy presided over a large attendance. The meeting was opened with the singing of the hymn "Stand up for Jesus." 4 pod programme was submitted which consisted of a piano- forte sale by Miss Diggory, solos by Misses Ireland and Rovtledge and Master Alex Plumpton ; Messrs. Ray and Phcenix contributed a duet in fine style. Master A. Bates delighted the audience with a Lanca- shire dialect reading; recitations by Miss Ruth Loxley, Messrs. F. Carrington and Victor Owens were well receicei ; seven of the children gave a delightful action song entitled "Grow little mushroom grow." Mrs. James Dodds gave an address on temperance work. A vote of thanks which was proposed by the secretary, (Mr. Thorcaa Dunabin) and seconled by Mr. Loxley to Mrs. James Dodds for presiding, and all who had taken part brought the meeting to a dole.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>THE PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Parish Council was held on Mond tv evening. Dr. Murray MIS re-elected chair- man and Mr. J. G. Witbinshaw vice-chairman.</p>
        <p>The oversee ts were re-elected, viz :—Dr. Murray, Me.Esrs. J. G. Withinshaw and James Ditchtletd.</p>
        <p>Dr. Mtirray read a letter from Mr. Alfred Seed, resigning from the School Management Committeo as be had to leave the district. The committee accepted his resignation. His successor will be elected at the next rceetine of the Council•</p>
        <p>Mr. Witbinshaw proposed that the Chairman represent the Council on the Education Bub-committee (Earlestown). Mr. Routledge seconded, and the motion was clrricd</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL</head>
        <p>SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Sankey Paris' Council was held on Monday evening in the Council Schools, when there were present :—Messrs. W. Taylor (chairman), W. Furness, J. W. Walters, W. H. Ireland, H. Pair dough, W. Ditchfield and J. Smith (clerk). Tbe Clerk informed the Council that that was a special meeting of the Council to discuss the sanitary collection.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fairclough asked if there had been any corres- pondence. The Clerk said that the Chairman bad recei‘.,ed a letter from Mr. John Randall, of Barnes Bridge House, Sankey, stiting that he had enclosed a copy of a letter which he was sending to the Chairman of the Rural Council and he thought it was his duty to acquaint them with its contents. Thsre was in his opinion no justice or fairness or reasonableness in-the fact that the ratepayers of Sankey had got to contribute:to the sani- tary arrangements of the Garden City scheme and they ought to pay for their own sanitary arrangements out of their own pockets. The Clerk added that although Mr. Randall said be enclosed a letter which he had sent to the Rural Dis- trict Council he had failed to do so.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fair°lough said he did not understand a man writing a letter of that description and Mr. Furness said the inference to be drawn from that was that the man wanted the sanitary collection to go on the rates. Mr. Walters observed that under present conditions a man paid 4s.,and in addition to that he had to pay for his sanitary collection by private contract. Mr. Fairclough maintained that if the charge went on the rates it would only benefit a few property owners and no householders. Mr. Walters said that the householders were in a worse position now than if it went on the rates. Mr. Ireland said it would be all right for the matter to goon the rates now, but in future years it would increase and they would never know where it would end.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walters maintained but the ratepayers were not satisfied with the present condition of thing. Mr. Robert Wood,of -Lime Kiln Lane Farm,Burtobwood, had intimated to the Cluncil that he would take on th,3 work that Mr. Wuittleld left. •</p>
        <p>Mr. Ditchfield proposed that the matter remained as it was, and did not go on the rates. • The Chairmsn seconded. • Mr. Walters moved an amendment tbat it be put in the hands of the District Councl. - There was no seconder and the resolution, was carried.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, Mr. Furness owl the Clerk asked Mr. Woods, who had been called into the room, several questions and he said he thoroughly understood that he bad to get the permission of the owners of the houses to collect the nightsoil and to get the money from the owners of the houses. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ The Parish Council could not help him in any way financially and Mr. Woods intimated that he understood that.</p>
        <p>At this point Mr. Woods left the room. Mr. Pairclough proposed that Mr. Furness mention to the District Council that they had got a suitable man who was willing to take on the work. Mr. Ditch&amp;ld seconded and it was supported by Mr. Ireland. Four voted for the4lfesolut'on, Mr. Walters voted against it; and Mr. Furness did not vote. The Clerk read a letter from Mr. F. Birch, Lovely. lane, thanking the Council for their motion of sympathy with the family of the fate Mr. A. Birch in their bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ANNUAL PARISH MEETING.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL PARISH MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of ratepayers of Penketh was also held on Monday evening, Dr. Murray presiding. The Chairman said this was the 27th annual meetiag and he would endeavour. to give tha parishioners an account of the work which had enraged the Councit's attention during the past year. They had now suc- ceeded in baying letters deliverel in a morning which in former times would not have been delivered until the afternoon's mail. They had also a late po3t at the mission box at 9.45 p.m., w'aich he coosidered waa a distinct alvantage for the village. The Council bad pushed the lighting of Perry-lans forward but the London and North-Western Railway Company could not see their way to light the lane with gas,-so that they mould have to put up with the ancient method Of having the lane lighted with oil lamps. With regard to the bridge at Sankey the County-Council had passed a resolutioa which he (the Chaim tre) considered as" shabby" ; that was the application that the War- rington Corporation should bear half the costs. In his opinion they were practically shelving the matter. ' The alteration of Station-road bridgewould have to wait till the funds of the District Council were in a satisfactory cindition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FOOTPATIIS_QUESTION.</head>
        <p>A FOOTPATIIS_QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mainley reminded the Council that a resolution was [weed at the last annual Meeting to have the flagging of the •Warrington-road footpath attended to, tut up to the present time the footpath was in the same condition, and he contended that it was a disgrace to a back lane. lie noticed that the roads were kept in good order for traction engines and motor cars to tear up, but tho footpath which the Council had promised to attund to at the last annual meeting had not yet been flagged, and he thought it was quite time that it was done.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: It will be done when we get the money. Mr. Msinley : We pay rates for these to be kept in order. Why are they not kept in order? The Chairman : You don't quite understand all the ins and outs. We shall have the footpaths flagged when the District Council have the money,if you are prepared to pay extra rates. It is a question of finance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>W AMONG THE ;BOUNDARIES.</head>
        <p>W AMONG THE ;BOUNDARIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward proposed that the boundaries be walked. It was 18 sears s'nce that was done. Mr. Mainley seconded. He did not like these old customs given up. Was there not a dinner at the end of the walk ? .</p>
        <p>•,•••• ••••••• • Mr. Woodward: Yes, at the Chairman's expense. (Laughter.) The motion was agreed to. THE RECREATIOAT GROUND. Mr. Woodwaid proposed that sand be put round the swings on the recreation ground. Mr. Garnett: I should think that the speed of the motor ca-s this summer would supply that for you. (Laughter). The motion was agreed to. Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>NURSING ASSOCIATION.—In an interesting report on the second year's work of this excellent institution the committee state that the totAl number of cases visited during 1908 was 214. Of these 192 received regular visits, and 22 occassional visits. Of the 192 regular' patients 147 were convalescent or relieved ; 19 dead ; six transferred to hospital, and 20 remain on the books. The Treasurer's report shows that after paying all accounts to December 31st, 1908, there was a 'balance in the bank ot £4B 16s. 3d. The total subscriptions amounted to £lll 12s. Bd. This result was satidfactory, but the total amon.nt collected fell short of 1907 by £2B 13s. Bd. That there was a balance in the bank was due to the lessened expenditure, the expenses of the year included about £2O for drugs and utensils required to start the work of the Association. HILL CLIFFE AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF HOPE.—There was a large gathering in the Provi- dence Schoolroom on Monday evening when the last entertainment of the Session was held. It was "chil- dren's night" andthe meeting was conducted by the younger members of the society. The first part was -presided over by Miss Carrie Cain, whe won approba- tion for an admirable little speech. The young lady selected to give an address was Miss Edith Kirkland, whose "maiden speech '` was one of the best ever delivered- on these occasions, being full of interesting points and well delivered. The second part was in the hands of the sterner sex, the chair being taken by. Master Thomas Pearse, ha took the position with much confidence and conducted-the proceedings in a praise- worthy manner. The i speaker n this pat t was Master 'Clifford Jones, who exhorted all present to zally round the flag of temperance. A good programme was gone through, consisting of songs byi, Miss Lena Powell, Francis Gamble and Edith Hahiblett. Recitations by Lily Isherwood, E. Nlacarty, C. Sanders, W. Bates, G. entre, F. Sandford, A. Strange, F. Daniels, B. Phillips and W. Stringer. Two violin duets were well given be Arthur and Frank Tilby, and the dialogues "Six little nurses," by D. Cain, E. Whitfield, B. Deighton, E. Follitt, A. Forsyth and M. Meikle ; "Schoolgirls at the ferry," by M. Cain, M. Mounfleld, L. Powell and T. Macdermid ; "The temperance foot- ball team," by M. Richmond, F. Daniels, E. Grounds, E. Stringer S. Mounfield, E. Bennett, E. Whitfield, R. Grounds and E. Clark, were all kwell given and much enjoyed. Humorous songs were contributed by G. Sanders and J. Macarty, which provoked roars of laughter. Mr. A. Moimfield (president) presented a number of medals awarded to the senior members under the " Holdfast system." A number of other members holding similar medals were awarded an additional "bar of honour," which signifies another year's faithful service. A vote of thanks to all who had taken part in the proceedings was proposed by Mr. G. Whitfield (secretary), seconded by Mr. H. Brereton. Mr. G. A. Whitfield accompanied all musical items on the harmonium.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Parochial Committee met on Tuesday evening in the Hall Cafe. Mr. George Pilling presided and there were present :—Mesars..J. H. Clarke, J. Hallows, H. J. Westbrook, J. Whitley, P. Pearson, T. R. Westbrook, G. .T. Brown, J. McDermid, G. F. Ashton (cleik), J. Swinton (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TREE-PLANTING</head>
        <p>TREE-PLANTING</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter which bad been sent from Mr. Edwin Bradshaw, clerk for the Parish Council,-to the Local Government Board stating that a 1 ical Society had planted -some • three or four hundred trees on the f ootpaths of the main roads in that parish. he road authorities were the Cheshire County Council and the Runcorn Rural Council. The trees had been attained by voluntary contributions and he had intimated- that the District Council would shortly ask the Parish Council to take over the trees and maintain them. Would they advise him whether the Parish Council funds were available for that purpose, if a Parish meeting adopted a resolution authorising the Parish Council to take them over.</p>
        <p>The. Clerk then read a letter from the Assistant Secretary of the Local Government Boerd in reply,and which stated that there was no statutory authority which would enable the Parish Council of Stockton Heath to incur expenses in connection with the trees On the footpaths of the main roads in the Parish. The Rural Council could, however, undertake the mainten- ance of the trees if invested with the powers of Eection 43 of the Public health Amendment Act, 1890, by an order of the power under section 5 of that Act, and if an agreement were entered into with the County Council for the purpose under the Highways and Bridges Act 1891. if an order were made putting section 43 of the Act of 1890 in force in the parish it would accord with the practices of the Board to declare the expenses to be special expenses chargeable to the pari,h. The clerk proceeded to read the Act which is as fol- lows :—Any Urban authority may if they see fit cause trees to be planted in any highway repairable by the inhabitants at large within their district and may erect guards or finces for the protection of the same pro- vided that this power shall not be exercised, nor shall any trees so planted be continued so as to binder the reasonable use of the same, or so as to become a nuisarrp.c.r.injurious Warty adjacent owner or occupier.</p>
        <p>- -- Mr.H.3.Wesibrook thought it was rather premature to discuss that. The clerk sent the letter more as a private (nquiry so that when the time came he would he ready with an answer, He (the speaker) suggested that it should be left over until they got the requtst from the tree plauting council to take the trees over. lite matter was referred to the Parish Cottacil with- out any further observations. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>swiso BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE swiso BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The clerk real a letter from the Manchester Ship Canal in reply to the councils of the lBth iust, which referred too the stoppage of the traffic, because of the bridge,on the :29th January. It explained that there bad been a lireakage and added that it was only the first, occasion since the canal had bcen opened. In conclu- sion the ari ter expressed regret and pointed out that there were other bridge h over the canal at a convenient distance.. _ -</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallows observed that that was not the first occasion. He remembered the bridge being stopped for two hours previously.</p>
        <p>THE LA':IIPS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Warrington Cor- poration, Gas Department, stating that with regard to the metal numbers on the lamps they in Warrington had found that the numbers were broken by the boys throwing stones. They (said they would, however, ask the makers the prices and eubmit them to the Council.</p>
        <p>STREET WORKS.</p>
        <p>The Committee approved the plans of Mr. Robert Garnett for the private street works and they were ordered to be submitted to the Local Government Boud.</p>
        <p>' A resolution was passei that the surveyor get quantities so as] to be able to proceed with that work immediately the sauction of the Board was received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>LIGHTING OF THE VILLAGE.</head>
        <p>LIGHTING OF THE VILLAGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Whitley -c-o—M-Pfain-ed of the lamps not being lit earlier than they were, alp instanced that en March 24th they were not alightliirs o'clock and the sun had set just turned six. Something ought to be done. Mr.H.3.Westbrook said that he supported Mr.W hi tley's complaint. Mr.J. El. Clarke suggested that they should not pay for what had not been given to them in the way of gas. They should knock it off the account. Mr. Whitley observed that the lamps were used when in an unfit condition.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brown moved that the mAtter be referred to,the Lighting Committee, and Mr. Westbrook seconded. This was agreed to. THE FIRE BRIGADE.-APPARATUS "A TOY."</p>
        <p>Mr. 'Warburton mentionel that the Fire Brigade had a practice on Saturday last. Mr. Brown said that the apparatus they :lad was a real toy. "It is one of those things .you would be inclined to buy fog your boy." They had no business</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>FARM FIRE NEAR WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The Widnes Fire Brigade were summoned betwe en eight and nine on Wednesday morning to a fire whi ch bad broken out at Marsh II Farm, almost within a stone's throw of Farnworth and Bold Station. The farm is in the occupation of Mr. J. Taylor. The fire originated in a large brick barn. Luckily the flames did not assume command, for there being a good supply of water available the brigade speedily checked their progress to the adjoining property. A large quantity of straw was stored in the barn, and this was corn pletely ruined.</p>
        <p>WIDNES IMPROVEMENTS. THE NEW ROAD QUES NON.</p>
        <p>At Widnes Town Ball on Tuesday, Mr. W. C. E. Meade-King, on behalf of the Local Gor3:nrnent Board sat to tear the Town Council's application to borrow £6,000 for the purpose of making a new road, to be named St. Bede's, from Moor-lane to Appleton. Mr. W. J. Jeeves appeared for the Corporation, and.said he applied for a provisional order to put into force the Lands Clauses Act, for the purpose of acquiring certain land; also for leave to borrow the sum of £6,009, the approximate estimate for the making of the road. After stating the geographical position of the road, he said that this large area of vacant land will be opened up; and if, as is hoped, the scheme is completed, it will increase the ratable value. The improvement is highly desirable in the interests of the community. Mr- Jeeves stnted that the road would pass through land in the occupation of people. At the Appleton end the Corporation asked for powers to acquire the whole of the land in the occupation of Mn'. Fitzpatrick.</p>
        <p>LOSS OF 4 HUSBAND. RUNCORN WOI.IAN GETS £256.</p>
        <p>At the Birkenhead County Court on Tuesday, before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., Mary Atkin, of Suffolk-street,, Runcorn, brought an action against Messrs. R. Abel and Sons, - Oldhall-street, under the Compensation Act, for the loss of her husband. Mr. Brockleburst (instructed by Mr. E. A. Connor, Man- chester) appeared for the applicant, and Mr. Morris (instructed by Messrs. R ichardson and Marsh) tepre- seated the respondents. It appeared that applicant's husband, John Atkin, had been captain of a barge, and in March of last sear he was struck by as bag of flour at the Birkenhead Docks. He was taken to the Liscard Hospital, where he remained three months, afterwatds being removed to his home at Runcorn. Septic pneumonia supervened, and be died in January lust. Counsel for the respondents admitted the accident, but denied that pneumonia was set up through the injuries. —His Honour found for plaintiff, and astarded her £236 with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CHARTER AT ST. HELENS. CHILD IN A PUBLIC-HOUSE.</p>
        <p>Thomas Kilshaw, of Burtonhead-road, plea3ed guilty at St. llelens on Monday to causing a child to be in the bar of a licensed house.—Sergeant Small said that at a quarter to nine on Friday night, he found Kilshaw in the bar of the Phcenix Hotel, Canal-street, with a boy named Isaac Kilsbaw, aged eight. The lad was his nephew, and the lad's grandmother was also there.— Kilsbaw admitted that he bad heard of the Act, but added," I didn't knf.,w that it bad started off." He had heard the Act discussed in the public-houses, but could not read. He had taken his nephew down town with him for a long time. The lad came to their house on the Friday, and stayed until Monday morning, when he weirt to school. The bench said they would take it that Kilshaw did not know the exact date, and the case would be dismissed, but it must be taken as a warning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>"FETTLING" A ST. HELENS CONSTABLE.</head>
        <p>"FETTLING" A ST. HELENS CONSTABLE.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Monday, two young roughs named Thomas M'Cormick and Joseph Hill were charged with violently assaulting Constable Broughton.—The officer stated that at 8.20 on Saturday he saw the two men and another in Liverpool-road creating a disturbance. He told them to get away, but they threatened him. They moved along Sandfleld-crescent, and he followed them. When they got to John-street M'Cormick said "If you come across herel will fettle you." He then took hold of M'Cormick, who immediately flew at him and kicked his feet from under him. Immediately tbey were on the ground M'Cormick shouted, "Come on, mates, be men for once." The officer, hearing the other two coming, looked up, and Hill at that moment came with a flying kick which struck him in the eye. This was followed by a shower of kicks from the other two. Witness battled with the men for several ,miuutes, and then got hold of his whistle and put it in his mouth. Hill was watching for this, and he immediately kicked the whistle out of his mouth. He was still being kicked when two boys went for the police, and on their approach Hill and the other man, who was not known, ran away. A great crowd assembled, and the women were crying shame on Hill 3n d attempting to render the officer assistance, but they were prevented by the men who were standing round.— Police-constable Baker said that when he arrested Hill on Sunday morning there were blood marks on the tees of both his clogs.—Two youths, named Pollock and Hughes, gave evidence that Hill kicked the officer on the head and face eight or ten times.—The Chief Con- stable said that Broughton bad the worst eye he bad ever seen. It was a wonder that the eyeball had not been burst. M'Cormick bad a record of thirty-four convictions and Hill seventeen.-111`Cormick was sent to gaol for seven months and Hill for six.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>THE URBAN COUNCIL ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The results of the cont?st3 at Earlestown were declared on Monday. There was one considerable sur- prise. Councillf,r G. F. Clarke, president of the Work- ing Men's Conservative Association being defeated by the Labour candidate. Mr. W. Davies, by a majoiity of 22. Results :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>CROW-L 4NE WARD.</head>
        <p>CROW-L 4NE WARD.</p>
        <p>Thvips 263 G. F. Clarke</p>
        <p>Majority ll WARGRAVE WARD.</p>
        <p>E. L. Bent</p>
        <p>233</p>
        <p>Rowland King 125</p>
        <p>Majority 173 TOWN HALL WARD. Mr. W. Seton E. Gcslin , 386</p>
        <p>Majority Mr. Clarke seems to have tried to run with the hare and hunt with the bounds on the religious question, And to have offended the Irish party. The Earlestown " anardian " said last week "the red herring is drawn' across the track in Mr. Clarke's electicn appeal to the Crow-lane Ward voters. The largest type in the docu- ment is devoted to his views on religious instruction, which, he says, are well-known. To those familiar with the chameleon-lite character of his "views," this may read like a bit of subtle humour, worthy of the Vicar of BrAy. Whether sarcastic or serious, that subject has nothing whatever to do with this Council election; it is put in to catch the votes of a certain section of the Ward -electors. It would have been more to the purpose if Mr. Clarke had told the electors his" views " un the County Edu- cation Authority's resolution that Education 'Clerks should not sit on k,ocal Councils. Whether he is directly or indireetlya p'A servant of the Council, does not i matter. He s paid out of our rates. The point is that as a member of the Council he. votes in the selection of members to form the Education Sub-Committee, of which he is the paid servant. The County Committee says he should give up one post'or the other. The Ward electors csn see to it that be does."</p>
