      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>Mr. Parker Invited to Consider "The Larger Social Problem." Sir,—l thank Mr. Parker for his kind appreciation of my letter, but he seems to have ignored that part of it which appears to me to call for his attention. He is correct in inferring that we agree on the limited issue that has been under discussion. But this question cannot be fairly treated without relation to the larger social problem. Mr. Parker admits that factors exist that prevent the diffusion of advantages, , and iisgrees ti these factors should be eliminated. He suggests that we would probably differ as to these factors, but hopes that, at least, we would be able to reduce our differences to a very small compass. With regard to the former, it is impossible for me to say, in the absence of a statement of his views, whether, or how far, we differ, and in respect to the latter, although our differences might be reduced to a small compass' if they were vital we would yet fundamentally differ. I would not suggest that Mr. Parker is disinclined to enter:the "region in which the problems become more intricate," but I would repeat my invitation to him in my last letter to suggest a method that would eliminate the factors that prevent a general and just improvement in the condition of the great mass of men short of making production a regulated social function. My letter indicates my belief that private competing caphils, with the ir resultant antagonism of interests and waste, are the cause of the huge inequality that exists to-day in the distribution of the social product. Production has enormously increased but the share of the workers has not relatively increased. The factory system has socialised labour, and has thus made it much more productive. But the increment has been too largely appropriated by the owners of capital. lam not forgetful that the pntion of the product that gees to the employer includes wages of superintendence and payment of ability. Still capital as capital, according to Mr. Chiozza Money, takes the lion's share. The economic advantage which the owners of capital have over the great mass of men enables them to absorb the surplus or margin value created by social labour. And it is this factor of accumulated wealth in the hands of a comparatively few individuals, used to create profit, that diverts a large share of the increased product to the owning classes, that would under a regulated system be more equitably distributed. The dominant element in our present industrial system is the private ownership of:capital, enabling its holders to exercise economic pressure on those who are dependent upon the purchase of their labour-power, creating a hostility of interes4s and resulting in an unfair inequality of distribution. Engels says that "even if the capitalist buys the labour-power of his labourer at its full value as a commodity on the market,he yet extracts more value from it than he paid for, and this surplus value forms the increasing masses of capital in the bands of the possessing classes. It is difficult to see how a general diffusion of the benefits of increased efficiency and production is to take place Unless this economic dominance of a class is removed, and a system of iegulated economi6 forces is substituted. In a highly developed industrial organismlin which man have become so interdependent, this regulation is necessary, to bring about an adequate harmony of interests. We have it on the authority of Dr. Ingram " that mere conflict of private interests will never produce a well ordered common- wealth of labour." It is not easy to see how a COlll- - of interests is to exist amidst the jarring con- flict of competitive forces. It is difficult to see how this conflict is to cease without an organisation of industry on a social basis. The great problem for those social reformers who are opp- sod to a funda- mental change in our industrial relation, is to deal effectively with a state of things involving such opposition of interests without an organisation that would essentially affect the cause of it. The enormous disparity in the possession of material wealth implies a serious error in our social economy. If this is not the private ownership of the means and instruments of production, what it ? Yours, etc.,</p>
        <p>, ... . \, •1 sa, ,' . i, If,. . tters ;'• to th e L . , e . - a: ' EEOITORgt.. • ,:_4 . \.___ ... _____...........„_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>April 6th, 1009.</head>
        <p>April 6th, 1009.</p>
        <p>P4-ker s last- letter accuses me of exaggeration, and I feel profoundly sorry if I have given him occasion for doing so. The object I aim at by indulging in Press discussions is to draw the obscure to the obvious, to extricate truth from the intricate, to do my share to make chaos into cosmos. But I do not believe I exaggerated when I stated that 20 millions of our people did not receive enough to maintain a healthy bxistence. The words of Mr. Frederick Harrison suggest that I rather under- estimated the situation. He says : "To me, at least, it would be enough to condemn modern society as hardly an advance on slavery or serfdom, if the permanent condition of industry were to be that which we behold, that of 90 per cent4of the actual producers of wealth, who have no home to call their own beyond the end of the week; have no , bit of soil; or go much as a room that belongs to them; have nothing of value of any kind except as much old furniture as will go in a cart; have the precarious chance of weekly wages which barely suffice to keep them in health; are housed for iha most part in places that mon thinks fit for his horse; are separated by so narrow a margin from destitution that a month of bad trade, sickness, or unexpected loss brings them face to face with hunger and pauperism. This is the normal state of the average workman of town or country." 'Similar sayings could be quoted from Professor Huxley, Sir John Gorst, Mr. Joseph Chamberlain and others. But perhaps the most acceptable authority to Mr. Parker would be Mr. Chiozza Money, Liberal M.P. Upon. the cover of his great work, " Richest and Poverty," is a diagram showing there are in England If million persons rich, 3.1. million persons comfortable and '3B million persons poor. Not a modest 20 millions as stated by myself. Nuttall's Dictionary defines the word "poor" as meaning necessitous, unhappy, &amp;c. In spite of all the efficiency in production we have 38 million people necessitous and unhappy. In spite of the 40 per cent. rise in wages we still have a mass Of our people necessitious, &amp;c., and Mr. Parker asks me" if the 40 per cent. increase were taken off then what would be his figure for the underfed " ? Let us go back 50 years or so and investigate the state of affairs then and see what Mr. Parker's 40 per cent. increase amounts to. Fifty sears ago ! ! ! Does not Mr. Parker know of conditions prevailing then. Perhaps it is the blackest period of England's industrial history. Who has not heard of the "hungry forties." A time about which Cobden said that "There are thousands of houses in England at this moment where wives and children are dying of hunger." Any student of sociology knows of depths of . poverty, the degrading conditions of labour of men, women and children which ended, as necessary to the perpetuation of the English race, with the introduction of the Factory. Acts and 'other legislation. If 40 per cent was deducted from the purchasing power of the people it would place them in conditions obtaining during the "hungry forties." Mr. Seebohm Rowntree says that a dietary "more stringent than would be given to an able, bodied pauper in any Workhouse in England or Wales" to maintain the average English family would cost 21s. Bd. And Mr. Rowntree's investigation in York showed him that 151 per cent were living under this primary line of poverty and. 121 per cent just over it. Mr. Charles Booth's investi- gation in London showed similar results. Here we have proof of 28 per cent of. our population living upon Workhouse fare. Then again we have it upim the authority of Mr. F. H. Rose of the Amalgamated Society of Engineers that the average weekly wage of its members was about 29 shillings. More detail is not necessary to prove the shallowness of Mr. Parker's argument, or to show how much or little the bulk of the people have received from efficiency in production. It is not a question of whether wages have risen during the last half century or some workers wages have advanced more than others. The individuals not having capital beyond their labour power have always been the least con- eidered at any period of time. Their share of the nations prosperity, with varying degrees, has been merely enough to keep them in existence. Some periods have been slightly better than others or the reverse. To-day owing to the ingenuity and efficiency in production we .can: produce wealth in almost boundless quantity. Taking the average English family of five, we produce enough now to supply every family with £2OO per year. There are some authori- ties who say, with industry run on a co-operative basis this could be tripled. But there is dfactor intervening, Mr. Parker says so, "Democrat" says so, and I have</p>
        <p>been saying so all along. Mr. Parker has great modified his statement, that efficiency in production cheapening commodities thereby, is handed down to the consumer, and I think my figures in this letter show whether he has or not. I still suggest that so long as the factor of monopoly of land and capital continues, efficiency in production will not benefit the people. As Mr. Parker seems of a doubtful 'dis- position, perhaps he doubts there is monopoly. Mr. Chiozza Money says the total accumlation of wealth in Great Britain is 5..11,500 • millions. Of this five million people own £10,900 millions and 38 million people own £6OO millions. Efficiency in production should automatically cheapen commodies, all other, thinks being equal, whatever definition we like to apply to that. But we can see all through history that landless man, the 'man without capital, having only his labour power, has always been the " bottom dog." Whether production has been efficient or not his condition has only altered slightly in comparison. And whether temperance creates more efficiency or not, so long as we have monopoly in land and capital, his share of it has been and will be imperceptible.— Lam, yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>LABOR OMNIA VINCIT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
