      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MR. CROSFELD AT CANNES,</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFELD AT CANNES,</p>
        <p>GOLF AS AN AID TO DIPLOMACY.</p>
        <p>As most of our readers are aware the Borough mem- ber is Captain of the Cannes Golf Club, and went over to the South of France last week to partake in some of the important social functions in connection with the club. Last Friday the 'annual distribution of prizes took place, and interesting; speeches were made by the Grand Duke Michael (President of the Club) and Mr. Crosfield. We append a report of the proceedings from the Paris edition of the" Daily • ' Mail." dated April 5. The prizes won at the spring meeting of the Cannes Golf Club made an imposint, display when they were set out on the tables in front of the club house for presentation by Countess Torby. There was a large attendance of members and guests. Mr. Shelton, hon. secretary, read out a list of the winners, and , Countess Torby presented- the prizes with a few words of congratulation to each recipient. The presentation over, the president, the Grand Duke Michael, said: "Ladies and gentlemen, I would like to say just a few words of thanks to those friends who have helped us so much by gildng these most beautiful prizes for ,our spring meeting. (Applause.) I must also express my deep thanks to our captain, and to our hon. secretary, as well as to those other friends who have worked so hard before the season, and during the season, as well as throughout the whole of this meeting, and ~for the excellent way in which their arrangements have been carried out." (Hear, hear, and applause). His Highness then suggested that before the captain addressed them the hon. secretary ought to say a few words. Mr. Shelton good-humouredly declined to yield to pressure, and a great deal of merriment was caused by this and by the President's remark that he regretted very much to have to tell the members that he feared their friend the hon. secretary was deaf and dumb. (Loud laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECFI.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECFI.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfiel, M.P., said : " I am sure we shall all be delighted if our hon. secretary will come out of his shell this afternoon. (Laughter). I think he has hidden his light under a bushel quite, long enough. If he would only tell us a little of his experiences as hon. secretary of the golf club that would be very interesting, instructive, and edifying; and I feel certain it would be a very useful addition tq the literature of golf. We have all seen our friend Mr. Shelton hard at work on handicaps and other things,!and with any amount of pressure upon him at a time like this; and we have seen him day by day striving in vain to harden hisleart and resist the charming and seductive influences brought to bear upon him. (Laughter and cheers). I really think it is time he took us all behind the scenes and a little into, his confidence.</p>
        <p>" On former occasions I have once or twice at these annual gatherings made 'some slight allusion to politics, but to-day I have received a gentle hint from Monseigneur that it will be more discreet at the present juncture to give politics a pretty wide berth. (Laughter.) And yet I think such references as I have made on previous Occasions were innocent enough ; for instance the suggestion made at the time a rather delicate series of manceuvres were being carried on at The Hague. I ventured at that time to make the suggestion,that our diplomatic friends would find Cannes an even better rendezvous than The Hague for this very good reason, that no diplomat can ever do full justice to his work unless he has first qualified on the golf links. lam sure that all Of us who ha v e had any considerable experience in the niceties of matchmaking will agree with me that it is only on the golf links one finds the work of -polite deception really raised to a fine art. (Laughter.) "But, ladies and gentlemen, I must not make any further allusion to politics this afternoon, as they are strictly barred to-day; and besides, if golf helps to rub off the corners in politics its soothing influence is to bp felt in other spheres outside the region of politics _ _</p>
        <p>altogether, and we have had many an-opportiinity of seeing on the links here how it may bring about not infrequently a repprochement of the most charming kind ;—(laughter)--and, apropos of that how natural it is that we should be devoted to and absorbed in our favourite amusement! ,I thought there was a good illus- tration of that this very week, for only yesterday when we got back to Cannes from the links the beautiful battle of flowers was just over. No doubt this is one of the most important festivities in the whole year at Cannes, and yet.l doubt if a single one-df us gave up our game to go back to see the pretty sight. No, we preferred Napoule, and after all there is no reason to wonder at that. Who would dream of going to see flowers in Cannes when there are so many still more charming flowers strewn about the links here." (Cheers and laughter.) Mr. Crosfield concluded by proposing a very hearty vote of thanks to the Grand Duke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>"A Monkey Music Hall," in which the performers, musicians, &amp;c., are all monkeys makes a unique feature of the programme at the Palace and Hippodrome this week. . Great credit is certainly due to Miss Maud Rochez for having brought her Simian troupe to such a state of intelligence. Monkeys per- form on the trapeze, the musical bells, and finally one undresses and retires to bed with a naturalness almost human. Then there is the monkey orchestra of seven small performers, which produces weird " music " under the baton of a very energetic monkey conductor. The show was received with much applause and laughter. In quite a different vein is the turn given by the Marconis, who exhibit some of the wonders of electrical science in a very striking way. Many of the feats they perform with, the aid of the deadly spark and the fatal current are very mystifying, and there is a reward of £5OO for any -person proving that any state- ment made by the Marconis is not absolutely correct. Some of the best variety dancing seen on the boards for a long time is that of the sisters Florence, whose accomplishments are beyond the ordinary and well illustrate the poetry of motion. Dot Moss is a juvenile lady with the soubriquet of "the toy comedienne." Her gollowog song is popular and 'well done. The three Orleans specialise in wooden shoe dancing at which they are both expert and. graceful, but we cannot say anything very compli- mentary about their vocal harmonies. It is always a puzzle to us why dancers now-a-days should so often deem it necessary to sing. As a rule there is more talent in their feet than in their throats. Other items in this week's bill are Bert and Dora as comedy entertainers, and Smith and Johnson, a negro pair of vocalists. There is a good selection of biograph pictures. __</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Special Easter attractions are announced for next week. - Mr. John R. Poole will present the Seven Bramusas, an all-British family of brothers and sisters introducing their nautical brass band extravaganza "Music Afloat." Harry -Tate's No. 1 Company will appear in the famous sketch "Fishing," and other attractions will be :—Norah Desniond, American sopg illustrator; St. Rutli and Conyers, duet tists ; Shrub, the premier operafic whistler; Bennett and Martett, the shipwrecked mariners; Elton and Edwin, the c elebrated banjoists.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND 'for Coughs and Colds. Of roven efficacy. 90. &amp; ls.lid. Of Chemists only.—Ad vt</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the New Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.—WHlTE &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw st., opposite top of Bold at., Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>(Continued front Page 3.) THE GOOD FRIDAY CONTEST.</p>
        <p>Warrington" Great Victory over Halifax. The match with Halifax, down to take place yester- day afternoon, had long been looked forward to by the Warrington supporters. They were favoured by beautiful weather and showed their appreciation by, turning up in large numbers, the" gate" being esti- mated at 8,000. Halifax, in view of tl•is important League engagement and the Cup semi-final to-day had been taking a course of brine Laths at North% ich, and they came direct from there to the Warrington ground. The visitors were at full strength, and Warrington were also well represented, the only change being Hockenhall for Bradshaw on thlo right wing. Mr. Renton of Hunslet was the referee, and teams were as follow : Warrington: Tilley, full back; Fhb, Dickenson, Taylor and Hockenhall, threequarters ; Brooks and Jenkins, half backs ' • Thomas, O'Neill, Boardman, Shugars, Belton, and Harmer, forwards. Halifax : W. B. Little, fullback; W. T. William!, J. Riley, E. Ward, Dia Thomas, threequarters • J. H. Hilton and J. L. Medley, half tacks; J. Bree.rley, F. Hammond, G. H. Langhorn (captain), F. Mallinson, A. Robinson and J. Swinbank, forwards. Halifax won the toss and started with a strong sun and light breeze behind them. Warrington set the ball in motion and the forwards following up well, Riley was collared in possession. The stay in the Halifax half was only brief for Little's ponderous kicking sent Warrington back. Hilton cleverly broke away from a I scrum at centre and the home citadel was immediately in danger. The Halifax half back was not sup- ported however, and he had to throw the bell away. Warrington were hard pressed, and a rush by the v isiting Trout rank took the ball to Tilley, who cleared grandly.oThen came a burst to the centre where Little fielded avunt and cleverly dropped a goal. This roused Warrington to greater efforts, and they rushed the game to the Halifax half. Here Hilton attempted to kick away, when Dickenson burst through at top speed, -nd charging the ball he gathered finely and raced over between the posts, Fish . easily gosling. Hilton led the way back' again with another grand run. He cleared all opposition and looked a certain scorer when Fish caught him five yards from the line and saved the situation. • Little -did some fine punting and kept Warrington on the defence until Jenkins got away and passed to Hockenhall. The latter was nut of place and missed a glorious chance. Halifax quickly got down to the home line again, and Little missed an easy penalty kick near the pests. Half-time: Warrington, 5 points;</p>
        <p>Halifax, 2 pants. 'the re-start vcas senwitional, as Hockenhall smartly fielded a punt at centre and sprinted down touch in fine style. When near the line he passed inside and Boardman dashed through the defence with a grand try. Fish failed at goal. There was some desperate work alter this, bat nothing much happened for a time, play being mostly at centre. Then Little got a long punt past Tilley, and Williams raced up and touched down at the corner. There was no goal, and with only 3 points between the teams another ding-dong struggle was seen, Riley looked like going through once, but was well tackled by Brook?. Fish responded with a fine run for Warrington which established play near the line. Halifax were penalised in attempting to get the advantage in the scrum, and Thomas kicked a goal right on time. Final : Warrington,2 goals 2 tries, (10 points); Halifax,l goal 1 try,(s points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Notes by " Ovalist."</head>
        <p>Notes by " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Warrington distinguished themselves by a 'fine win over Halifax yesterday before a record crowd for the season. The Tykes threw nothing away in order to keep their position in the League, and put a full team in the field. There was a grim struggle from start to finish, with Warrington holding just a slight advan- tage which materialised in a winning score of 10 points to 5. The teams were remarkably well matched, and it was only the superiority of the home centres that turned the scale. Dickinson and Taylor were indeed heroes and the former's try from a great-single- handed effort will not soon be forgotten. Halifax had an advantage at half, when Hilton played a wonderful game, and gave Warrington a lot of trouble... The home pair did not combine well behind the scrummage 1 and their usual forceful methods were much missed. I Hockenhall filled a vacancy on the right wing, and in the second half particularly he played football worthy of his Palmiest days. The forwards, and O'Neill in particular, were grand. Altogether, Warrington had good reason to be pleased with themselves.</p>
        <p>This defeat for Halifax causes them to drop from second to third position, and if subsequent matches produce no change Wigan and Oldham will have choice of ground for the finals of the League. Much depends on Oldham's visit to Leeds to-day, and Halifax are hoping to see the positions quickly reversed. They are a powerful team and will need a lot of shaking off when the Championship and N.U. Cup come to be allotted.</p>
        <p>I am glad to say that - Warrington have circum- vented the sharp tactics of Oldham in the Lancashire Shield Competition. The Watershedding's lot refused to play the game at any other time than the one arranged, aud insisted on Warrington coming to Old- ham if their ground was engaged for the Salford match. Warrington have gone one better by persuad- ing Saford to play at 11.30 this morning, and they will have mustard ready for" Oldham" A "at 3.30 this afternoon.</p>
        <p>The last League match wtll be played on Monday with St. Helens as visitors.</p>
        <p>After all Jack Fish did not break his record yester- day, though he came near breaking his neck. He only requires one more point now to equalise matters, and he will surely put up new figures at the expense of either Salford or St. Helens. At present he is a trier all the way, and everybody wishes him a happy wind-up to the season. The gate " yesterday realised £1.40.</p>
        <p>The teams for to-day are as follocd :—Against Salford —Tilley; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor and Landor ; Brooks .and Jenkins ; A. Boardman, P. Boardman, Chesters, Fairhurst, Belton and Hewitt.</p>
        <p>"A "team for the Shield Competition :—Mclntyre; Treharne, Jordan, Cartwright and A.ockenhall ; Lees and Daintith ' • Thomas, O'Neill, Shugars, Harrison, Bradshaw and Harmer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
