      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>STREET IMPROVEMENTS.</head>
        <p>STREET IMPROVEMENTS.</p>
        <p>Finding Work for the Unemployed.</p>
        <p>In order to find work for a considerable number of the unemployed and at the same time to effect neces- sary improvements, the Corporation of Warrington are anxious to expedite extensive private street works which are to be undertaken. This point was strongly urged at an inquiry held at the Town Hall on Thursday morning by Mr. W. 0. E. Meade-Kin. M. Inst. C.E., an Inspector of the Local Government Board, into the application of the Corporation for sanc- tion to borrow £24,094 for works of Private Street Improvement. Amongst those present were Aldermen Tinnion and Evans, Councillors Birtles, Skelland, Sankey and Rogers; Messrs. J. W. Hayward, W. Hick man, S. Barlow; A. 1% Hallway (deputy town clerk), T. IP Spinks, (assistant town clerk), T. Longdin (borough surveyor) A. M. Key (assistant surveyor), and J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr. Hallaway said'he was instructed by the Corpora- tion to apply to the Board for ten years for the repayment of the loan in order that the burden of street making should not press so heavily upon property owners.Warrington was a very old town and in order to bring it up to date it was necessary for the Corporation to expend a great deal of money upon private street works,--coming in the first place out of the coffers of the Corporation but in reality being paid by the property owners. £783 14s. 11d, of the amount asked for as a loin was in respect of property of which the Corporation were owners and of property such as chapels which could not be appor- tioned. It hhd been the practice of the Board to allow an even longer period for repayment when the Corporation themselves were the owners. So recently as 1907 they allowed twenty years for the repayment of a private street loan in respect of the stet-making near I3eamont schools.</p>
        <p>Mr. Longdin stated that the work includel in the application was made up as follows :-37 front streets, estimated cost £10,659 ss. 6d. ; 101 back streets, £12,084 ss. 2d. ;'25 passuges, £1,350 17s. 6d. None of them had been touched as yet, and some of the streets were in a very bad state. Mr. Hallaway informed the Inspector that the majority of the objections to the apportionments had been settled.</p>
        <p>The Inspector: I see the application is made to a great extent to provide work for the unemployed. Mr. Longdin : That is hardly the object, but it will provide labour. The Inspector: It is not the main object, I suppose. Mr. Hallaway : We wish the Bolrd to expedite sanction of the loan as much as possible, as Pt will pro- vide work for those in want of it.</p>
        <p>The Inspector: Is there lunch distress in the pitied? Mr. Longdin : I don't think there is. The Inspector: Is this work such as those who are out of work can undertake?</p>
        <p>Mr. Longdin : The excavation and cementing will. We have no unemployed paviors in the town and we cannot make a pavior of any unemployed man. The Inspector : No you cannot possibly. In framing the estimates is anything included for the employment of unemployed labour or are they the ordinary estimated prices? Mr. Longdin : They are the ordinary estimated prices. Alderman Tinnion said that, as chairman of the Paving and Sewerage Committee, be wished to ask the inspector if be would kindly use his influence to get the loan as early as possible. There was a certiin amount of distress in the town, although he was glad to say it was not as acute as it was in most towns, and these improvements would gip employment to a con- siderable number of men. He could only express the hope that the inspector would do a little more than in ordinary circumstances to get consent for the loan, so that the Corporation could begin the work. The time of the year was favourable and many men it would employ had not been in anything like continuous employment during the winter months. In answer to the map or, Mr. Hallaway stated that whatever period the •rd allowed the Corporation for repayment would be granted to the property •owners.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hickman said the Property Owners' Association, which he represented, supported the application, but at the same time they wished the Board to allow ten years for repayment. Many owners had all their capital sunk in property and owing to unemployment and poverty, rents bad not come in very well of late. If the pay- ments could he spread over two or three years longer it would be a great boon to poor property owners. Many of the members of the association were working people owning their own houses, and a longer term would be of great advantage to them. The association represented over half of the property owners of the borough and about two-thirds of the assessment. The inquiry was then closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Wood—Houghton.</p>
        <p>At the Independent Methodist Church on Thursday afternoon, the marriage took place of Mr. J. Ernest Woods, youngest son of Mr. and Mrs. R. B. Woods of Latchford, and Miss Jane Houghton, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. John Houghton, of Stockton Heath. The ceremony was performed by Mr. R. B. Woods, and the wedding breakfast was afterwards partaken of in the schoolroom. Miss F. E. Woods was principal brides- maid and Mr. R. W. Woods of Southport acted as groomsman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Fleming—Ellison.</head>
        <p>Fleming—Ellison.</p>
        <p>A very large _number of people witnessed the marriage at Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Thursday of Mr. James Marshall Fleming, son of the late Mr. James Fleming, of Priory-street, Warrington, to Miss Mary A. Ellison, first daughter of Mr. Jolm Ellison, of Academy-street, Warrington. The Rev. T. Miller officiated. The bride.* wore a dress of lawn satin with hat to match and carried a bouquet of white carnations and maiden hair fern. The bridesmaids Were ,Miss Elizabeth Ellison, sister of the bride, the Misses Mary, Marion, and Maggie Fleming, sisters of bridegroom. They were attired in dresses of pink voile and blue satin and carried bouquets of white tulips and pink carnations. Mr. John Fleming acted as best man. A large con- gregation assembled to witness the ceremony. The bride and bridegroom are well known and esteemed,</p>
        <p>Miss Ellison being an active worker at Ellesmere- street Mission, Afterwards the guests adjourned to Winmarleigh Cafe where the wedding breakfast was served. The tables were most tastefully set out and the bride's cake was supplied by the Café proprietors. The happy -couple were the recipients of a large num- ber of handsome presents. "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Osborne —Clay.</head>
        <p>Osborne —Clay.</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding of much local interest (especially to the teaching fraternity who were present in large numbers) took place at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Wednesday, when Miss Leah Clay, the only daughter of Mr. and Mrs. George Clay, tof Irosfield- street, Warrington, was married to Mr. F. W. Osborne, Hawthorne, Villa, Chingford. The bride, who was given away by her father, was handsomely attired in an Empire gown of ivory chiffon taffeta, trimmed with _ _</p>
        <p>muslinlde-coie and sill embroidery, with touches of silver. She wore a beautiful veil of Brussels net surmounted with a corocet of orange blossom. The bridesmaids, Miss Preston and Miss G. Preston, of Dewsbury and Earlsheaton, were prettily dressed in pale blue eolienne over white silk. Mr. H. Osborne of London, did duty as best man. The officiating minister was the Rev. Yonge, while Mr. W. If Payton presided at the organ and played choice selections. Subsequently the party 41.journed to the Winmarleigh Cafe where the breakfast was served. The presents were numerous and costly including a very handsome ease of silver 'fish carvers and fish knives and forks from the teachers land scholars o People's College. Messrs. W. T. Williams, of Sankey- street, were responsible for the bridal outfit.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-04-10_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>VOTE OF CENSURE DEFEATED.</p>
        <p>The members of our local Parliament concluded the arduous labours of the winter session on Tuesday evening, and several months will now elapse before they again address themselves to affairs of State. As a departure from the usual routine, the subject for debate on Tuesday was a vote of want of confidence in the Government. Originally submitted by the Nationalists, it was abandoned by that usually militant section owing to some difficulty of procedure, and the Opposition took it up. The attendance was not as large as usual and the discussion was of a rather half-hearted character. The Nationalists took the strange course of refraining from voting on a resolution solely concerned with Irish affairs, and the resolution was defeated by 35 votes to 19.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. ENGLAND (L., Warrington) submitted the motion in the following terms :—" That the present Ministry have forfeited the confidence of this House by their failure to deal with the press- ing needs of the people of Ireland." He said there was a party in office who would not listen to the just claims of Ireland, to place her in a position of equality with this country. Ireland waS a very law- abiding country as compared with England and Scot- land, as was shown by the small amount of crime in the island. We hid granted Home Rule to all other dominions across the seas, but we refused to confer the boon on Ireland. We were driving the people out of the country, and many of them came to live in England. Home Rule would increase the loyalty of the Irish people to the Empire, and be a benefit both to England and to Ireland.</p>
        <p>Mr. N. COOK iL., Forest of Dean), in seconding, said the only offer of the Government in the King's speech to Ireland, that of providing a royal residence-was such, as they ought to be ashamed of. He absolutely denied that Home Rule would lead to Romanism in Ireland. The proposals of the Liberal Party in ISB6, which were still cherished by that party, had changed the feolings of Ireland towards England, as they had roused feelings of hope for the future. The matters affecting Ireland should be discussed in a congenial atmosphere.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. L. WILLIAMS (Secretary for India) asked why the question was raised at all, because it had been dealt with adequately in the Bill for the Federation of the Empire. The Liberal Party at Westminster had a glorious opportunity, but they were afraid of carrying out Home Rule. As a matter of fact, they dare not face the constituencies with that question which had smashed the party in the past. Mr. M. G. HEARNE (South Kilkenny) said that owing to their superior wisdom his party had decided not to vote at all. They had hoped for a drama in two acts but it had turned in a farce in one act. ("Oh, oh.") They had not time to discuss such an important question in one night. He contended that the record of his con- stituency with regard to lightness of crime could scarcely be equalled. In all Ireland, leaving out the three cities of Dublin, Belfast and Cork, there were last year an average of only four indictments per county. He ended by quoting figures to show that Ireland was grievously over-taied. Mr. E. G. BURGESS (Secretary for Ireland) described the resolution as a trivial one and procceeded to con- demn the "hollowness and pretence" of the Irish party in their attitude. A beTeat'deal of the present lawless- ness in Ireland could be allayed by firm and resolute administration of the present laws. Coercion seemed a terrible word in a free county, but if it only meant protection of those people who wished to carry on legitimate business in lawful ways,it was not so terrible after all. Good, resolute government was absolutely necessary to make Ireland contented. The Irish bad in season and out of season tried to thwart the Govern- ment., and he could not bee why they should expect very much consideration from the Government. He con- tended that Ireland was on an equality with England al-eady, and it would not be right to give a'preferential tariff to Ireland in the shape of Home Rule. Mr. J. JARMAN (L., Burton-on-Trent) in supporting the vote of censure, regretted that its terms were not wider. Boycotting was not confined to Ireland, and-he had even beard it suggested that Co-operative Societies should be boycotted. (Laughter.) It would be a good thing to have Home Rule all round, so that the details affecting each country in a particular sense could be dealt with by local government, imperial matters being left to the Imper:al Parliament. The Liberals granted Ireland Home Rule last session,but there was evidently only one way of dealing with Ireland—by including a Home Rule Bill in every King's Speech. (Laughter). Mr. W. H. STANSFIELD (Prime Minister) said the vote of want of confidence was farcical. If it were passed, it would mean that the Opposition were dissatis- fied with the measure which they themselves had placed on the Statute Book. (Laughter.) He claimed that the Bill for the Federation of the Empire which the Conservatives bad passed gave Ireland as near an approach to real Home Rule as it was possible to give. Mr. P. O'BRIEN (L., St. Pancras) claimed that the Home Rule cause had made great progress of late years and bad actually received the approval of the House of Commons which represented the voice of the people.-It was entirely contrary to the history of Ireland to sup- pose that Home Rule would mean Rome Rule and the Irish had never been behind in their loyalty when times of stress arose.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. CH RIMES (Home Secretary), said there had not been a single solid argument that night against Home Hule. (The slip of the tongue occasioned great glee, and the member hastened to correct it with "in favour of Home Rule.") No-one bad yet been able to devise a scheme wlich would satisfy all parties. He further contended that 99 per cent. of the Noncon- formists of Ireland were against Home Rule, and he could not understand English Nonconformists in their support of Home Rule thus taking no consideration of their co-religionists in Ireland. Mr. R. G. PIERPOINT (L., E. Manchester) said the government of India was almost a parallel to the govern- ment of Ireland. The interests of the two peoples were not consulted ; they were both dominated from Eng- land. The ignorance of the , Tory party was only equalled by their enthusiasm to tie tbemselves to the be4r barrel. (Laughter). They had for years misled the electors of this country. They had used Home Rule as a bogey just as they used tariff reform to cover up the remount scandal in South Africa and as they were now using the Dreadnought scare to-day. It was an absolute scandal. Mr. E. WYNNE-JONES (Leader of the Opposition) said the past session had been barren in the extreme. If the Tories had not bon so busy manufacturing scares and fostering panics they would have had some time to deal with Ireland. The Government that aspired to</p>
        <p>govern could not evade the Irish question and the Id mate solution must be Home Rule. Mr. Gladstonel of left the Liberal party a legacy, and they were pro"the it. The one great barrier in the way was .00, House of Lords, which was the bulwark of coerciflio loci That barrier would, however, be overcome and Ire; would be a free,contented and prosperous nation. Tile Tories could never settle the Irish question coif' central principle for which Liberals stood vras 'tte government. The central principle for which ailtl,op Tory Imperialists stood was the subordi of self-government to other interests, „co other words, ascendancy. Between those w'bey ideas there could be no reconciliation. opci formed a fundamental cleavage of .political opinlo9 the it was only In the hands of the Liberal party that Irish question could and would be settled. „ of, The resolution was pat to a vote and the Lea'f ;Leo the Opposition challenging a division, it was Me' by 35 votes to 19. „cite Mr. E. PLINSTON (1., Gateshead) • During tbe ' tO</p>
        <p>, the Prime Minister pointed across with ridicu certain members, and I object to that. pri The SPEAKER: How can you tell that the Minister did it with ridicule ? It is only your erl" Mr. PLINSTON : I saw him call the attentoe</p>
        <p>another member. The SPEAKER (ringing his bell): This is no citte.,ol4 for the House. Matters of this sort must be bro,o rorward in committee. 301</p>
        <p>In committee the 'PRIME MINISTER diSelailned</p>
        <p>intention of ridiculing other meta;i:i:</p>
